  "ST_JournalEntries": {
    "StoneClub": "A big stone attached to a stick; not terribly durable, but surprisingly handy. This simple weapon has saved the lives of many warriors. When caught between a rock and a hard place, why not take advantage of the rock? \r\n\r\nGoblin children play a game called bonk-bonk-bash, a rather unpleasant little pastime that acts as a form of population control. Each of the goblins takes a rock, attaches it to a stick and then takes it in turns to thwack their friends with it. This is a traditional household game that keeps the costs of feeding the family low. ",
    "Harralander": "A bitter tasting herb that makes for a poor tea, yet is often used as a morning pick-me-up by people with no tastebuds. It’s useful in crafting certain potions.",
    "StoneBlock": "A block of stone... there's not really much more to say here. ",
    "PaddedCloth": "A bolt of cloth that has been reinforced. Makes for sturdier armour than normal cloth.",
    "CapeOfVelgar": "A cape depicting a powerful dragon. ",
    "TatteredCape": "A cape made from tattered strips of cloth. ",
    "PinkCape": "A cape worn by those with a love of pony clubbing. Which is not as sinister as it sounds…honest.",
    "GraniteMaulHead": "A chunk of granite imbued with powerful magic. On its own, this hunk of stone isn’t useful, but when combined with other components it becomes part of a much stronger weapon. ",
    "MeatStew": "A classic meal across multiple realities. The aviansie enjoy a version of this using insects called Tobi’it. Goblins make their meat stews out of anything they can find… results vary.",
    "SaradominCape": "A cloak embroidered with the symbol of Saradomin, the human god of order. You'll be the envy of priests everywhere. ",
    "SpectralChinchompaCape": "A cloak of stitched furs taken from spectral creatures. It is uncertain how one might craft such a thing, and perhaps it would be impossible anywhere other than Fellhollow – where the world of the dead and the world of the living eerily coincide.",
    "AnimalHideScraps": "A collection of pelt scraps collected from smaller creatures. Some druids would insist on crafting cloaks made only from smaller animals that died of natural causes. This meant that each cloak often took months, or sometimes even years, to craft. Naturally this wasn’t particularly helpful on the cusp of winter.  ",
    "LeggingsOfLightness": "A cousin to the better-known Boots of Lightness, popular on the Gielinor mainland. These leggings were created by Shana, a Bramblemead villager who hated the cold. While the boots were functional, and useful, making it easier to carry things round the village, she always felt the winds chill no matter what she wore.\r\n\r\nHer solution was simple. Since enchantment created an excess of energy, at least when she did it – she was never going to become a wizard – she laced the leggings with the lightness charm.\r\n\r\nThe effect was twofold. Firstly, she could carry things around the town as easily as if she were wearing the boots. Secondly, the enchantment kept her legs nice and warm.\r\n\r\nFortunately, the designs for these leggings improved over the years, removing the overheating problem that Shana had been so fond of. Although she was doubtless less fond of it after her house caught fire.\r\n\r\nProgress demands sacrifice.",
    "CrystalOrb": "A crystal sphere containing a shimmering fog. It’s part of a magical ritual needed to craft a powerful staff, one that is infused with celestial power. The Icyene, of Hallow, once gifted these orbs to the followers of Saradomin, so that they might better serve the god of Order.",
    "ZombiePack": "A decayed and blighted sack. Contains the meagre belongings of the restless dead. ",
    "WildPie": "A delicious pastry crust seals the flavours inside. Warm and filling, each bite designed to fill you with comfort. The flavour is earthy, and rich, letting you shrug off almost anything.",
    "MeatPizza": "A delicious pizza, covered in juicy tender pieces of meat, and seasoned with a delicious sauce. You might ask yourself where the cheese comes from, but it’s best not to think too hard about it. Ashenfall has many secrets and those who survive here must do many strange things. No one judges you for this “cheese”, it’s fine.\r\n\r\nJust enjoy the delicious flavours and try not to have an existential crisis.",
    "RedCape": "A fashionable scarlet accessory for the most daring fashionistas.",
    "SkeletonPack": "A foul-smelling sack, with threads frayed by the cruelty of time. Contains whatever strange possession were being hoarded after death. ",
    "PersonalChest": "A good bag is surprisingly difficult to make in Ashenfall. First, leather must be obtained by hunting down deer and the deer of Brynmoor are skittish creatures. They flee at the first sign of danger and chasing them can be more trouble than it's worth. Wood, on the other hand, stays pleasantly still and so it was that the druids invented this very useful little spell. \r\n\r\nNow, they no longer need to traipse through the forest in search of deer, when they can simply summon a chest to wherever they are. Much faster. Much easier and you don't have to lug around all those heavy resources all the time. \r\n\r\nThis spell made druids very popular among locals, less so among hunters. ",
    "VegetableSoup": "A good soup won’t just fill your stomach, it’ll lift your spirits. Warm and comforting, the taste of home. Popular across all of Gielinor by anyone with a pan and some vegetables.",
    "GrilledMushrooms": "A great meal for a real fun guy… sorry.\r\n\r\nPopular with terrible pun writers and villagers alike, roasted mushrooms were a staple diet across much Ashenfall. Wild Knights would forage for the best mushrooms to enjoy on their long hunts.\r\n\r\nFor those braving the poison filled Bloodblight Swamps, the fortifying effect of mushrooms made them even more popular.",
    "ChefsHat": "A hat worn by masters of the culinary arts. There are no special enchantments, no mysterious contraptions, indeed nothing that should make this hat stand out. Yet, it remains one of the most well regarded and respected pieces of clothing across the whole of Gielinor. ",
    "VaultGuardianCore": "A heart of alchemically modified rune essence. Layers of complex enchantments, and anima veins run through this core. It allows it to absorb ambient anima from its surroundings, which in term power the various functions of the vault guardian servitors.\r\n\r\nThis marvel of dragonkin science had revolutionary potential. The ability to create power sources that thrived off of the ambient anima of the region. Ashenfall, with it’s surfeit of magical power, was the perfect place for such a technology and the dragonkin would apply it to all of their arcane machinery.",
    "IronBar": "A heavy ingot of iron.",
    "ZogrePack": "A large sack covered in mould and mildew. Once belonged to the terrifying zogres that wander Fellhollow. ",
    "LeatherShield": "A leather shield consists of a thick piece of leather stretched across a wooden frame. Wooden shields are bulky and heavy, making them more unwieldy. In contrast, a leather shield’s relative lightness makes it much easier to move into position. \r\n\r\nLeather shields were popular with the villagers of Bramblemead since the materials were comparatively easy to gather. Young warriors often wielded them as they accompanied druids into the woods, where the druids gathered the herbs for their medicines and rituals. \r\n\r\nThe Children of Shadows also enjoyed leather shields, which they decorated with Zamorakian symbols, hoping to intimidate anyone who saw them.",
    "Soulscourge": "A long time ago, a cataclysmic event struck Ashenfall. It split the land and poisoned the air, sending clouds of tainted dust up into the air where it was caught by Guthix's veil. Over the years this dust has seeped into the water of Ashenfall, tainting it with a dark sort of energy. Now, if someone drinks from this tainted water, and their ancestors hadn't spent their life being slowly warped by the strange anima of Ashenfall, they build up Soulscourge, as the anima contained within their soul is damaged. \r\n\r\nA soul is a container of anima within a person, shaped and enriched by their experiences. It is not piety, nor devotion, that grants a strong and robust soul, but adventure. Heroes, wanderers and explorers have strong and durable souls, allowing them to withstand the Soulscourge buildup for longer. \r\n\r\nShould someone gain enough Soulscourge buildup, they will be afflicted by the condition of the same name. Their soul rips, tears and starts to unravel, dealing constant damage until it can be healed. This can be fatal, as one needs an intact soul to survive. \r\n\r\nFortunately, souls heal surprisingly quickly. Soulscourge buildup will fade over time, as the soul repairs and enriches itself with the adventures of Ashenfall. \r\n\r\nTo avoid Soulscourge buildup from drinking water, you need only boil it at a campfire. The taint in the water can be separated from it by boiling it and letting it evaporate off, where it harmlessly disperses into the atmosphere.",
    "RuneEssence": "A magical stone with the ability to absorb the ambient anima around it. On Ashenfall, the rocks hover in the air as if resting on some invisible structure, held aloft by invisible currents of magical energy.\r\n\r\nWith the right equipment and skill, rune essence can be infused with certain magical resonances, taking on the elemental power of the air, or the flame.\r\n\r\nThis discovery is one of Guthix’s greatest triumphs and perhaps his longest lasting miracle.  ",
    "AshTree": "A mature ash tree, strong and sturdy and a great source of timber. ",
    "LargeWardstone": "A more powerful and refined wardstone, this one allows you a much greater return on investment. When activated, it draws energy from the soul more cleanly and efficiently, reducing the impact of Soulscourge on the user.",
    "SoulFragment": "A piece of a soul, half devoured by Imaru. Death seeks these to try and restore what he can of the lost, so that they might move on to the Afterlife. ",
    "BluriteDeposit": "A rock containing the most blue ore you've ever seen. ",
    "Sandstone": "A rock with a grainy texture. I really don’t know what you’re expecting here, there’s no deep lore about sandstone.",
    "CoalRock": "A rock, with coal in it. The implications of so much coal would cause any Gielinorian historian a headache, reminding them that there is so much about the world that they may never know.",
    "ImarusHead": "A rotting and twisted dragon head, momentary twitches still flicker through it, the last remnants of necromancy at play.\r\n\r\nThe tragedy of Imaru is that she was once a sweet and gentle creature. Cruel dragonkin experiments had robbed her of the only thing she had ever wanted, to be a mother. So when the humans came to her land, she saw in them the children she never had. Xikotal, her mate, encouraged this nurturing behaviour, knowing the joy it would bring his beloved.\r\n\r\nShe could have been happy, and the people of Fellhollow could have been happy with her.\r\n\r\nBut Kuldra could not allow it. The queen’s hatred and rage became an infection that took hold of Imaru’s grief and, like some foul parasite, twisted it into something worse.\r\n\r\nIt is better that Imaru is no more. There is none who would argue otherwise. Yet, whilst we should not mourn the monster, perhaps we should take a moment to mourn the mother she could have been.",
    "BandosianAmulet1": "A seemingly simple amulet carrying the symbol of Bandos. It feels strangely warm to the touch, and a faint sensation like the memory of a heartbeat thrums through your fingertips.\r\n\r\nZanik would be very interested in this.",
    "DragonbaneCape": "A seer once dreamed about brave warriors who walked across Ashenfall in another time. An age both past and present, where the rules of the world were different. These great heroes challenged and slew an echo of Velgar, saving a land that never was. \r\nWhile the seer’s memory of that dream fades with each passing moment, she was able to embroider the event into a cape, so that all might know of these legends from another time.  \r\n",
    "VaultGuardianShard": "A shard of rune essence, refined and manipulated by dragonkin alchemy. Tiny lines, like veins, run through the shard in very specific patterns, allowing the precise control of anima flow through the construction. When used correctly, these shards can create magical circuits, allowing the vault guardians to move, sense and attack with impunity.\r\n\r\nIt was the dragonkin Lacrussa that discovered this technology and it paved the way for powerful servitors to be constructed. Ultimately she would consider the technology a failure, albeit a useful one.",
    "ImaruDragonfireShield": "A shield with the visage of Imaru the devourer welded to the front. Holding the shield feels cold to the touch and instils within you a hunger for battle. Your blood pumps faster with a terrible need to rend your enemy limb from limb.",
    "BandosianAmulet2": "A simple amulet bearing Bandos’ symbol. Every once in a while you could swear you hear strange whispers from it, like a song you can’t quite remember. It’s cold to the touch and makes the hairs, on the back of your neck, shiver constantly.",
    "AdventurersBody": "A simple tunic brought with you from the mainland. It barely classes as armour, but it’s better than no armour at all. \r\n\r\nWhen you joined the expedition, you had nothing but the clothes on your back. All your worldly belongings were lost, and all your gold drained away. Debt collectors would appear out of the shadows, swarming toward you like rats seeking cheese. What choice did you have but to leave? \r\n\r\nThe expedition offered you a chance away from all your troubles. An opportunity to escape the cruel hand that fate has dealt you. These clothes once represented everything you ever lost, but now they represent the threadbare start of a new beginning. ",
    "LesserHealingPotion": "A step up from the weaker version, this potion allows the body to absorb more of the ambient anima, which in turn ramps up the healing. The potion allows the drinker to shrug off greater physical trauma than before.\r\n\r\nFor years herblore masters have worked on versions that might work on emotional trauma, but to these days there has been little success.",
    "UnfiredClayVessel": "A sticky clay container in need of a kiln.",
    "Marrentil": "A sweet smelling herb that is used as an ingredient in many poison remedies.",
    "PumpkinSoup": "A taste of autumn reduced to slurry and warmed over the fire. In Fellhollow, pumpkin soup was a popular dish when the temperature started to drop. Locals would gather together and share enormous cauldrons full of soup, as a way to build bonds with their community. It is said that once the villagers created an enormous cauldron, so that Imaru and Xikotal could join in the tradition, though for the great dragons it was barely an appetiser.",
    "DwellberryGlazedFlank": "A thick and juicy steak, combined with a rich and sticky dwellberry sauce. Each mouthful is like a taste explosion, and it can be difficult to hold yourself back. Orik the Bear was a Wild Knight who excelled in this dish, and other Wild Knights would seek him out at the gatherings for his culinary expertise.",
    "HeartyStew": "A thick and tasty stew, rich in flavour and nutrients. Bramblemead’s head chef, Leander, use to say that eating this stew is like sitting around a warm fire with friends, the aroma of the rich gravy filling your nostrils makes you feel like you’re home. Villagers would gather when he made a big cauldron of the stew, each of them ready for an evening with friends.",
    "UnfiredClaySlab": "A thick slab of clay, not very useful without being baked in a kiln.",
    "ClaySlab": "A thick slab of clay. Used in construction projects.",
    "RedberryGlazedFlank": "A thick, delicious and juicy flank steak, glazed with subtle redberry tones. Delicious and nutritious.",
    "DireWolfHide": "A tougher, more resilient hide, obtained after slaying a deadly dire wolf. It is said that if you wear the hide of a dire wolf, during the light of a full moon, then you’ll rapidly reduce the size of your circle of friends.",
    "Opal": "A very shiny stone. Prized by magpies around the world, who are drawn to it’s magnificent luster.",
    "SwampWeed": "A weed found in desert regions – haha just kidding, swamps. It’s a weed found in swamps. I don’t know what else to tell you.",
    "AshSapling": "A young ash tree, easily torn down by just your bare hands. Doesn't provide much wood, but it's a good start. ",
    "PumpkinSpiceInfusion": "Across dimensional rifts this drink calls out to a part of us we try and hide. That piece of us that wants a touch of whimsy, when the world turns darker than we feel comfortable with. As the approaching shadow of winter looms, its cold chill reaching into our souls, drinks like these offer a little warmth against the oncoming frost.\r\n\r\nStill, its allure can be overwhelming. There are some who succumb too heavily to its charms, becoming thralls to the flavour. Their entire personality clasps onto this mixture with frightening zeal.\r\n\r\nIt is tasty… but at what price?",
    "WeakHealingPotion": "Alchemists have long strived to create potions to rid mankind of all their ills. Normally these potions are regeneratives, that boost the body’s ability to repair itself by drawing in the ambient anima and infusing it into the blood and tissue of the drinker. On the mainland this changes healing from a matter of days, to a matter of hours. On Ashenfall the results are far more pronounced.\r\n\r\nThe ambient anima of the region is so strong, that wounds heal in moments. Cuts vanish as if they never were, the wounds healing so quickly that even scars cannot form. The weaker potions burn themselves out before they can heal massive injuries, but the results are impressive none the less.",
    "RedberryCrunchies": "Although a crude facsimile of gnomish cooking, the redberry crunchy is a pleasant snack that will get you through the day.",
    "LesserMagicPotion": "Although humans cannot use magic without runes, they can become quite proficient conduits for power. A wizard and a farmer, when possessing the same runes, do not produce the same results, the former far outshines the former. \r\n\r\nThis made some farmers pretty upset. For years they had ranted to their cows and sheep about how wizards were just snooty uppity types with shiny rocks. Yet, faced with this revelation, there was no denying that a farmer and a wizard simply weren’t equals in disguise. \r\n\r\nOne farmer wanted to level the playing field and brought in a local witch who was had a grudge against the snooty men in blue robes. \r\n\r\nBetween the two of them, they found a way to improve a person’s connection to the magic within them via a magical potion. Those who drank it cast spells more efficiently and with greater skill than they ever could before. \r\n\r\nThe farmer wanted to use this new advantage to put the wizards in their place, raze down the tower and claim magic a tool of the people. Unfortunately, the witch saw a different future, one that sparkled with glittering gold coins. \r\n\r\nShe died happily, wealthier than she had ever dreamed, surrounded by adoring young suiters who wanted a piece of her inheritance. The farmer died after being trampled to death by cows that were tired of his grumbling. ",
    "DarkMageBody": "Although the name may be sinister, the robes and the people who wear them are not automatically evil. \r\n\r\nBrynmoor had a string of so-called “dark wizards.” Most were little more than bandits with a penchant for magic. The guards were enough to deal with most troublemakers, but a small group of them caused real trouble. \r\nThey were members of the “Children of Shadows”, a Zamorakian cult that began to spread through the population. The cult fed on distrust, paranoia and greed, some very human traits. Among this number were three apprentices of the wizard’s guild, who were frustrated that they were not given the promotions they felt they deserved. \r\n\r\nIn their quest for power, the ‘Children of Shadows’ stumbled upon ancient vaults, repositories of the dragonkin’s long-forgotten secrets. Here, they discovered the remnants of a forbidden text, a relic of immense power. Driven by their arrogance, they chose to follow its instructions, unaware of the horrors they were about to unleash. ",
    "DarkMageHead": "Although the name may be sinister, the robes and the people who wear them are not automatically evil. \r\n\r\nThe first set of dark mage robes were so named because the technique to craft them required cloth that had been dyed by a unique alchemical mixture. As the years passed and Guthix introduced runes, the mixture was replaced with subtle enchantments, though the appearance was kept the same for tradition’s sake. \r\n\r\nNaturally, many practitioners of magic preferred this armour because of its darker aesthetic. Indeed, many rogue “dark wizards” would don this armour and use magic to engage in sinister undertakings, earning this armour’s moniker a more sinister meaning. ",
    "DarkMageLegs": "Although the name may be sinister, the robes and the people who wear them are not automatically evil. \r\n\r\nThe most infamous dark wizards became a fairytale for the people of Brynmoor, a cautionary tale about greed and the desire for power. They sought power in the darkest of places, and it turned them into something monstrous. \r\n\r\nThey appeared in the village in the dead of night as twisted versions of themselves—mindless creatures infused with a shadowy essence. Those who found themselves in their path had their lives ripped out of them until nought but an empty husk remained. \r\n\r\nIt took all the visiting Wild Knights to take them down. Arrow after arrow, spell after spell, the task seemed almost insurmountable until the light of the sun made the creatures vulnerable. In their death throws, it was as if some horrible chill fell across the world as if a door somewhere had been opened into somewhere it shouldn’t. ",
    "FriedOnions": "Although the preparation leaves you weeping, and the smell makes it worse, fried onions are a delicious meal to get you through a difficult day.",
    "SacredOil": "Although the recipe is significantly different to the oil created in Morytania, this oil serves a similar purpose. The priests of Emberwood created this oil to assist in funerary rites, the strange scent of the oil would help the dead pass on when cremated and, crucially, in the time of the Withering, the bodies would be reduced to ash, unable to get up and cause further trouble.\r\n\r\nOriginally this oil used pressed olives, and the influence of the gods to create an oil that would burn a body sufficiently to avoid the dead rising. As the Withering increased and civilisation began to fall into ruin, the priests found alternative methods to create a sufficiently strong flame.",
    "Seeds.Toadflax": "Although they prefer the toxic atmosphere of the Bloodblight swamps, these seeds can grow just about anywhere with the right care paid to them.",
    "AshShortbow": "An ash shortbow is the first weapon most young archers train with. It’s easy to make and, therefore, not so problematic if it breaks during training. Human villagers would often craft their first bows together under the tutelage of a visiting Wild Knight. They would use branches that had naturally fallen from the trees and carefully whittle them into the bow’s limbs. Bowstrings would be created by gathering flax from the nearby fields and spinning them into thin but hardy threads. \r\n\r\nMost would break on first use. Some would break before the first arrow was fired. Still, village youngsters would laugh at their misfortunes and start the whole process again. Each time improving. Each new bow sturdier than the last.  ",
    "PioneerScarf": "An attractive scarf, worn by those who first ventured forth into Ashenfall. ",
    "BlueCape": "An elegant cloth cape in a delightful azure hue.",
    "PioneerCape": "An embroidered cape, commemorating the first to explore the untamed wilds of Ashenfall. ",
    "BronzeBar": "An ingot of a copper-tin alloy. Highly prized by blacksmiths.",
    "UndeadRat": "And you thought the giant rats were bad enough!",
    "BoneClub": "Animal bones are surprisingly durable, especially those found on Ashenfall. The magic of the continent seeps into their marrow, slowly strengthening them and making them tougher. This goes some way to explaining the ferocity of ordinarily docile creatures, but it also helps when crafting tools. \r\n\r\nCows and similar beasts were celebrated in the age of Guthix. The people of Brynmoor preferred to take an animal’s life only on the hunt or after long years of service. When an animal passed, it was thanked for its sacrifice, and the materials helped strengthen the village. \r\n\r\nA club forged of bone was a simple tool but one that made defending the village much easier. Roving beasts were easily pushed back, and the village was protected by the animal’s last gifts. ",
    "AnimalHides": "Animal hides have a variety of uses and have made hunters very popular throughout history. In Bramblemead there was an annual competition to see who could find the most interesting hide. While anima twisted animals were plentiful, many hunters turned to cheating, leading to a rise in the dye trade. ",
    "Defence": "Armour will make life easier, reducing the damage you receive. The better the armour, the greater the damage reduction. Be sure to upgrade your gear at every opportunity. \r\n\r\nYou may also want to avoid damage from time to time. A well-timed block will negate all damage by parrying the attack, making it easier to counterattack. While, if like me you prefer to avoid a knife in the stomach, you can always dodge an attack and get out of the way.\r\n\r\nPerhaps the best way to protect one’s most precious internal organs, is to get out of the way of an attacker before they can stab you. Dodging is the key to this. Dash, roll and scream incoherently as you whiz away from an enemy’s attack before I can reach you. You can dodge in multiple directions, so make sure to keep an eye on your surroundings so you don't dodge off a cliff or anything. \r\n\r\nI did that once. Not fun. ",
    "HeartySoup": "As the Bramblemead villagers discovered heartier vegetables, their recipes improved just as much. The simple soup now felt richer, tastier and more fulfilling. A nice, hearty, meal to get them through the tough jobs on the farm.",
    "WillowShortbow": "As the dead rose in greater and greater numbers, the villagers needed stronger weapons to defeat them. Their ash and oak longbows failed to unleash sufficient force needed to stem the relentless tide of the undead. They needed a stronger material, that was also flexible enough to deliver arrows of great enough force.\r\n\r\nWillow was their solution. It is said that the first willow shortbow was gifted to a villager by a spirit that dwelled within the grove. Legends say that the spirit had an unnatural beauty, and would lure foolish villagers into the waters where they would be drowned. Yet, somehow, one villager earned not the spirits ire, but their admiration.\r\n\r\nThe spirit gave up their immortal life, to court the villager. They brought gifts of flowers, fish and finally they gifted a shortbow made from the willow trees in the spirits grove. The validity of this tale is hotly debated, with most assuming it is little more than myth, yet willow groves are still considered spiritual places, where one must be careful who they talk to.",
    "WitherReductionPotion": "As the hunger of Imaru spread across Fellhollow, it tore the souls of its victims from the bodies they had been previously so attached to. It was impossible to work out who would suffer the affliction next, and that created great unrest among the people, since their friends and family could become mindless undead at any moment.\r\n\r\nThe priests of Zamorak, surprisingly, were the first to discover a temporary solution, creating a potion infused with the rebellious nature of Zamorak. As Imaru attempted to draw the souls from the bodies of people, they would drink this and then she found this meal much harder to digest. The souls fought back against her hunger, locking themselves to their flesh with chains Zamorak would not have approved of.\r\n\r\nThe effects weren’t a permanent end to the problem, but they delayed the condition long enough for people to spot the signs and remove the problem from the Emberwood border. Infected villagers would be cast out of the protective barrier where they would face their fate alone.",
    "Lifeward": "Ashenfall is rich in anima, it flows through every rock, every tree and every living thing on the continent. This means that armour that is forged, on Ashenfall, is inherently more magically attuned than armour made anywhere else. This extra magical energy manifests as Lifeward, a protective barrier generated by the armour that shields the wearer from harm.\r\n\r\nLifeward acts almost like extra health, taking damage in place of their bearer’s vitals. Save for a few conditions, such as poison or bleed damage, the Lifeward protects against any attack.\r\n\r\nOnce it’s gone, it’s gone. Health regeneration has no effect on it. Instead Lifeward must be recharged with Wardstones.\r\n\r\nWardstones are small devices crafted at a rune altar. They’re made from rune essence, stone and just a drop of the crafter’s blood. This last, macabre, ingredient is what allows Wardstones to forge a connection to their creator, allowing them to recharge their Lifeward at the cost to a small amount of damage to their soul, creating Soulscourge buildup.\r\n\r\nDamage to one’s soul heals over time, so it sounds much scarier than it is.\r\n\r\nAdditionally, some moments provide a temporary increase in soul that extends beyond the person, becoming energy that the ward can absorb. Happy moments in life are the best examples of these, just little moments that make life worth living provide an excess of soul. On Ashenfall, this is most often provided from a good meal, providing just enough excess of soul to give the lifeward a little boost. ",
    "FocusedRunecraftingPotion": "Ashenfall is the birthplace of runecrafting. When Guthix settled here, he discovered the strange anima pouring out of the ground, different strands of magic resonating with certain concepts and natural laws.\r\n\r\nThis potion lets you see the world as he did, just for a while. You can spot the colourful flows of anima as they shimmer in the air and you learn how better to trap them in the rune essence, creating the potential for future magic.",
    "InspiringRingOfRunecrafting.RunecraftersRing": "Ashenfall was the first place that Guthix created runes, but he never passed those secrets on to his followers. It wasn’t until the legendary human, V, discovered the secret, that it was taught to the general population.\r\n\r\nStill, something about Ashenfall makes the process easier. No altars are needed, nor talisman’s required, yet runes can be crafted with an abundance that would make the mainland guild’s jealous.\r\n\r\nThis ring taps into that ease, infusing the soul with some of that ancient knowledge of the island. Be careful sleeping with this on, as you may dream the memories of runecrafters past.\r\n\r\nOr of a dog made of cheese. No one knows why.",
    "AstralRune": "Astral runes borrow resonance from the stars, that silent hum when looking up at the night sky. They embody the power of distance and displacement, action from inaction, movement in the dark. Astral runes are used primarily in spells designed to summon an object to the caster’s location, making it a very popular rune with the lazier magicians. ",
    "CadavaberryInfusion": "At first glance you might just assume this is poison, for the cadavaberry is a dangerous fruit. Yet, with careful preparation, and an appropriate amount of boiling, the berry can become a sharp, yet not fatal addition to any dessert.\r\n\r\nMixed with water, it becomes an empowering infusion, that puts a sprint in your step. Partially because your body is reminded at just how close it came to death in drinking it, so makes the most of the life it has left.",
    "SteelSword": "Atilius Rasmodel was a proud and powerful knight. Her skill with the sword was without compare and her bravery seemingly infinite against the dangers in Fellhollow. She was chosen by Imaru as one of her guards, not that a dragon needed such a thing, and she was made responsible for the defence of the dragons when they fell into the weeks long torpors they were known to suffer.\r\n\r\nShe called herself the Dragon Knight, even though such a title was unofficial and self-proclaimed, it soon stuck among the village people and they would bow and nod in appreciation of her skill as they passed her.\r\n\r\nWhen Kuldra descended on the region, demanding humanity’s destruction, Atilius climbed on Xikotal’s back in hope to drive her steel blade through the Godeater’s heart. She died beside the dragon she had sworn to protect, free of all remorse, in the line of duty.",
    "AxtralProjection_name": "Axtral Projection",
    "Movement": "Back in my day movement was much more civilized, everyone worked in neat little squares, moving entire steps at a time. Yet modern adventurers feel a much greater need for precision, yearning to swap between tiny steps and enormous leaps.\r\n\r\nHere on Ashenfall you can walk, run, jump and crouch around to your hearts content. Certain spells can also allow you to jump further, or even teleport short distances.\r\n\r\nEfficient, yes, but it’s just not as elegant as the grid-like movement of my day.",
    "Potato": "Baked, fried, mashed, there are few vegetables as versatile as the potato. Buried beneath the soil, these tubers absorb the nutrients around them, ready to be ripped from the soil and consumed.\r\n\r\nSome mages noticed that you could pass certain magical currents through them, and one even attempted to enchant one and fashion it into blades. Suffice to say their plan was not successful, but it did create the very first chip, so was largely considered to be a good thing.",
    "GreenCape": "Be the envy of druids everywhere with this fabulous accessory. ",
    "MoltenGlass": "Beautiful, yet hard to handle, the creation of molten glass was a turning point for Gielinorian civilization. It allowed the creation of vials, windows, and those pretty little animals that grandmothers love to collect and put on shelves.",
    "FineThread": "Because “corpse thread” is far too sinister a title, this thread is referred to as fine thread. It’s stronger, thinner and more malleable than cotton threads. The villagers of Emberwood enjoyed this thread in their clothing, creating garments that would suffer greater strain than their previous gear. Farmers were particularly fond of it.",
    "BoneDagger": "Before he learned to use the bow, Ulv survived with simple daggers of bone. He lived outside the village, cast out for his silence and strange ways. Ulv did not follow the polite nonsense of human society, nor did he seem to care for their troubles. The boy was happy just to survive and whittle small carvings of animals from fallen branches. \r\n\r\nHe hunted silently, using the daggers to slay small prey such as rabbits and chickens. He ate what he needed and sold the excess at the markets for enough coin to purchase simple clothes and tools. \r\n\r\nWhen he was thirteen, he was beaten by bandits and left for dead. But a Wild Knight found him and brought him to safety. Recovering in the knight’s home, Ulv would be taught how to use a bow, a skill he soon mastered. \r\n\r\nWhen a wolf attacked Bramblemead, this outcast slew it. Villagers thanked him for his bravery and asked him what he wanted as a reward. He refuted their offers and asked only for the wolf’s skull in return. He fashioned it into a mask that he chose as a new face, one he wore away from the village that had spurned him. ",
    "BerryCompote": "Berries and more berries, what could be better? A sweet and sour sensation in each mouthful. A refreshing, yet enjoyable meal. Well, more like a dessert, but no one’s judging you.",
    "IronArmourHead": "Blacksmiths everywhere favour iron as a material to forge with. It is strong, durable, and malleable when hot and yet rigid when cool. It has been used to create weapons and unbreakable containers. Yet, it is most popular when turned into protection for those forced to ride into battle.\r\n\r\nDuring the third age, the blacksmiths who travelled with the Saradominist priests brought the technique of iron to the people of Ashenfall. Previously relying on leather, bone and more easily extracted metals, the introduction of iron brought great change to the village's way of life.\r\n\r\nThey crafted more robust tools, mightier weapons and armour that practically erased the natural fear of heading into the woods. Only the Wild Knights opposed this new material, fearing its sudden popularity would impact the continent's ecosystem.  ",
    "IronArmourLegs": "Blacksmiths everywhere favour iron as a material to forge with. It is strong, durable, and malleable when hot and yet rigid when cool. It has been used to create weapons and unbreakable containers. Yet, it is most popular when turned into protection for those forced to ride into battle.\r\n\r\nFrom the outset, the search for iron ignited a fierce conflict between the humans and the Garou. Each incursion escalated the tension, pushing both sides perilously close to the brink of war. However, the situation reached a critical juncture when Bandos dispatched an emissary to the villagers of Brynmoor, offering a solution to their Garou predicament.\r\n\r\nThe Saradominist priests met with emissaries of Bandos and came to an alliance. The humans would claim the mines of Ghornfell, as long as Bandos could claim the head of the Garou goddess, Lougrim.\r\nA win-win, as far as the priests were concerned.",
    "IronArmourBody": "Blacksmiths everywhere favour iron as a material to forge with. It is strong, durable, and malleable when hot and yet rigid when cool. It has been used to create weapons and unbreakable containers. Yet, it is most popular when turned into protection for those forced to ride into battle.\r\n\r\nThe desire for iron was so strong in the village of Bramblemead that they exhausted all the iron deposits in Brynmoor. The Wild Knights cautioned them to look for more sustainable methods. Still, the villagers had become too accustomed to the luxury of iron.\r\n\r\nMilitia groups began to form to search Ghornfell for the precious metal ore. They marched in large groups, crushing plants beneath their metal heels and destroying whole areas of woodland in search of iron ore.\r\n\r\nTheir noise and bluster drew the ire of roving Garou, who saw the invasion of their forests as a violation of their treaties. ",
    "BloodwoodSap": "Bloodwood trees are a genuinely horrible creation. Their very existence a wound in the decency of the world, because this tree grows only in places steeped in violence and death.\r\n\r\nThe biggest bloodwood trees tower over battlefields. Sinewy spiderlike branches cast shade over the homes of serial killers, and you should never trust anyone that can grow one in their garden.\r\n\r\nLeech-like roots soak up the suffering, sorrow and cruelty that has seeped into the soil, and then converts that misery into a sticky red sap of raw emotion. This sap can be used to amplify feelings, to convey anger, fear or hatred, across a much greater range.\r\n\r\nThe dragonkin Lacrussa was very fond of this medium, and used it’s power to shackle dragons to her will.",
    "BluriteBar": "Blue concentrated and refined, melted down and reforged into a cuboid shape so that you may carry around purest blue with you wherever you go.",
    "FerociousInfusion": "Boiling away the tainted material, in the water, while infusing it with an animal bone, causes the mixture to draw in some of the primal resonance of the bone. Drinking the infusion temporarily allows the drinker to feel some of that animalistic ferocity.",
    "CleanWater": "Boiling the water separates the tainted elements in the liquid, allowing you to scrape it off and enjoy a more pleasant beverage.",
    "WoolenCloth": "Bolts of durable woolen cloth, popular with mages and anyone that likes a little thermal insulation in their armour.",
    "LinenCloth": "Bolts of sturdy linen cloth, excellent for crafting magical armour with.",
    "BonesToPeaches_name": "Bones To Peaches",
    "AnimalBones": "Bones are not just the favoured materials of necromancers, they can be incredibly useful to a skilled survivalist as well. Designed to withstand great force, while still being as light as possible, bones make excellent components in an array of crafting recipes.",
    "GhornfellHighlands": "Bones of ancient wyrms litter this region. Gargantuan dragons that are now mercifully dead. Forged by the mad science of the dragonkin Lacrussa, these titans could well have ended the god wars themselves. Fortunately, none still live in this region.\r\n\r\nA great storm rages across the region, the result of desperate sorcery in a long drawn-out war. The thunder here is hateful and spiteful, striking any that walk the land, as if driven by the vengeful fury of its long-dead caster.\r\n\r\nWatching over the Highlands stands a great monument to a fallen garou monarch. A king who challenged impossible foes. A lasting memory of the garou people, hewn out of stone.\r\n\r\nThe garou here are well trained and merciless, seeking nothing but the death of all who dare step foot in these sacred lands.",
    "FracturedCape": "Brakka’s hubris was a marvel to behold, perhaps more impressive than any monument of his construction. When Rasthin shared with him the elemental power she had wrung from Ashenfall’s mysterious vents, Brakka became a master of the earth itself.\r\n\r\nWith the power to shape stone and soil, by will alone, Brakka could have created great villages. He could have constructed aqueducts and watermills that could brought Ashenfall into a new, golden age. Instead, he made a tower.\r\n\r\nA single tower that rose as far as the eye could see. Forged through his own power, and the blood, sweat and tears of garou slaves. It is, perhaps, fitting, that this is where he died. Kuldra, the godeater, crushed him beneath her great claw and feasted on his anima-rich soul. Stealing Brakka’s power for herself.\r\n\r\nShe gifted Velgar, her most trusted general, with the elemental mastery of the fallen Nodon architect, and, to mock the dragonkin further, Velgar took Brakka’s monument as his new lair.\r\n\r\nNow the tower stands for all to see, as a reminder of dragonkin hubris, and rightfully deserves its new moniker; Brakka’s folly.",
    "BramblemeadValley": "Bramblemead valley is home to the village of its namesake, a once tranquil location bustling with life. It was founded in the first age by the god Guthix and his followers, and converted to the worship of Saradomin sometime in the third age.\r\n\r\nThe Keep of Blue Flames, was built in the popular style of the Saradominist faithful. The ordered shape a symbol of the god of order’s will. Powdered metals were cast into the braziers, so that the flames would burn in the hue favoured by Saradomin. Here the lord of the region would watch over the village and surrounding areas, under the careful guidance of the high priest of the church.\r\n\r\nTo the northwest stands a symbol of mankind’s thirst for knowledge, a great guild dedicated to uncovering the secrets of the runes that had been first discovered on Ashenfall by Guthix himself. Here, powerful wizards learned spells and enchantments, pushing the boundaries of mankind’s knowledge.\r\n\r\nAshenfall is rife with danger and the need for heroes has always been strong. A guild was created, to the east of Brynmoor, where heroes and champions would gather together to seek out the deadliest monsters and slay them where they stood.\r\n\r\nOnce this valley was the hub of human activity, but now it sits silent, and overrun with monsters. Goblins spread across the region claiming it in the name of their new god. Rats swarm through the ruins with sharp and deadly fangs. Soil that was once fertile farmland, is now soaked to the roots in blood.",
    "FriedCabbage": "Brassica Prime – the god of cabbages – gifted the world with this delicious vegetable, to lead the faithful on the noble path of nutrition. In his infinite, leafy, wisdom, he taught the early settlers about how one might fry the leaves, so that they would be even more delicious.\r\n\r\nNow these fried cabbages offer those who consume them, the strength and stamina needed to brave the hardships of Ashenfall.",
    "BoneArrowBroadhead": "Broadhead arrows are more dangerous than their standard counterparts. The tip is like a razor blade designed to make the target bleed. It was a favourite of the Children of Shadows, especially when turned on their White Knight opponents. \r\n\r\nOne such cultist, a woman known only as The Athame, would hunt down priests using the arrows to bloodlet them before going in for the kill. She delighted in watching them run as they bled, only to chase them into a quiet corner and end their life at the edge of a blade. \r\n\r\nShe was an aberration in the cult. Most members treated it as little more than a social club, and few actually enjoyed the Zamorakian ideal. But the Athame lived for chaos. \r\n\r\nShe was never caught. ",
    "BronzeArrow": "Bronze arrows offer greater armour piercing than their non-metallic counterparts. Copper and tin were relatively uncommon in Brynmoor, so using the resources on arrows seemed like a waste of money. One faction of bandits delighted in this premise; their leader, a man known as the Auburn Ocelot—for reasons he would never explain—took great amusement in their use. \r\n\r\nThe Auburn Ocelot would laugh as his bandits hurled expensive arrows at the villagers, pilfering coins worth far less than the ammunition used to corral them. To him, it wasn’t about the money but the look of confused terror on their faces and the knowledge that no one could stop him. \r\n\r\nHe was above it all. Better than everyone. \r\n\r\nIt was a rat that killed him. Not even a giant one. Just a tiny creature carrying a virulent plague. Money could not save him, no matter how much he flaunted it. He was cremated, and his ashes buried in the middle of nowhere so that no one might risk catching the infection that cost his life. \r\n\r\nNow, he’s almost completely forgotten. ",
    "BronzeDagger": "Bronze is a common enough alloy, a combination of copper and tin. Durable, defiant, and deadly in the right hands. For Mia Larsdottir, it was a bronze dagger that pierced her heart, cutting her life short at the age of twenty-one. \r\n\r\nAs the first of the Amalgamated, she had never expected to reach any of the milestones her peers traditionally followed. She did not expect to be married, and she never let herself dream of being a mother. She thought, perhaps, that she might be allowed to carve a quiet little place for herself, somewhere with room for cats. \r\n\r\nBut a bronze dagger stole that dream from her. \r\n\r\nHer killer was never brought to justice, though there were rumours about who they were. Her father, being the chief suspect, ashamed as he was to bring the first of the unnatural Amalgamated into the world. He denied it, of course, but he never held a funeral for her. Instead, her body was left in the forest, where nature might put her at peace. ",
    "BronzeArmourLegs": "Bronze was once a secret held by the ancient dwarves. It is a more robust metal forged from an alliance of two others. \r\n\r\nAs the Saradomin faith became more popular, the bronze-clad inquisition enforced Saradomin’s laws, even when they conflicted with the Guthixian faith. It was a slow change, but they went from clearing shrines of monsters to clearing shrines of the Guthixian heresy. \r\n\r\nWhen the druids finally rebelled, the bronze-clad enforcers were called against them, not the White Knights. The cruel irony of being arrested by the force created to protect them. ",
    "BronzeArmourBody": "Bronze was once a secret held by the ancient dwarves. It is a more robust metal forged from an alliance of two others. \r\n\r\nThe alliance of two different faiths had its own warrior force to defend its shared ideals. Clad in polished bronze armour, these warriors stood out against the stark green of Brynmoor’s fields. They upheld traditions and punished those who blasphemed against either faith. \r\n\r\nThe bronze advisors, as they were known, would offer advice from both a Saraodminist and a Guthixian viewpoint—order and balance side by side. Such was the intention, but the scales were already tipped. Policies began to slip more and more in favour of the Saradominist approach until the Guthixian faith slowly dwindled as the Saradominist view took centre stage.",
    "BronzeArmourHead": "Bronze was once a secret held by the ancient dwarves. It is a more robust metal forged from an alliance of two others. \r\n\r\nThis union of metals was often used as a symbol of cooperation. Alliances would store the signed documents in bronze scroll cases or sign them with a pen made of tin and another of copper. \r\n\r\nOn Ashenfall, it was used to show that the old ways could stand beside the new. For a while, in the third age, druids wore pins of copper, and the Saradominist priests wore tin pins. They would then stand together, side by side, in village council meetings, a promise that neither one stood stronger than the other. ",
    "CourseAnimalFur": "Bundles of animal fur, scratchy and thick. While not an ideal trait on pets, this sort of coarse fur has a multitude of uses in the wild.",
    "GroundClay": "By grinding clay down into powder, you can create a much smoother medium for crafting. The grinding process gets rid of various impurities and allows for more resilient results.",
    "SkeletalArcher": "By the time you’ve heard their creaking bones, it’s already too late. Despite their lack of sinew, sensory organs, or anything that should make them this dead, the skeletal archer is surprisingly deft.\r\n\r\nIn life they were scouts, hunters or Wild Knight archers, in death they’re simply something best avoided.",
    "NecromancerRobeTop": "Callius Waithe created an army of the undead and marched them on Emberwoord. Or, rather, he called his shambling little horde an army, even though it only consisted of five shambling skeletons in various degrees of disrepair.\r\n\r\nThe village guard took aim at the skeletons, but their arrows lacked the strength to shatter bone, and Callius marched them through the gates with barely any opposition. Still, once he had made it through the gate he was not met with the terror he had hoped for. While shambling skeletons were certainly a fright that caused much shock, their limited numbers helped to reduce the terror.\r\n\r\nA small child even giggled and tried to offer one skeleton an apple. An act that filled Callius with unreasonable rage.",
    "AdventurersCape": "Capes are stylish and protect against the weather. They can be elaborate symbols of stature, or simple cloth to act as a ward against the cold. This cape is the latter.\r\n\r\nSimple, elegant, and functional. Worn by adventurers and explorers across Gielinor’s mainland and beyond.\r\n\r\nAfter the dragon attack, there are few of these capes worn on Ashenfall. The rest sink beneath the ocean waves, are torn to pieces on the cliff rocks, or have long since burned to ash beneath draconic hate.",
    "ZombieAxe": "Captain Artorius Flamhanger was a brave man, who stood against the rampaging forces of the damned even as his defences crumbled around him. He never wavered, never faltered, and he never left a man behind.\r\n\r\nIn a better world, there would be songs about him. But Fellhollow scorns legacy, leaving only ruin and despair where hope might take root.\r\n\r\nHe held the line against the encroaching horde far longer than anywhere else in the region. Days turned into months and months grew into years. As the rest of the region vanished into death, despair and rot, Captain Flamhanger stood strong. As his children died and rose again, the captain returned them to the earth with sombre compassion. When his wife joined them in death, he gave her a final kiss of polished silver that cleaved her head from her shoulders.\r\n\r\nIn a better world he might have held back the darkness, stood as the single ray of light in a world of shadow. But this is not our world.\r\n\r\nThere are no sagas of this man. No great texts telling of his heroic deeds. The only sound he made in the time after his death, is the bubbling song of digestive acids within a zogre’s belly.\r\n\r\nHistory is penned by the survivors and the victors, yet the deeds of the dead echo across time. This axe, simple as it may appear, is infused with vengeance against the dead that had taken so much from  a good man.",
    "Cathan": "Cathan is the first to tell you that he was raised in privilege. Wealthy family. Loving parents. Everything a growing boy could need. Yet, like for so many of us, it wasn’t enough. He craved excitement, exploration and adventure, yearning more than anything to discover the wonders of the world. \r\n\r\nAs a child he slipped away from his family, in search of adventure and monsters to slay. He found more than he bargained for, and the lesson for his hubris cost him a leg. \r\n\r\nIn the months that followed, the boy fell into a deep darkness. Life offered little meaning when he could barely walk a few steps. His parents, in response, hired him a tutor, so that he might rekindle that spark of adventure that had once burned so brightly. \r\n\r\nIt worked. In Vannaka he found someone that could push him out of his melancholy. Who taught him how to wield a blade and target the weaknesses of his foes. Vannaka brought in others, who helped train him further. In Zanik, young Cathan, learned how to survive in the wilds. In Doric he learned to craft weapons and armour to take down his foes. Finally, the Wise Old Man taught him a little bit of magic, and young Cathan’s curious excitement burned brightly once more. \r\n\r\nWhen the opportunity came to explore a new land, Cathan was the first to sign up and convinced his mentors to join him. As they sailed to Ashenfall he stood at the bow of the ship smiling at the adventures ahead. \r\n\r\nIn the chaos that followed, he’s keenly aware of that age old phrase “be careful what you wish for”.",
    "SummoningHorn": "Cathan’s challenge horn design is a collection of materials hastily thrown together. A dragon’s tooth, carved with dragonkin sigils as well as metal wires used to connect them together –  like a spiderweb lattice, glistening with traces of magical power.\r\n\r\nWhen placed in the dragonkin altar, the horn acts as a focus point for the magical power stored within the tower, sending an invisible beacon off into the air, in a perfect draconic pitch.\r\n\r\nVelgar has been summoned many times before. By heroes, kings, warriors and gods, and yet still the tower stands.\r\n\r\nBe ready, when you send out the call.",
    "StormtouchedCape": "Centuries ago, a spell was cast over the Highlands that changed it forever. Once temperate skies became pendulous clouds filled with lightning. Electric hate that strikes at anything and everything that dares step beneath their gaze.\r\n\r\nWar changes everything, even the sky.\r\n\r\nThe land itself is strewn with the dead. Ancient skeletons of colossal wyrms, the monstrous creations of dragonkin science, lie across the terrain, dead eyes watching over everything. Lacrussa considered these monstrosities her finest work. Yet she shed no tears at their destruction. She could always make more, or so she believed.\r\n\r\nIt was a lone girl, a necromancer of great power, who turned the sky into a curse. A last act of vengeance for a fallen friend. She dug into forbidden magic, drawing power from the countless dead slain in constant conflict. The garou, lords of this region, were not prepared for the power of grief, for it was a concept alien to their primordial nature.\r\n\r\nThese stories weave into the threads of this cape, barely remembered, yet never quite forgotten.",
    "Snapdragon": "Children are often disappointed that this plant does not resemble an actual dragon. There are no snapping motions in these colourful leaves, and a depressing lack of firebreathing lizards in the stems.",
    "Choppertunity_name": "Choppertunity",
    "ClayDeposit": "Clay can be found in deposits around Ashenfall and can be recovered with just a few pickaxe swings. It is said that the Elder God created the universe out of clay, albeit of a more interesting vintage, and the unwanted remains hurled into the abyss. The truth of this claim is hard to establish as the Elder Gods are rarely prone to a friendly chat. Not least because, were you to hear them speak, your head would explode. This makes conversation tricky. ",
    "WhiteCape": "Cleanliness is next to godliness, so some of the white knights claimed. Live your bleached dreams with this immaculate accessory.  ",
    "Coal": "Coal is the petrified memory of a long dead ecosystem. Plants and animals, who lived countless aeons ago, crushed down into rocks of rigid carbon and becoming a powerful fuel that changes civilisations.\r\n\r\nWhen you burn the coal, imagine the worlds that came before and the lives forgotten. Let each piece become a funeral pyre for a long-lost age.\r\n\r\nOr enjoy shiny new steel armour, your call!",
    "Swingslash": "Contrary to popular belief, a goblin is as smart as any other species. It was Bandos that instilled within them the notion that intelligence was a sin, and demonstrating your education was something to be discouraged. As a result, goblins often go out of their way to make themselves seem less intelligent than they really are.\r\n\r\nHence “swingslash”, the name given to this axe because of how it swings through the air, slashing anything it hits. It’s so reductive and silly, it can only be an overcorrection, a desperate attempt by the creator to seem dumber than they are.\r\n\r\nYou can ignore, therefore the curve of the blade and the sharpness of the edge, created by carefully thought-out techniques that make the weapon deadlier than those before them. The name, chosen by the blacksmith, so none might accuse them of the crime of being smart.",
    "NatureRune": "Contrary to popular belief, the nature rune does not solely govern plants, roots and the natural world. That is only part of its design. Instead, nature is about the connection of things, threading together the way things could be, rewriting them to a state more desirable. Favoured by wizard’s for their ability to adjust the flow of time to an entity, dragging it back to a point when it was in a more preferable state. ",
    "InspiringRingOfCooking.ChefsRing": "Cooking is a form of magic that few people truly master. Meals can return lost memories, shatter dour moods and rekindle friendships long since abandoned.\r\n\r\nThis ring was created by accident, when a parent was trying to teach a child how to cook. They were holding the unfinished piece, absent-mindedly, as they explained to their progeny about all the steps needed to understand a recipe.\r\n\r\nThis knowledge, infused with the emotion of a parents instruction, enchanted the ring so that all future wearers might benefit from their knowledge.",
    "BronzePickaxe": "Copper and tin conspire well together, forging an alliance to destroy their former friends. The bronze pickaxe can shatter the mightiest stones and can mine through more durable ore than bone and stone could ever dream to battle.\r\n\r\nThe dwarves that Guthix brought with him to Ashenfall, were fond of the bronze pickaxe. While iron and steel are sturdier, the bronze pickaxe was more readily available, and they could risk a little decoration. Many would customise their pickaxes with symbols of their family crests, or a nickname that embodied them well. Often these would make the pickaxe impractical, yet they became a popular heirloom handed down between family members.  ",
    "SodaAsh": "Created by burning plants with a high salt content, such as swamp weed and other marine plants, this powder if a vital part of glassmaking. When mixed with ground sandstone and charcoal, it creates the alchemical reactions that allows the creation of molten glass.",
    "VeganFryUp": "Crisp and delicious vegetables, neatly fried mushrooms, there are few breakfasts quite as delicious as a fry up, even for those who abjure bacon.",
    "CrystalBow": "Crystal bows are not crafted but sung into existence. Harmonies are layered over one another to teach the weapon a story so that it might understand why it needs to be strong. Traditionally, the plight of the elves, their loneliness without their goddess, and the many dangers they must face are sung. \r\n\r\nThe shimmering substance they’re made from is traditionally a shard of the elven goddess Seren, left behind when her crystal form shattered at the end of the Third Age God Wars. \r\n\r\nThis bow isn’t the same material, and the song is different, too. There’s no divine essence in the crystal, no lament of the elves. Instead, it is sung memories of a lifetime together, distilled down into facets reflecting in the crystalline flaws. In the moonlight, those facets sparkle like tiny stars held within glass—moments of hardship, conflict, happiness, and grief. \r\n\r\nIt is music as a weapon, with memory as the handle and sorrow as the string. ",
    "FireBronzeArrows": "Deadly arrows made deadlier with fire. Each arrow burns with the fury reserved for the most unpleasant parts of Infernus. They sear, they singe, and they melt the flesh, turning their victim into little more than ashes floating away on the wind.\r\n\r\nIn the days of the great war against the Garou, the priests of Saradomin encouraged the use of these weapons. They saw it not just as a means of eradicating the hated beastmen, but to purify the world from their taint. Lougrim was a false goddess, in their eyes, and her followers had to be cleansed from existence.\r\n\r\nModern scholars would doubtless be confused at their monotheistic mindset, considering that the literal god of war, Bandos, walked beside them into battle. The mental gymnastics employed by the Brynmoor priesthood, could have rivalled any athlete in the Gielinor Games.",
    "CadavaberryBush": "Deadly, poisonous berries. Delicious deadly berries.",
    "DriedDwellberries": "Delicious berries. They make a great snack. Very popular among garou druids as a seasoning with their latest quarry.",
    "RedberryGlazedRoastMeat": "Delicious roasted meat, with a redberry glaze. The fruit flavour combines with the meat in a sublime manner that makes it hard to eat this slowly.",
    "BronzeArrowBrutal": "Designed to deliver a ruthless punch, the brutal arrow is not to be trifled with. It was used by the warriors of Brynmoor to take down more dangerous foes, such as roving garou or dangerous wolves. \r\n\r\nOne cold night in autumn, a creature appeared in the valley and massacred a local farm. The villagers were horrified, running screaming to the guard, who rallied the forces as quickly as they could muster. The beast was large and lumbering, and its red eyes shone out in the night. Its flesh was covered in mangy black fur, and its teeth were sharp as daggers where they hadn’t rotted away. \r\n\r\nThe creature tore through the guard, leaving a trail of fallen warriors in its wake. It took the courage and skill of a Wild Knight, Aurin, to stand against it. Armed with brutal arrows of polished bronze, he launched a relentless assault. The creature, despite being struck by a whole quiver of arrows, refused to fall. Instead, it let out a chilling howl and vanished into the shadows, leaving the battlefield in a state of eerie silence.",
    "UndeadMeat": "Despite the appearance of their former owners, and the deeply unpleasant smell emanating from it, undead meat is not rotten. It, like the nature of its former owners, is preserved in a state between life and death, as if it’s on the cusp of souring, but never quite getting there.\r\n\r\nThis unique nature gives it some very interesting properties, but sadly taste isn’t one of them.",
    "DetectOre_name": "Detect Ore",
    "WiseOldMan": "Dionysius was once a legendary adventurer. In his youth, he single-handedly defeated the troll invasion of Keldagrim. He crushed the enemies of one of the mountain tribes and taught the people of Karamja how to use powerful herbs that might aid them in battle. He committed great deeds throughout his life, and the world is a better place with him in it.\r\n\r\nOr it was.\r\n\r\nAfter he settled down, finishing his book on destructive magic, Dionysius began to feel that the world was a little unfair. He had tirelessly given his youth for the betterment of the world, and he had nothing to show for it. Even his devout faith in Saradomin wasn’t making his life more comfortable.\r\n\r\nSo, like so many do, he turned to crime. He targeted the nearby bank, his magic making quick work of their security, and he lifted himself a great deal of gold as well as a snazzy blue party hat. Content to consider this repayment for saving the world so often, he now sits back and enjoys his well-earned wealth.\r\n\r\nThe Temple Knights consider him an ultraviolet archangel threat and watch him very closely... from very far away.  ",
    "Naptha": "Distilled from Swamp Tar and Charcoal.\r\n\r\nOnce known as “thick water”, naptha is liquid fire in waiting. It takes little to bring this substance to ignition, even too bright a sunny day can bring the raging flame from within.\r\n\r\nAs such, this material is widely sought by anyone with a strong desire to set things aflame. Pyromaniacs, alchemists and dragon cultists alike yearn for this liquid.",
    "DraconicHide": "Do not pity the dragonwolf. Oh, you don’t? That’s fine then. Yes, horrible little blighters aren’t they? Their teeth are too jagged and they’re far too aggressive for my tastes. Still, apparently deadly critters don’t just want to roll over and die so you can harvest their skin, who’d have thought?\r\n\r\nThe dragonwolf is an experiment that didn’t quite go right. It wasn’t wrong, exactly, it was certainly a step in the direction their creator wanted, but like most Dragonkin experiments, they was never “good enough”. Too warm-blooded. Too fluffy. Too weak.\r\n\r\nFortunately, the crafters in Emberwood were not quite so picky. They found that hide to be incredibly durable and hardy, whilst remaining surprisingly flexible. They would craft wonderous outfits from the tanned leather, trying to emulate their winged overseers.",
    "IronRock": "Do you know the riddle of iron? No, me neither, asking for a friend. Iron is a durable metal, capable of creating some of the strongest arms and armour available. ",
    "Cadavaberries": "Don’t eat them. No, seriously, don’t. At least not raw. Cadavaberries are poisonous and deadly, often used in sinister ceremonies of ritual sacrifice. The cadavaberries on Ashenfall are equally toxic, but can be coaxed into good behaviour with the judicious addition of fire.\r\n\r\nThe toxins can be burned away, providing a surprisingly pleasant tasting berry beneath. But, one must be careful, for preparing them wrong is the last mistake someone is likely to make.",
    "MincedUndeadMeat": "Don’t think about it. Just burn it until it’s unrecognisable and try not to think about the implications of consuming the flesh of rotting zombie animals.\r\n\r\nI’m sure it’s fine.\r\n\r\nDo I want a bite? No, no, I’m good… err… trying to watch my figure. You go right ahead.",
    "Doric": "Doric is a master smith who trained in the dwarven capital of Keldagrim. He owns a smithing shop in Asgarnia alongside his son Boric. The two of them have a strained relationship because Boric took it personally when Doric sent him to Keldagrim to complete his training. \r\n\r\nTo rebuild trust and show his son that he has the uttermost faith in his abilities, Doric has left the running of the shop to Boric in his absence. Now, Doric comes to Ashenfall in search of new metals, lost smithing techniques, and the blueprints of ancient weapons. \r\n\r\nNot much of a fighter himself these days, he prefers to stay out of harm’s way, but he knows how to wield a hammer if push comes to shove. ",
    "BronzeWarhammer": "Doric would be the first person to praise the simple hammer. It is a large solid chunk of metal attached to a long haft, allowing the weapon to benefit from the unmistakable might of physics. Weight and speed are a terrifying combination. \r\n\r\nDwarves, in particular, favour this weapon. They feel it speaks to their long-held desire to shape metal by pounding into shape by sheer strength alone. They apply the same logic to their enemies, beating them into a shape that the dwarves feel suits their enemies better.  \r\n\r\nThere were once dwarves on Ashenfall who were masters of this technique. However, when the humans began their infighting, and Guthix turned away from them, they vanished deeper into the continent, leaving behind a shroud of mystery in search of something extraordinary. \r\n\r\nThey found it and have never returned. ",
    "GreenDragonHide": "Dragons are never fond of surrendering their natural armour, a natural feature they’ve used to great advantage.\r\n\r\nIn the earliest days of the Dragonkin experiments, the scales of a dragon were something to be proud of. They would turn away blade and arrow with equal skill, and even magic struggled to pierce their natural defences.\r\n\r\nStill, this wasn’t good enough for their makers. Lacrussa, herself, saw the hide of dragons as a promising start, but little more, and she would strive to forge newer and more resilient scales on her creations. The green dragons were, in her opinion, some of the weakest of the dragons, and she only kept them around because her sibling enjoyed the glittering green reflections of the sunlight on their scales.",
    "WitheringWater": "Drawn from pools of wither-rot water, lakes and streams poisoned by the waters of the river Noumenon – the river of the dead.\r\n\r\nThese waters have become corrupted by Imaru’s hunger and each drop longs to devour one’s soul.",
    "BlackCape": "Dress like a creature of the night with this sinister cloak.",
    "YellowCape": "Dress like a king with this fabulous yellow cape.",
    "Shrapnel": "Dropped by Bleed inflicting enemies.\r\n\r\nSmall collections of sharp little objects. Stone, metal, bone, each of these combines to create a little bit of hate in the palm of your hand. The brutal points of these bits of shrapnel can tear the skin and rend the flesh, making them perfect for crafting weapons to make things bleed.",
    "PoisonIchor": "Dropped by poisonous enemies.\r\n\r\nNature is full of malicious little critters who carry toxins. Snakes, spiders, politicians, each of them has ways to make other creatures suffer.\r\n\r\nThis ichor is prized by poisoners and alchemists, as it can be used to create deadly concoctions that will give someone, or something, a really bad day.",
    "WoodenArrow": "Druids dislike violence but understand its place. The natural world is full of blood, murder and death. The strong consume the weak. Predator against prey. This they understand, though they seldom like it. When they must resort to violence, they choose the simple approach. Man’s strength comes not from their muscles but from their creativity and ingenuity. Just as a wolf has claws, a hunter has a bow. \r\n\r\nWhen a bow must be drawn, the druids prefer arrows made of simple wood. Gathered twigs and branches, whittled down to sharp points. They see it as the forest giving them the blessing to go on the hunt. Traditionally, feathers are gathered from around the forest floor, taken when dropped or from a bird’s moulting. This keeps the number of arrows low, forcing a hunter to be accurate and precise, lest they run out of chances. \r\n\r\nThus, death is swift at the hands of a bow, and balance isn’t damaged. This the druids preached to the villagers. ",
    "RangerHat": "During the final years of Bramblemead corruption had run rampant. Nobles took more than their share and the priests who watched over them turned a blind eye in exchange for their own share of glittering coin. Those who did not possess noble blood, found that their lives became harder, their earnings became smaller and joy became a much more fleeting feeling than it had done before.\r\n\r\nOne woman decided to change that. She would attack trader caravans marked with goods for the castle, taking from their purses of coin. Tax collectors would also feel the sting of her arrows, surrendering their wealth to the woman clad in green. She was given the name Nightingale, because of the whistling noise her arrows made as they flew through the air, an almost musical tone that sent fear through her targets.\r\n\r\nVillagers would then wake in the morning following an attack, to find purses of coin in flowerboxes, window shelves, or resting neatly under the fattest, laziest, cats.",
    "DwellberryGlazedRoastMeat": "Dwellberries boiled down into a sticky sauce and liberally trailed across the meat. At first you might expect the combination wouldn’t work, but as you take that first mouthful, you find your tastebuds writhe in delight for this delicious combination.",
    "DwellberryCrunchies": "Dwellberries on Ashenfall are heartier than their mainland counterparts and make for excellent ingredients. The Wild Knights were particularly fond of this combination, though they referred to them as “Dwellberry splats” because Olis, their inventor, wasn’t a particularly good chef.",
    "DriedRedberries": "Easier, than standard redberries, to eat on the go, tastier too.",
    "Choppertunity": "Edgar was a lazy lumberjack, who was good friends with some of the wizards at the guild. He would skive off work, to visit the taverns with his blue-robed buddies, where they would laugh and discuss the fundamentals of magic, whilst their world blurred out of focus. Yet, Edgar always listened to his friends and he picked up on concepts that his more academically minded companions would miss. Magic, whilst it could be wielded like a scalpel, was also perfectly capable of being used like a club. \r\n\r\nWith some runes in his pocket and laziness as his shield, Edgar created a spell that would make his job go much faster – shattering tree trunks into useful logs, in a matter of moments. \r\n\r\nHe was later fired, when he tried the spell when drunk – demolishing the beams of his master's cabin.",
    "EyeOfOculus": "Elias Oculus was an avid birdwatcher. He delighted in nothing more than seeing sparrows, falcons and corvids nest. In the spring, he would wake just before the sunrise, so that he could watch the flocks of birds emerge from their nests and take to the skies in a cacophony of song. How he longed to join them, to fly beside them and witness their world in the skies. But, alas, as a human, he was chained to the floor by the unbreakable bonds of gravity.\r\n\r\nWhen he started studying to be a wizard, he realised a way to live his dream. A spell that would move his vision outside the confines of his mortal flesh, and allow him to soar among the heavens. In addition to creating a spell found invaluable by architects, Elias also published the most comprehensive studies of bird flight patterns.\r\n\r\nWhen he passed on to the next life, a statue was created in his honour.  It isn't found on any ruin, nor on grand display, but resting in the branches of a great tree, so that his beloved birds could remember him fondly. ",
    "AmuletOfMagic": "Emberwood had its fair share of spellcasters, though lacking the funding of the tower to the south. With more limited resources the mages of Emberwood found other methods to strengthen their art, and the Amulet of Magic was popular among all of them.\r\n\r\nDruids worse these amulets while performing rituals around the standing stones, hoping that it might strengthen their bond with nature. The self-proclaimed warlocks, of the Children of Shadows, would sit in darkened basements wearing these glittering amulets as they intoned dark rites that had no effect. The Wild Knight mages would use them more pragmatically, wielding magic against all the threats that the villagers were unaware of.",
    "EnchantCamfpire_name": "Enchant Campfire",
    "Weeds": "Even the plant world has its outcasts. Weeds are unloved, unwanted and yet they always find a way to turn up in the most unexpected of places. Just like my cousin Terry.\r\n\r\nStill, at least weeds have some uses... unlike Terry.",
    "BlightwoodShortbow": "Every hunter reaches a crossroads in life when a choice becomes clear. They have honed their skills with a bow, surpassing the challenges posed by the Brynmoor prey. They could settle into a comfortable life in the village—hunting the same old deer, earning a regular wage, and basking in a relaxing level of respect from their peers. Or, they could dare to dream bigger, to push their skills to new heights. \r\n\r\nThe Wild Knights journeyed into more dangerous territories, facing off against deadly monsters warped by the anima twist. Joining them would mean abandoning the comfortable life of friends and family but earning a chance to face challenges that they would never face at home. \r\n\r\nMost choose comfort, as one might expect. But those who choose to improve would be taught how to craft a more powerful weapon, casting aside the oak bows that carry their names. They would carve a new name and identity into the limbs of their latest weapon and join the knights. Their names would be whispered, and gossip about them would transform into fireside stories—ghost tales about those still alive—the first embers of legends. ",
    "RingOfThePhoenix": "Everything dies, this is the truth of existence. Even mountains and forests will eventually fall. Time is the great equaliser that consumes stars and mayflies with equal relish, and every living being always fights against it.\r\n\r\nMagicians and alchemists have gone mad trying to defeat the arrow of time, doing whatever is possible to stall death’s embrace. This ring is one of the most successful approaches so far.\r\n\r\nThe ring stores magical energy within the band and at the moment where death should claim it’s quarry, the ring cracks and the power is released, infusing their host with a burst of magic – undoing damage and restoring health.",
    "EyeOfOculus_name": "Eye Of Oculus",
    "NecromancerRobeBottom": "Faced with the humiliation of villagers not fleeing in terror, the necromancer Callius Waithe was forced to use more harrowing tactics. He grabbed a jar of oil from a nearby blacksmith and threw it over his skeletal minions, and then he set them aflame. For what could be more terrifying than the shambling dead? Why, the shambling burning dead, of course!\r\n\r\nThis did cause the panic he wanted. Villagers fled the flames quickly, running to the wells in case of the inevitably flaming spread. Spread it did, for while the skeletons themselves did not burn, the oil covering them erupted into excited tongues of flame. Callius laughed at his evil genius, until the flames spread beyond the means of his control.\r\n\r\nIt is said that on autumn evenings, when the moon is full, the ghost of Callius can be seen screaming in the village, running through the streets trying to put out spectral flames.",
    "SteelSpade": "Fallen from too many tall buildings, only to be punished for your misstep by the cruel and malicious ground? Well, now is your chance to get your revenge. The smug soil doesn’t think you can touch it, but you can prove it wrong by stabbing it through it’s smug topsoil smirk with a nice steel spade.\r\n\r\nOh, sure, it’s great for gardening, but really this is a tool for sweet, sweet revenge.",
    "PlantCure": "Fashioned from clean water, weeds and animal bones, this mixture leeches out the vitality from those ingredients and transfers it to the sick and dying plants in your care. Cooked over a range, this concoction was first thought to be the domain of druids and herblore masters, but in truth it was a cook that put the pieces together. \r\n\r\nOld mother Potterington was much beloved by the people of Bramblemead. On weekends, when people allowed themselves brief breaks form their toils, she would whip up a great batch of something delicious and simmering, and neighbouring farmers would drop by for a meal. \r\n\r\nThis kindness extended not only to people, but to her garden and farming patches too. She whipped up a broth so infused with nutrients, that Brassica Prime himself is said to have blessed it. When fed to plants, their withering and wilting leaves would stiffen and return to vibrancy, life and vitality theirs once again.\r\n\r\nNow you can brew such a concoction yourself, so that your plants might always live their healthiest days.",
    "FellhollowCape": "Fellhollow could have been a new utopia, were it not for the cruelty of the godeater. Imaru and Xikotal adored their human neighbours and dreamed of a society where both dragon and human could thrive together in peace.\r\n\r\nNow the land, like that dream, has broken. A hateful hunger from beyond the grave has consumed all the life, love and joy in the land. The dead roam desperately seeking to tear the tiniest threads of life from any living thing that crosses their path, and the afterlife itself, here, is poisoned by Imaru’s grief.\r\n\r\nYet, once, long ago, this land was beautiful. A myriad of colours danced in the breeze, the anima twist turning the region into a kaleidoscopic wonder. The now ruined streets were once filled with laughter, joy and song. And the dragons danced across the skies in loved-laced poetry.\r\n\r\nThis cape is a burieal shroud for a world that could have been.",
    "FerociousFurnace_name": "Ferocious Furnace",
    "Bandos": "Few deities are responsible for as much death and destruction as the self-styled “big high war god” Bandos. On his own world, he brought destruction and became the last one standing in a planet-wide war. When he ascended, he brought this war to other planets, wiping out countless civilisations for the Grand Sport of war.\r\n\r\nHe came to Gielinor for the God Wars, delighting in death and destruction between entities as powerful as him. He aligned with Saradomin and Armadyl to battle Zamorak, helping corner the lord of chaos and forcing his hand.\r\n\r\nZamorak’s revenge destroyed a whole region, wiping out Forinthry and creating the Wilderness. A deadly region where monsters and murderers thrive.\r\n\r\nBandos met his match after the death of Guthix, when he returned to Gielinor to start battle once again. He began work on a great weapon that would be strong enough to kill the other gods, but the god of justice, Armadyl,  built his weapon first.\r\n\r\nThe goblins, ourgs and other minions of Bandos, watched as the aviansie deity took Bandos’ mace and crushed the remains of Bandos’ skull.\r\n\r\nThough the god of war is dead, his people still live on, following the dogma of endless conflict.",
    "DivineCabbage": "Few gods offer their bounty as freely as Brassica Prime, lord of nutrition and god of deliciousness. In exchange for this bounty of crisp and delightful vegetables, he asks only for the runes that make the spell possible. No prayers are invoked, nor altar laden with sacrifices, just a few runes and a gesture of fingers, to provide a most healthy meal. \r\n\r\nThe cabbages here are perfect. Their leaves are crisp. Just the right amount of moisture is contained within each bite. Nutrition abundant in every morsel. A true gift from the gods. ",
    "Zanik": "Few have lived a life as rich with adventure as the cave goblin Zanik. She was the first of her people to brave the sunlight, the first cave goblin to meet humans and the surface goblins, the first to learn about the gods and their influence on the world, and the first cave goblin to stand side by side with the World Guardian in defence of creation.\r\n\r\nIt cost her her life. Twice. \r\n\r\nShe was once the Chosen Commander of the war god Bandos, who tried to use prophecy to put her under his thrall. She died; he resurrected her and then used magic to seize control. She resisted, fighting off his influence. Bandos’s lingering affliction on her soul would claim her life after the War God’s demise, but even death wouldn’t stop her.  \r\n\r\nShe became the emissary of the Underworld, resurrected once more by the god Icthlarin, and brought back to the surface to join the fight against the Elder Gods. \r\n\r\nNow, she comes to Ashenfall in search of a lost and forgotten tribe. Hoping to stop history from repeating itself.",
    "IronMace": "Few instruments are as good at shattering bones as the iron mace. It was a favourite weapon of White Knight paladins, who dreamed of shattering the bones of the risen dead. Necromancy was rare on Ashenfall, though not unheard of. Two necromancers had been reported to the order, one a girl with a knack for bones and another who whispered with the dead. Neither summoned skeletal hordes for the knights to shatter. \r\n\r\nBut garou and goblin bones broke just as well. In the war against the garou, Sir Lear, a white knight paladin, used the mace to great effect. He crushed warriors, battered druids, and shattered the shields of powerful thanes. He was a symbol of the might of Saradomin’s faithful when faced with overwhelming odds. \r\n\r\nHe joined the Wild Knight commander, Vemmeck, in their siege against a northern garou stronghold. With countless goblins at their side, their victory was almost assured. Yet a single druid’s magic held the forces at bay until Sir Lear learned how ineffective his mace was against the scales of a dragon. ",
    "GoldRock": "Few metals are as beloved as gold. Its glistening nature has attracted countless pursuers, and those who find a viable vein need never work again. Sadly, on Ashenfall, the economy is not quite so well established and gold is merely another crafting component. Still, remember where you found it, because once all this dragon malarky is dealt with you might make a tidy sum selling the location to prospectors. ",
    "IronGreataxe": "Few sights are as intimidating as a great axe hefted toward you. It can only be carried in a pose that exudes raw strength, and a bearer of such a weapon can rarely look meek. \r\n\r\nBandos was also a fan of the aesthetic. While he favoured the sheer brutality of the kyzaj, he chose to wield a great axe against Velgar. He stormed Brakka’s Folly, great axe in hand, and challenged the dragon to single combat. A god versus a dragon, the outcome felt obvious, yet it did not play out how Bandos expected. \r\n\r\nIt should have been easy. Yet Velgar’s stone-touched flesh turned away blows, and his poisonous breath leeched even Bandos’ strength. Bandos cut a great scar down Velgar’s side, yet the wound was not fatal, and the counterattack sent Bandos reeling. \r\n\r\nHe never surrendered, but there was no victory for the big high war god. He sounded a tactical retreat, seeking superior tactics – far, far away. ",
    "GoldOre": "Few things have led to quite so much wanton destruction as gold. Wars have been fought over it. Brother has slain brother just for a handful of the glimmering nuggets. Yet, without the trappings of commerce, it is but a metal like any other. Pretty though, good in jewellry.",
    "Cabbage": "Few vegetables are as worthy of such reverence as the humble cabbage. Delicious. Easily portable and possessing of a leaf more noble than any other. This, at least, the followers of the god Brassica Prime profess.\r\n\r\nNo one is entirely sure how there is a god of cabbages. Fewer still are sure why there is a god of cabbages, yet the existence of such a being cannot be denied.\r\n\r\nUnlike other deities, who guard the focus of their faith with great jealousy, Brassica Prime delights in nothing more than seeing people enjoying the delicious, nutritious, wonders of the humble cabbage.\r\n\r\nBest enjoyed fried or mixed into a stew. Cabbage wine is a failed experiment that should never be spoken of again.",
    "FireSpirit_name": "Fire Spirit",
    "WeakAntiburnPotion": "Fire is one of the first discoveries made by man, burns came shortly after. Fires warm, they illuminate and they create opportunities for progress, yet their touch scorches and scars, destroying everything they touch. An antiburn potion undoes some of this danger. They add a thin coating to the skin, via the drinker’s natural sweating process, that can resist the rage of the flame just a little longer.",
    "RatRoast": "First discovered by a Lumbridge chef after a kitchen fire burned his mentor to ashes. Beneath the master chef’s hat was a deliciously roasted rat. Sure, it may not be the greatest delicacy of all time, but it’ll keep hunger at bay.",
    "FuelPellet": "Flammable objects pressed together into a smaller, even more flammable object. The fuel pellet burns hotter and stronger than basic fuels, making it much more desirable for more complex smithing techniques. ",
    "Haunch": "Flavourful roasted meat. A comforting meal to enjoy in the wilds of Ashenfall.",
    "Flax": "Flax was a popular plant during the time of Guthix. Found all over the continent, it could be easily harvested and refined into various materials. Villagers would enjoy the seeds as tasty snacks, but they mostly prized the flax for the threads that could be woven from the stems of the plant.",
    "IronCrossbowLimbs": "Following certain incidents with cats and crossbow limbs, a superstition began to build in Bramblemead about the danger of crossbows and felines. It was considered bad luck to house a cat in the same home as a crossbow, with reasoning ranging from the safety of the cat, to the safety of the human population should the cats rise up and develop opposable thumbs.\r\n\r\nWhilst harmless at first, when the need for crossbows grew due to the conflict with the garou, cats found themselves with fewer and fewer homes willing to house them. Over the years this led to domestic cats being a rare sight in Bramblemead.\r\n\r\nIt also created a deep-seated resentment in the cats, and they plotted their revenge. Most would tell you that cats had nothing to do with the destruction of Bramblemead and the Brynmoor settlements, yet if you ask a cat, they will simply purr with a sinister grin on their face.",
    "Sustenance": "Food is a basic need for all living creatures. We consume, or we die. Life on Ashenfall is no different.\r\n\r\nYour Sustenance meter tracks how hungry you are. It is filled by eating food and it will deplete over time as you exert yourself trying to survive the wilds of Ashenfall.\r\n\r\nA high Sustenance meter causes your health to regenerate more quickly. However, should you run out of Sustenance, your health will start depleting as your body canibalises itself in an attempt to survive.\r\n\r\nKeep an eye on how hungry you are. Higher tier foods will provide more Sustenance than lower tier foods, but berries will help you survive in a pinch.",
    "BoneArrow": "For a short time, a necromancer arrived on Ashenfall. She had found her way here by boat, fleeing pirates who wanted revenge for her misdeeds. Her skills were limited; she could not raise undead legions, but she had a knack for controlling simple fangs and bones. \r\n\r\nShe fled into the forests – pirates having found Ashenfall and hunting her down –  where she would unleash fang arrows against her foes in the hope of stopping their chase. It didn’t work. Broken, lost and without hope, she prepared herself for death. \r\n\r\nBut death never came for her. \r\n\r\nInstead, a Wild Knight, who wore a mask made from the skull of a wolf, rescued her. He pushed back her assailants and brought her to safety. Their fellowship after that was unbreakable, though no bond of romance flowed between them. She made the arrows, he fired the bow, and together, they feasted on their victories and kept the woods safe from unwanted intrusion. \r\n\r\nEven now, there are signs of their friendship. Hidden across the forest, miniature carvings of a wolf skull and a bat can be found in places that meant something to them.",
    "BluriteCrossboq": "For centuries alchemists have yearned to create gold. To transmute base metals into something pure and shiny. While gold is the goal, there are other, stranger metals, along the way, and what could be more bizarre than a bar of purest blue.\r\n\r\nBlurite is defined by its colour. It’s in the very name. Yet it offers no real magical, or alchemical value beyond other metals. Artistic, perhaps, it was favoured by poets, bards and artisans of more eccentric tastes, but it still offered little in the way of superior weaponry.\r\n\r\nThat was until Gothren Actillian took a crack at it. He was an blacksmith, obsessed with the less desired metals and materials. He would hunt for hours in the darkest caverns for slivers of blue, ignoring glittering silver and gold. To him the metal was special, radiant and beautiful. The hues generated a joy in him that he could not describe, and he longed to craft it into something befitting its splendour. He was delighted when he found the ore glittering away in a hidden dragonkin vault in Ghornfell's central region. \r\n\r\nHe was never any good with a sword, and his magical prowess wouldn’t frighten a kebbit. Yet crossbows, those he could wield. He was a skilled marksman, and could knock an apple from a chinchompa’s head before it exploded.\r\n\r\nThe crossbow, blue as the ocean, caused much mockery from his companions, yet their mockery soon died as he speared a bolt through the heart of a dragon. Something all of their weapons had failed to do.\r\n\r\nHe was just disappointed that the dragon’s scales were green and went searching for that ever elusive blue.",
    "MixedGrill": "For the carnivore in you, a plate of meat mixed with meat.",
    "OrangeCape": "For those with a love of the autumn, this cape is a must have.",
    "GoldBar": "Forged from gold ore, this glistening block of metal has been the cause of many conflicts over the ages. Stockpiles of them have been instrumental in dwarven civil wars and there are still plenty of people today who would gladly slit their sibling’s throat, just for a single bar.",
    "SilverBar": "Forged from silver ore, this glittering cuboid is highly prized by jewellers.",
    "RotswornWarrior": "Foul creatures, warped by Imaru’s terrible hunger. They were once convicts and criminals, now cursed to serve as Imaru’s claws.\r\n\r\nIn the ages past those who had committed the gravest sins were given to Xikotal for conversion. He would breathe into them a new way of thinking, draconic magic creating a fog in their minds that erased their wicked past and offering them a newer future.\r\n\r\nSome would be farmers. Others might live their lives in service of the village, even taking roles as priests or healers. The strongest would be given new identities, soldiers to stand as the dragon’s forces.\r\n\r\nThey were closest to Imaru when she died and as she drew in the souls around her they were the first to be afflicted. Withered and rotten, these tragic creatures remember nothing but a desire to infect and destroy.",
    "HardLeatherScraps": "Fragments of thick and sturdy hides, these scraps are of little use on their own, but combined together they can become a resilient leather with a wide range of uses.\r\n\r\nThe Children of Shadows would scavenge these scraps across Fellhollow, using the pieces to craft profane totems to their sinister god. Unaware that he would be offended at such a low-effort offering.",
    "BagOfFlour": "Fun fact, these tiny granules of wheat are surprisingly combustible. So be careful with fire magic around them.",
    "HunterStagbow": "Gahl was a garou druid who lost everyone he knew. In the wars inflicted on his people by the other races of Ashenfall, he grew jaded with the cruelty he saw all around him. This reached its peak when he found a garou village in ruins and goblins dancing and laughing as they threatened a newly born pup, in her crib.\r\n\r\nThe village is gone now, dragged beneath the soil. Gahl’s vengeance unleashed upon the goblins, erasing them from existence. He took the child as his own and raised her in a way alien to his people – he raised her with love.\r\n\r\nShe had no talent for magic, and could not learn his druid spells, so he taught her how to hunt with a bow, and she was a natural. Dahnu, as he named her, made a bow out of the first deer she and her father had hunted and that bow became a symbol to all she encountered.\r\n\r\nIn time she would claim a king’s seat. Defeating the prior monarch with reason and wit. Charming him out of his power and offering her people another way.  \r\n\r\nHer people thrived, making her father proud.\r\n\r\nUntil Velgar brought her reign to an end.",
    "Skullsplitter": "Garou children would often play pretend at great, epic, battles. They would create their own stories, true, but they enjoyed reenacting the battles of legend, and no character was more popular than King Raluh, the mighty.\r\n\r\nPups would wander into the forests in search of the sturdiest stick they could find and then announce to the other children that they were the king, and this stick that most legendary of weapons – Skullsplitter.\r\n\r\nAs the legends go, King Raluh had been granted a weapon by the moon goddess, Lougrim. A weapon capable of destroying any foe. Armour was as paper to Skullsplitter and the fables had him crush the bones of villain and monster alike.\r\n\r\nIt is, therefore, no surprise that as the garou pups mature, they create weapons to match the legends, and garou warriors wield mighty maces carrying that mythical name. Though the true Skullsplitter may be just a story, these powerful imitations are no less deadly.",
    "Seeds.Dwellberry": "Garou consider dwellberries to have spiritual significance. Their priests believe that if they are crushed into paste and used as war-paint, they can ward off evil spirits, misfortune and vermin.\r\n\r\nThe seeds are often used in this process, creating a powdery texture that can make it easier to spread the paint across the fur.",
    "VelgarsHead": "General Velgar was one of Queen Kuldra’s most feared disciples. Infused with the power of the earth itself, his poison corrupted entire regions and slaughtered countless foes.\r\n\r\nThe fall of Blue Flame Keep, Bramblemead village, and the Saradominist presence on Ashenfall, was orchestrated by Velgar’s hand. His army incinerated villagers, priests and soldiers alike. His forces tore down castles and houses with equal relish.\r\n\r\nFor thousands of years, Velgar has been a thorn in everyone’s side, until one hero took him down.\r\n\r\nBring this to the Wise OId Man, rumours are that he can make something useful from it.",
    "PungentOmelette": "Get a nose peg for this one. The aroma that wafts from this culinary creation is almost unholy in its sensation. It reaches down from your nostrils into your throat, then it winds its way into your mouth and twists your tastebuds in ways that must surely be illegal.\r\n\r\nStill, if you can fight through the stench, then this eggy abomination is surprisingly tasty and helps fortify your body against toxic elements.\r\n\r\nThe “if” is doing a lot of work in that statement though.",
    "RingOfTheSun": "Glittering during the daytime, the magic of this ring makes you feel more alive in the light of the sun. It was popular among farmers who spent their lives toiling in the fields, beneath the glare of the noonday sun.  ",
    "FruitPie": "Grannies everywhere are masters of this dish. Delicious fruits, a touch of sugar, some delightful pastry and that final ingredient – a dark blessing to ancient gods.\r\n\r\nOr love, that works too.\r\n\r\nWith the pact complete, these pies allow you those fond memories, of comfort, hope and family. Something much needed out in the wilds of Ashenfall.",
    "Seeds.Redberry": "Grow your own tasty berries with these lovely little seeds. In ancient Ashenfall, young villagers would gift a potential romantic partner with a handful of berries, and if the plant flourished it was seen as proof that they were meant to be together.\r\n\r\nMost considered it a rather cheap and rubbish gift, and so contrary to the folklore, these rarely resulted in meaningful relationships.",
    "FireRunes": "Guthix created runes in the first age, here on Ashenfall. He found a way of capturing the ambient wild anima, of the continent, in the floating rune essence around the region. He would then give these runes to his druids, teaching them the fundamentals of magic.\r\n\r\nFire runes contain enough magical fire to light a campfire. Battlemages prize them for the ability to lob that small fire at an enemy instead. Either way, they’re a very handy resource.",
    "Seeds.Cabbage": "Hail Brassica Prime, lord of deliciousness and nutrition! Within these tiny seeds rest all the power of creation, ready to burst forth into spherical orbs of crisp deliciousness.\r\n\r\nGuthix once encountered Brassica Prime when he wandered the moors of Ashenfall. On a strange outcrop, beneath a waterfall that reflected the silver moonlight, he spied a cabbage so large and so full of nutrition that he was briefly stunned at its magnificence.\r\n\r\nWhen the cabbage spoke, the god of balance found himself quite at a loss. According to the cabbage, he was the lord of all vegetables, and keeper of the ancient secrets of a balanced diet, seeing some small kinship in Guthix’s search for harmony. They spoke long and strange conversations, deep into the night, about the nature of magic, divinity and, of course, food.\r\n\r\nBrassica’s stories changed every night. Each moonrise offered a new excuse for his divinity, and new lessons on how he had supposedly shaped creation itself, by mixing together the base vitamins needed for the universe to thrive.\r\n\r\nMad, perhaps, or just composed of an alien way of thinking, Guthix took some small comfort in his strange new friend. As such, he made a point of having his druids sow cabbage seeds in every field, and around all his settlements, so that Brassica might be honoured in some small way.",
    "HardLeatherArmourBody": "Hardened leather is a sturdier defence, able to turn away more powerful attacks. Favoured by hunters and rangers, who like the defence it offers without the reduction in mobility brought on by sturdier armour.\r\n\r\nDuring the schism and the inner conflicts between ‘pure’ humans and the Amalgamated, Wild Knights would sneak into the village and rescue persecuted Amalgamated under the dead of night. Their armour was silent enough to allow them to move through the village quietly, and they had enough mobility to make a quick escape if caught.\r\n\r\nThose who captured Amalgamated were rarely well-equipped soldiers, and their knives were often turned away by the hardness of the leather. During those dark times, this simple armour saved many lives.  ",
    "HardLeatherArmourLegs": "Hardened leather is a sturdier defence, able to turn away more powerful attacks. Favoured by hunters and rangers, who like the defence it offers without the reduction in mobility brought on by sturdier armour.\r\n\r\nIn poor weather, particularly rain and cold, the armour would create a distinctive creaking noise. Barely audible for the most part, but in the silence of night it felt like a deafening roar. Wild Knights made up for the sound of their armour, as they crept through the streets to rescue Amalgamated, by using simple spells to distract people.\r\n\r\nThey called animal spirits, notably crows, ravens and magpies, which would caw angrily at anyone nearby. This would earn the term “dark tidings”, referring to whenever the Wild Knights were seen near town – in reference to the magpie spirits they would summon.  ",
    "HardLeatherArmourHead": "Hardened leather is a sturdier defence, able to turn away more powerful attacks. Favoured by hunters and rangers, who like the defence it offers without the reduction in mobility brought on by sturdier armour.\r\n\r\nWild Knight hunters would favour this armour when visiting the forests of Brynmoor. Hard leather was sturdy, and the colours blended more easily with the treeline, allowing them to stalk their prey more stealthily.\r\n\r\nFor those of Amalgamated heritage, the armour was more easily shaped around any inconvenient mutations, and many a ranger would theme their armour to match. Fire-touched amalgamated were poorly suited to the armour, cooking the leather as they went. But those affected by plant-like mutations would enjoy blending leather and leaf.",
    "RingOfLife": "Harold has a job for you and won’t let something as trivial as your expired mortality get in the way. This piece of enchanted jewellery is primed to go off when you’re about to land on Death’s list, whisking you away to safety before the scythe needs to be swung.\r\n\r\nThere are many sorcerers that have studied these rings for years, going slowly mad as they try to uncover the secrets of immortality. If they could just learn the methods by which this ring operates, then they could extend their lives indefinitely.\r\n\r\nOnly one wizard ever actually learned this secret, but he forgot the method by which this ring works, for it teleports you at the moment of your death. As the ring does not stop the aging process, this unfortunate mage now spends his eternity in perpetual teleportation – hurled through the abyss, never to take form again.",
    "RotswornNecromancer": "Hatred guides these creatures, a vitriolic disgust at a world too cruel to function. They were once mages who had delved into the secrets of necromancy, desperate for power they were too foolish to wield.\r\n\r\nThe power within them has twisted and rotted, infected by Imaru’s vengeful rage. Their souls have been long hollowed out and now they are driven by a need to infect and corrupt, raising the dead in further service of vengeance.",
    "Vaults": "Hidden across Ashenfall are the Vaults, the ruined remains of Dragonkin research. Each contains lost knowledge or riches, sealed away so that none but their jealous masters might possess them.\r\n\r\nParanoid and wary of intrusion by the “lesser races”, the Dragonkin of Ashenfall filled each of the vaults with an array of deadly traps and they created servitor guardians to watch over them.\r\n\r\nAlthough time has whittled away at most of the mysteries, the cruel claws of entropy rending secrets to shreds, there still remain pieces of powerful knowledge that bold adventurers might be able to salvage.",
    "GarouCloak": "Highborn garou are better than their lowborn kin, or so it is claimed. It is true that the highborn are stronger and capable of wielding magic denied to the lowborn, yet what makes them great is their will to rule.\r\n\r\nGarou claim power not through birthright, but by proving their strength over all rivals. A garou leader must be ready to face all challenges, because to refuse even one is a sign of weakness.\r\n\r\nWhen a challenge is made the two garou battle to the death. Mercy is a concept alien to them, once fangs are bared then blood must be spilled.\r\n\r\nThanes are garou wanting to be kings. Each day they push themselves further, to be ready for the challenge that will see them burn moon-bright as king, or fade away as a forgotten challenge. All or nothing, the garou way.\r\n\r\nEven after turning from the moon-goddess Lougrim, becoming servants of the High Queen Kuldra, the garou continue their ancient rituals. Might, always proving right.",
    "Cosiness": "Home is where the heart is. Which is a horrifying statement when you think about it.\r\n\r\nStill, home has its advantages, and cosiness is one of those. When you build a home and claim a bed, you’ll find that life just feels much easier to manage. Specifically, you’ll find that your Hydration, Endurance and Sustenance bars become much easier to manage.\r\n\r\nWhile you remain within the comfort of your home, you’ll need to eat less, drink less and rest less. Meaning that you can craft to your hearts content, without worrying about little things like starving to death.",
    "RingOfPursuit": "Hunters have always needed an edge, and this ring is just that. It enhances a hunter’s senses and quietens their movements. Their prey becomes less wary of them, and more vulnerable to attack.\r\n\r\nThe garou were particularly fond of these rings, and during the few years of peace between the humans of Bramblemead and the garou of Ghornfell, villagers could barter these trinkets for far more than they were worth to other villagers.",
    "Stability": "I don’t know about you, but I collapse easily without the proper foundations. Well, buildings are no different. Unsupported, you can only build so high, without your new constructions falling in on themselves.\r\n\r\nYou can build higher with stronger materials, or by carefully placing support structures, but be aware that, much like me, your buildings can only be supported so far before their inner demons destroy them… err… I mean before gravity wins the battle.",
    "DamageTypesWeaknessAndResistance": "I have been punched, stabbed, and shot with arrows, over the course of my many years of adventuring and each injury feels like a unique moment. Damage, as you might image, comes in all shapes and sizes. Different weapons just hit differently.\r\n\r\nSome weapons stab. Some slash away flesh. Others break bones with mighty crushing movements. Ranged attacks pierce you with unreasonably sharp arrows, and magic strikes you down with the primordial elements, air, fire, earth and water.\r\n\r\nWe humans are vulnerable to each of these in equal measure, but this isn’t true for everything. Some creatures have tougher skin, but bones more susceptible to crushing. Others might burn easily, while the chill of water barely touches them.\r\n\r\nIt’s important to learn the weaknesses and resistances of your enemies, so that you can make sure you’re dealing the sort of damage that really takes them out.",
    "SteelBar": "If iron had been a marvel, steel was a revelation. Sturdier structures. More durable armour. Weapons sharper and stronger than ever seen before. If the people of Brynmoor had known about steel, then perhaps the garou threat would have been over almost as soon as it began.\r\n\r\nFor the people of Emberwoord, it led to superior farming tools, stronger buildings and better hunting equipment. After all, what need did people have for weapons and armour with a dragon protecting them?",
    "InspiringRingOfWoodcutting.LumberjacksRing": "If trees could move, they would kill us all. That’s the terrible truth that no lumberjack speaks of, instead they silently protect the rest of us from arboreal wrath. When a lumberjack was trained by the druids in Brynmoor, and taught the secrets of the murderous woods, they would be gifted a ring to help them in their battle against the vile trees.\r\n\r\nThese trinkets have helped lumberjacks defend us all from the cruelty of the woods, in a war that most don’t even believe is happening.",
    "Egg": "If you prescribe to the theory of evolution, then this object wins the debate. After all, what came before the egg wasn’t technically a chicken, but something similar yet different. So, the egg would have come first, to then hatch the first chicken.\r\n\r\nOf course, the real question here is whether chickens evolved, or whether they were created by the gods? No god claims to have created the first chicken, nor, indeed, the first egg, but they never deny it when asked.\r\n\r\nSo, which came first? The god or the egg?",
    "MixedMeatPlatter": "If you thought the mixed grill was a monumental mountain of meat, then the mixed platter makes it seem like a molehill. Thick and juicy steak, mixed with other delicious meat dishes.\r\n\r\nGarou would regularly make dishes such as this. Mountains of meat, crafted from their latest hunts. They would flavour the dish with local herbs, and perhaps threaten it with a vegetable once in a while.",
    "Seeds.Wheat": "If you’re trying to reduce your carbohydrate intake, then you probably want to toss these seeds into the fire. If, however, you’re a fan of bread in all its many and sundry forms, then you can start your life as a wheat farmer with a handful of these seeds. Just plant them in a farming patch and start planning the name of your future bakery.",
    "RoastDinner": "Imagine it. You gather around the table, the warm smell of a freshly cooked roast wafts across the room. Your mother places a bowl of roast potatoes, so divinely crisp that you wonder if they’re even real. Your sibling places a tray of freshly cooked vegetables, a kaleidoscope of colourful foods waiting to be consumed. Your father, doggedly sticking to traditional gender roles as if the past is an anchor weighing him down, slices into the roast serving each of you juicy cuts of meat that make your mouth water.\r\n\r\nAnd what are you doing in all of this? Hrmm? Nothing, that’s what! Pull your life together Stanley!\r\n\r\nIf your name is not Stanley, you can ignore that above message.\r\n\r\nHere on Ashenfall you do not have those smiling faces to reflect upon, only the memories elucidated by the roast meat before you. As you tuck in, you’ll recall those moments of happier times, before you got stranded here with nothing.",
    "EarthRune": "Imbued with the harmony of soil and dirt, the earth rune stands firm in a world of mercurial change. Predominantly used to manipulate or control natural elements associated with, soil and rock, it can also be used to reference the permanence of things. ",
    "IronWarhammer": "Imcando dwarves preferred the forge to the battlefield but were not afraid to join the frontlines if their aid was needed. They waded into conflict with warhammers held high, ready to smash their enemies out of existence. \r\n\r\nOnly one Imcando dwarf made her way to Ashenfall, and she came here for vengeance. Her foes were not the garou or the dragon army, but the green-skinned goblins who had earned her ire. \r\n\r\nShe was given a wide berth in Bramblemead, partially because of the enormous hammer hefted across her shoulder but mostly because of the fury that radiated out of her as if her hate were flames and she was perpetually burning. In the rare times the dwarf spoke, she simply mentioned that they were looking for someone the goblins had taken, and she would never rest until she got them back. ",
    "UndeadShovel": "In Fellhollow the dead refuse to stay buried. All save one.\r\n\r\nJenniva Milgrim was a fan of her rest, she liked nothing more than to ensconce herself within her blankets and disappear into a nice dream. Mornings became lost to her and soon evenings joined her campaign and she would spend most of her existence in the myriad worlds of her subconscious.\r\n\r\nThen, one cruel day, she died. A victim of one of the first waves of the risen dead. She died, then awoke again as one of the rampaging horde.\r\n\r\nExcept she did not rampage like the others. She did not cross the lands in search of souls to claim, or flesh to consume. Instead she sought comfort once again in blankets and cushions, but found that sleep was denied her, as dreams are the province of the living.\r\n\r\nThere was precious little capability for thought in what remained of her brain, but a singular idea took hold. Since one buried the dead, perhaps that was where the dead could dream? Six feet under the soil, in blissful sleep.\r\n\r\nSo it was, that Jenniva became the first of the dead to bury themselves, using this very tool to do so.\r\n\r\nIt is said, that if you can find her grave, buried deep in the woods, you can still hear her snoring.",
    "RingOfTheHermit": "In a world of extroverts it’s for introverts to get by, so when the magic of this ring was discovered it made many lives easier. Plenty of people preferred to work by themselves, without needing to keep an eye out for anyone near them.\r\n\r\nWild Knights, who often hunted alone, were particularly fond of these rings and would often wear them on their long missions in the forests",
    "SkeletalHoplite": "In life the hoplites named themselves in honour of the legendary forces of Saradomin, back on Hallow. They took their identity from the sacred texts, creating a disciplined and loyal legion of skilled warriors.\r\n\r\nNow, that discipline has all but faded. They forget the shield walls, or the turtle formation. They forget the bonds they once shared with their friends. Now they only remember that a spear must be thrust forward, preferably into someone else.",
    "DragonTooth": "In the Highlands of Ghornfell lay the bones of titan wyrms. These dread creatures were once the pride of the dragonkin forces. Massive beasts, with wingspans that blotted out the sun and claws that could tear down a fortress. They were destroyed in a cataclysmic battle against a powerful mage with a command over lightning, who summoned a terrible storm that still rages to this day.\r\n\r\nThe wyrms are dead now. At least, we hope they are. All that remain are their bones. The teeth are particularly prized and are a vital component in some dragonkin craftwork.",
    "StaffOfLight": "In the third age, before someone could wield a staff of light, it would first need to be blessed by three people. \r\n\r\nA priest would bless it first, instilling the weapon with Saradomin’s wisdom. A monk would bless it second, imbuing the staff with the humility of Saradomin. Finally, an icyene general would give their blessing, dedicating the weapon to Saradomin’s righteousness. \r\n\r\nThose battlemages chosen to wield such a sacred weapon would dive into battle against Zamorak’s forces and believe themselves invulnerable. \r\n\r\nThey were often proved wrong. \r\n\r\nYet, those who carried the staff into battle seemed to survive longer than their compatriots. The defensive magic of the staff kept them alive longer than others. A demon once stole a staff and tried to use that power for himself. Still, when he tried to channel demonic power through the staff, it is said that the explosion could be seen from across the battlefield.",
    "WoodenSword": "In the village of Bramblemead, children grew up knowing battle. The God Wars hadn’t reached them yet, but the priests always knew that the danger was coming. So children were taught combat in the form of a game.\r\n\r\nCarpenters were encouraged to make little wooden swords, so that the children could emulate the noble paladins that would wander the village. These soldiers were held up as the peak aspiration, young men and women daydreamed through school, about taking up those glittering swords and running off into adventure.\r\n\r\nThese weapons clicked and clacked against one another, harmless it seemed to the adults that watched. But wood in Bramblemead was hardened and preserved by druidic magic, and it soon became clear the damage these swords could deal. Injuries were common, bruises and broken bones were the rite of passage of every child.\r\n\r\nIt was fortunate, perhaps, that these weapons were so painful – because the children of Bramblemead grew up with a better understanding that war was no game.",
    "PurpleCape": "Inspired by royalty, this cape is worn by the most elegant adventurers.",
    "InternalAlchemy_name": "Internal Alchemy",
    "ThornyIritRoot": "Irit is normally much more manageable and leafy, but here in Fellhollow it was twisted into a hateful thorn-covered vine. Mind your fingers when harvesting it.",
    "IronArrow": "Iron arrows are sturdy and sharp, capable of piercing most armour. Brynmoor soldiers regularly employed them in the war against the garou. \r\n\r\nVillage blacksmiths toiled for weeks at a time, creating countless arrows to be slung into battle. They poured literal blood, sweat and tears into the cooling metal, each arrow containing a shard of their hate for the beastmen. \r\n\r\nThe arrows were effective, brutally so. The arrows forged by blacksmiths who had lost loved ones to the conflict seemed to hit harder still. Vengeance manifested in metal. ",
    "IronDagger": "Iron daggers are dangerous weapons in the hands of someone skilled. Light, quick, and sharp as can be, they cleave flesh from bone before the victim knows they’ve been assaulted. This is why they became a favourite weapon of the Children of Shadows. \r\n\r\nThough the cult rarely harmed anyone -- it was mostly a social club -- there were those among their number who took their calling seriously. \r\n\r\nThey smith daggers themselves, and as the blade cooled, they added a single drop of their own blood in honour of their master. Some carved demonic pacts into the blades, though none are known to actually create such a contract.",
    "IronOre": "Iron is sturdy, durable and malleable. Blacksmiths prize it highly as a material for crafting superior arms and armour from. It is rumoured that certain fae creatures are repelled by the ore, perhaps due to it’s rigidity and refusal to change to the whims of magical beings.",
    "IronCrossbow": "Iron turned the war against the garou more than some people would like to admit. They would claim superior training, tactics, and skills of their soldiers, but the truth is far simpler. Iron was strong and iron weapons were brutal.\r\n\r\nRanged weapons proved effective against the garou leadership, bound as they were by strict codes of honour. A thane was a deadly opponent up close, but if you could pierce their internal organs before they were within a swing’s reach, then they were no more dangerous than any other beast. It is perhaps no surprise then, that there was a race to create weapons to challenge them.\r\n\r\nThe people of Brynmoor had learned of a Wild Knight invention, a weapon that would allow one to deliver powerful ranged attacks, whilst still allowing the bearer to wield a shield. They took this design, studied and refined it and then upgraded it to include the power of iron weapons.\r\n\r\nGarou numbers dropped rapidly as a result, turning the war heavily against them. In their desperation, the garou turned to their goddess and she, in turn, sought allies with deadlier weapons still.",
    "GreenDragonhideLeather": "Is it ingenuity of hubris to refine the mighty scales of a dragon down into this thick and sturdy leather?\r\n\r\nTo the Wild Knights it was the natural order. Hides became leather, which became a resilient second skin they would wear whilst riding into battle. Favoured by archers and scouts due to the nimble flexibility of the scales, whilst still acting as a resilient bulwark against sword, arrow and claw alike.\r\n\r\nTo the people of Emberwood village, however, such sights were akin to blasphemy. Whilst the green dragons showed little mercy, the villagers loved and adored their majestic draconic protectors, Imaru and Xikotal, and they thought that wearing the skin of their kind might potentially cause offence.",
    "StonePickaxe": "It is always a sad moment when brother must turn against brother. When we must strike at the heart of our own people. Such is the curse of the stone pickaxe, whose role in life is to strike at its brethren, cutting large rocks into pieces, so that they might better be put to use. The smaller stone, carved so sharply that it has nothing left but the edge, turns the action of a single swing into an force that can shatter the largest boulder. There’s a metaphor there, I’m sure.",
    "InspiringAmuletOfMagic.MagesRing": "It is perhaps not surprising that wizard’s made use of magical jewellery as early as possible. Indeed the first few pieces worn on Ashenfall were little more than decoration, and gaudy wizards would attempt to outdo one another with shinier and shinier pieces.\r\n\r\nAs part of their ever increasing “prank war” wizards began to create jewellery that was enchanted with effects designed to frustrate, or otherwise hinder, the wearer, believing that enchantments in jewellery were little more than silly trinkets. One mage created what she assumed was an auditory illusion, within an amulet, the intention being that when the wearer would try and cast a spell, the jewellery would play an annoying earworm tune, distracting the poor caster.\r\n\r\nThis did not happen. Something about the threads of magic, the structure of the crystal and the concealing spell that was intended to hide the prank, created something unique. Instead of making it harder to cast spell, the wearers found it easier. Understanding came faster, spells and incantations clearer than before.\r\n\r\nWhat was intended to distract instead focused the wearer, allowing them to concentrate in ways they had never been able to before.",
    "HollowBark": "It is perhaps strange that bark could be referred to as hollow. It’s generally extracted in strips, or patches, but nothing that could act as a container. Indeed there would need to be an entire tree without a centre to make it hollow, and that’s exactly the case here.\r\n\r\nWhen the withering began it was souls that suffered first, torn away and dragged into the underworld to be consumed by the cursed dragon. Yet, as time drew on, Imaru’s hunger did not diminish, only grow, and so the world itself began to suffer.\r\n\r\nThings taste worse in Fellhollow. Dreams are vapid and empty, if you have them at all. A campire feels colder, even if it warms you just as well as before. Because the substance, the soul of that thing if you will, is being consumed.\r\n\r\nHollow bark comes from a tree that has been hollowed out from within. Its insides less than they should be, weaker and wrong. As awful as this is, it makes the bark develop in strange ways desirable by skilled mages with a poor sense of fashion.",
    "FortifyingSoup": "It is said that Guthix created the first fortifying soup, as a gift for his chief druids. While the first few attempts created terrible food poisoning -- Guthix was a god, not a chef -- the perfected recipe soon became a favourite once he mixed in mushrooms. This was because the mushrooms absorbed much of the toxins, making it much more palatable.",
    "SteelScimitar": "It is said that the origins of the scimitar come from Al Kharid, where skilled swordsmen would wield them in a dazzling manner akin to a dance. Travelling dignitaries would be wowed by the spectacle, applauding great choreographed sword clashes that dramatized legendary battles of history’s past.\r\n\r\nThey were brought to Ashenfall by a noble family during the god wars, who had taken up the scimitar as their weapon of choice, in service, of course, of the mighty Saradomin. This family was proud and pious, which made them all the more concerned when their daughter was one of the first to show signs of Amalgamation.\r\n\r\nLove, for once, triumphed over prejudice, and the family scorned their wealth and prestige in order to protect their child and they joined many others who fled the growing unrest in the village of Bramblemead and travelled north to Fellhollow. There, they taught the secrets of the scimitar to the local blacksmiths, and the weapon found itself a great deal of popularity in the times to come.",
    "GourmandRing": "It is said that this ring was first created by a traveling chef, a visitor from strange lands who sought culinary adventure. They experimented with a wide range of dishes, consuming foods thought unnatural by some, always looking for the next best meal.\r\n\r\nWhen they found a meal that did not fill them with revulsion, they would don this ring so that they might savor the meal far longer than before.\r\n\r\nNow, this ring is popular with anyone that wants to get the most out of a good meal",
    "WhisperingCape": "It is said these swamps are haunted. Those who wander it at night, tell stories of whispering that bubbles up from the fetid water.\r\n\r\nThere is a coldness to these swamps, that is more than just temperature. It reaches beneath the warmest clothing and presses itself into bone and blood. Heartbeats slow and bravery breaks beneath its touch.\r\n\r\nThe trees here are old and spiteful, their roots wrapped around the bones of adventurers, villagers, and warriors alike. They care not for goblin, human, or garou, they will strangle the remains of any within their reach.\r\n\r\nOccasionally tiny stones will bubble from the surface, black as midnight and cold as the darkest winter night. A villager once tried to craft jewelry from these stones, thinking them beautiful. It took twelve village guard to put an end to her murder spree.\r\n\r\nThis cape is woven from the fibres of swamp plants, and shields its wearer from the swamp’s whispers. Or so the old wives tales claim.",
    "AntiDragonShield": "It is uncertain who first crafted the Anti-dragonfire shield. The dwarves claim the honour, as do several human blacksmiths. The goblins tried to take the credit, but their smithing techniques were not nearly refined enough to be believed. The aviansie might have been capable of such a feat, but if they did craft the shield, they never said. \r\n\r\nThe aviansie that did visit Ashenfall only stayed in Brynmoor for a short time. They disappeared deeper into the continent, never to be heard from again. \r\n\r\nThe Anti-dragonfire shield is a formidable defence, surpassing the strength of iron and capable of withstanding a dragon's lethal breath. Its previous owner, hopeful it could be a weapon against Velgar, was proven wrong. \r\n\r\nOnly the most powerful individuals can hope to wield such an object. Yet, even so, it is said that the shield can be improved by combining it with the essence of a dragon, gaining some of the fury of the legendary beast.",
    "Irit": "It is unsurprising that irit grows in Fellhollow, considering it has such a close association with the dead. Whereas normal irit grows around graveyards and near the temples of death deities, the irit in Fellhollow grows everywhere.\r\n\r\nYet, unlike its leafier version, the irit here is twisted and sharp. It is formed of thorny roots that writhe out of the ground and must be cut down to access the herb beneath. After all, the dead in Fellhollow refuse to stay buried without a fight, why wouldn’t the irit react the same?",
    "ShadowSword": "It might be better to refer to this sword as a smoke sword, given its wispy nature, but there is no denying that this is an ephemeral blade aligned with the world of the dead.\r\n\r\nIt feels cold to the touch and sometimes, when you least expect it, you are almost certain you can hear whispering coming from the blade.",
    "ShockingPlantBulb": "It seems almost unfair that plants could become electrified – a secret guarded closely by eels until now. These bulbs still contain a residual trace of that lightning charge, making them much desired in herblore.",
    "GroundGranite": "It takes a lot of effort to crush granite into powder. Blood, sweat and tears go into the process of refining this solid stone into something more manageable. In doing so you prove yourself a master over the earth itself, though the earth rarely acknowledges your claim.",
    "Zogre": "It was Bandos who brought the ogres with him, in his crusade to destroy Lougrim and wipe out the garou. The ogres were powerful and deadly brutes, that smashed through the garou defences as though they were paper.\r\n\r\nWhen Bandos left and the war stagnated, the ogres discovered peace for the first time in their lives. It was, refreshing. When some of the Bramblemead villagers left to travel north, the ogres followed them. When they arrived in Emberwood, the ogres offered to lay down their weapons and pick up farming tools instead.\r\n\r\nFor a while it was peaceful, even pleasant. The humans and ogres worked side by side and built a camaraderie that could have brought the region into a new age of prosperity.\r\n\r\nAlas, that was not to be. Now these zogres wander the region with murderous intent. Hulking undead beasts capable of tearing a person a part with their bare hands. They see no reason. Seek no understanding. They exist only to destroy all life in their path.",
    "BluriteCrossbowBolts": "It was Mittens that learned how to turn blurite into crossbow bolts. His pet, the blacksmith Gothren, had continued to rely on iron and bronze to get the job done, but Mittens had realised that there was great potential in this strange blue metal. He would ruin sand moulds, crafted by Gothren to forge blurite bars, making them appear as long thin strips. It took more ruined moulds than the cat had hoped for his owner to realise that blurite bolts would match their namesake crossbow.\r\n\r\nWhether they were more effective, is of no value to a cat, appearance is, after all, everything.",
    "DetectOre": "It was a dwarf who asked Guthix for this spell. Ashenfall promised ores and metals that the dwarven smiths could only dream of, but they were often hidden away in the bowels of the earth. So it was that Guthix created this spell and taught it to the dwarven blacksmiths that had joined him on Ashenfall. \r\n\r\nThe result was the creation of wonderous new weapons, armour and tools that pushed Bramblemead into a new era of prosperity. ",
    "IronSword": "It was iron that nearly saw the end of the garou. Swords forged of the metal were death in handheld form. \r\n\r\nThe blacksmiths of Brynmoor churned out countless legions of iron swords, eradicating the village's iron supplies. Each soldier was equipped with such a weapon, and each warrior was ready to cut through the horde. \r\n\r\nThe garou are strong, dangerous, and deadly. Their weapons are powerful, and their strikes fierce. Even the magic of their druids puts the wizard's guild to shame, yet their armour fails to turn away iron's wrath. As humans advanced, iron weapons in hand, they razed villages to the ground and wiped out whole families. \r\n\r\nLougrim wept for the loss of her children, and her cries changed the course of the war. \r\n\r\nIron is strong, but it melts at great temperatures, and there are no flames as strong as a dragon's breath. As Lougrim bound herself to the service of the Cabal, all that would be left of the villagers would be puddles of molten metal. ",
    "SteelHelm": "It was late in the development of Fellhollow that the people needed armour. They made do with old hand me down leather and bronze gear, brought with them from Bramblemead, for the most part, because what need was there for armour when you were protected by dragons.\r\n\r\nKuldra would put a stop to that mindset in the end, but it was the Children of Shadows who forged the first steel gear in Fellhollow. One member, in particular, Sadia Vulketra, began to feel that the order lacked the strength it may one day need to defend against the Saradominist forces.\r\n\r\nThere was no war. No conflict like that in Emberwood, indeed the four major faiths got along rather well with one another. Yet, the history books told Sadia exactly what the priests of Saradomin would do to the worshippers of the god of chaos.\r\n\r\nShe forged a helm first, with the assistance of one of her many lovers, a blacksmith Amalgamated called Vesk. The first helmet was styled with great curling horns, stained black by the forge’s flames.\r\n\r\nNaturally, this style didn’t prove popular with the rest of the village, as they began crafting steel helms of their own, favouring a more simplistic approach, and a feathered plume, to look fancy.",
    "SteelBody": "It was late in the development of Fellhollow that the people needed armour. They made do with old hand me down leather and bronze gear, brought with them from Bramblemead, for the most part, because what need was there for armour when you were protected by dragons.\r\n\r\nNot all members of the Children of Shadows were as paranoid as Sadia, many enjoyed a blending of faiths and would regularly attend the inter-faith meetings held by the elder priests of the different religions. Still, many felt a growing unease building, even in their idyllic village, and so they began to push for the construction of steel armour.\r\n\r\nThey took the recipes discovered by Vesk and Sadia, and they found cheaper and more efficient blacksmiths to work with. The elaborate designs favoured by Sadia and Vesk, were soon forgotten in favour of much simpler and efficient designs that could be worn by anyone, regardless of faith.\r\n\r\nWith the abundance of resources in Fellhollow, soon everyone seemed to have a set of steel armour and for many it became an excuse to forge family crests and trim with filigree that set them apart from one another.\r\n\r\nThey had no idea how necessary these suits of family armour would prove to be.",
    "SteelLegs": "It was late in the development of Fellhollow that the people needed armour. They made do with old hand me down leather and bronze gear, brought with them from Bramblemead, for the most part, because what need was there for armour when you were protected by dragons.\r\n\r\nWhen Kuldra arrived it was as if the air had grown heavy, pressing down on everyone. A whole village fell silent as the Godeater turned her gaze upon them. As Imaru and Xikotal rose to face her, villagers instinctively ran to don their heirloom steel armour, hoping that they wouldn‘t have to use it, but knowing in their hearts that they would.\r\n\r\nYet, it wasn’t Kuldra that found a use for the armour. After Xikotal’s death, Kuldra left the region, leaving Imaru to her grief. And it would be that grief and the rage that came with it that would ultimately lead to the rising dead and the sad and sorry need to fight against them.",
    "RangerTights": "It was on a cold winter night, that Nightingale and Moragrin finally met. The assassin had given up on interrogating the villagers and worked instead to lay a trap for the highwaywoman. A caravan, loaded with gold, made its way from village to castle under the cover of night, and Nightingale arrived to claim her bounty.\r\n\r\nIt was a short, but brutal battle. Arrows whistled through the air, making music like an orchestra. Daggers glinted in the moonlight, in a dance of death that would have shredded the life of any normal person. But Nightingale and Moragrin were evenly matched, until an errant magpie brought it all to an end.\r\n\r\nConfused by the battle, the magpie had woken from its sleep in a mad panic and dived down at the fighting pair. The distraction was just enough for Moragrin’s blade to pierce the Nightingale’s heart and as she pulled her mask from her victim, Moragrin’s world shattered as she saw the moment the life left the eyes of her younger sister.",
    "SteelGreatsword": "It was the followers of Armadyl who enjoyed the greatsword so much, in defiance of the bows that were more the more traditional choice. They said that it reminded them of the legend of the godsword, and would lament not knowing the secrets of forging one, as it would be quite useful against the godeater, Kuldra, and her call for humanity’s death.\r\n\r\nSadly for the villagers, the art of godsword creation was never discovered, so they turned to steel instead. Whilst significantly less flashy, and almost certainly not going to terrify any deities, a steel sword will do significant damage to anything not divinely attuned.",
    "AshStaff": "It's not entirely clear why a staff helps channel magic. Druids would claim that it was because they were working with nature, aligning the magic spell through the plant's systems. Members of the wizard's guild argued that it wasn't that the wood itself was special but that they worked as a focus, helping to channel the mind of the wizard rather than the wood being special.\r\n\r\nThe inclusion of rune essence in a staff's design is a testament to the meticulous craftsmanship and artistry involved. This essence, often crushed into a fine powder and dispersed throughout the wood's careful grooves, creates a magical circulatory system within the staff. In this way, the staff becomes a living entity, with the wizard as its beating heart, channelling power through its veins to unleash upon its foes. \r\n\r\nA staff is rarely helpful for less violent magic. They are brute force weapons, where magic is forced into elemental blasts, obliterating the target. More subtle spells rely on the caster's will and are powered directly by their magical skill. ",
    "FocusedCookingPotion": "It’s easy to forget that cooking is more than just throwing ingredients into the pot and applying fire. There are balances of flavours. Subtle blendings of ingredients to create that perfect mix. This potion assists with that by enhancing the smell and tastebuds of the chef, so they can learn just how much of an ingredient needs to be added for optimal results.",
    "TheStorySoFar": "It’s not every day that an entire new continent appears out of nowhere, but Ashenfall really wanted to be different to everywhere else. They say that the clouds just parted and there it was, an entire region, unveiled as if by magic. \r\n\r\nWhich, of course it was. \r\n\r\nThe lure of exploration sparked a great deal of interest. Wealthy backers reached out to adventurers across the realm and promised them a land of wonder, mystery and undiscovered riches. \r\n\r\n“Riches” appealed immediately to the old hero Dionysius, aka the Wise Old Man, who never felt that he really got the life of luxury he deserved for all his decades of selfless heroism. He met with the backers and they hastily agreed to make him the leader of this expedition. More accurately he told them he would be the leader, and none of them had a strong enough shield to disagree. \r\n\r\nSeeing it as a learning opportunity, the Wise Old Man reached out to his latest student, a young man named Cathan, and encouraged him to join up. After all, what better training could you offer than the wild and dangerous unknown? \r\n\r\nIn turn, Cathan encouraged other adventurers, including his other tutors, Zanik – the cave goblin adventurer, and Vannaka – a slayer master with a need to prove himself. At first they turned him down, but stories and legends of these new lands soon reached the two of them and they could not turn their curiosity away. \r\n\r\nFinally, the dwarf Doric, found himself lured into the expedition, after a very foolish night’s drinking. It had seemed like such a good idea when his brain was swimming in mead, but in the morning after, once he’d signed himself up, he found himself overcome with regret. \r\n\r\nStill, along with a small group of brave adventurers, the expedition members set forth, sailing north from the mainland, across the ocean in search of new horizons. \r\n\r\nIt didn’t go according to plan. \r\n\r\nAs they reached the shores of Ashenfall, and the lush green region of Brynmoor, the sky darkened, and the air filled with the deafening sounds of beating wings. Dragons, a legion of them, descended from the sky and tore the expedition fleet apart. As the fires tore through the ships, the dragons laughed. \r\n\r\n“Queen Kuldra bids you welcome!” they sneered as the expedition turned to ash. \r\n\r\nOnly a few members made it to shore, dragging themselves onto the land, exhausted, beaten and bruised. For days they survived by the skin of their teeth, relying on the strange magic that this continent provided. Anima was freely available, bursting out of the very earth itself. Magic, pretty much on tap. \r\n\r\nWhen they found the remnants of human civilization, it raised questions. Who had lived here? What had happened to them? Though the dark shape of the dragon, Velgar, in the sky, felt like the answer to the last question. \r\n\r\nWithin a temple on the top of the hill, overlooking the endless blue seas, they found the remnants of an ancient lodestone and this sparked the opportunity they needed. After repairing the lodestone the Wise Old Man reached out with a magical message to the waiting explorers in the mainland: “Come to Ashenfall, be ready for trouble.”\r\n\r\nYou didn’t have much going for you on the mainland. A series of unfortunate events had robbed you of home, wealth and status. You had nothing to your name but the clothes on your back and when you heard the message you knew you had nothing to lose. \r\n\r\nThis adventure offered a new start, a chance to make something of yourself. So you took the opportunity and answered the call. \r\n\r\nAshenfall awaits!\r\n",
    "Rocksplosion": "Kethren was a wizard who could rarely sit still. He found his thoughts flittering around the place like bees on too much sugar. As an apprentice, he was tasked with shadowing the Brynmoor miners, to see if there was anyway that magic could assist them in their tasks. He found it interminably dull. \r\n\r\nThe slow clang of metal against metal. The painfully tedious focus on one piece of ore at a time. It was too much for him, and his solution was simple – what if the stone went boom? Imbued with chaotic instability, his spell would turn an ore cluster into an explosion waiting to happen. The result was that ore was gathered far more quickly, and Kethren was banned from a second cup of coffee a day. ",
    "Seeds.Potato": "Know this secret, child, the potato is a source to foods both delicious and dangerous. They can be baked, roasted over the fire to create a delicious fluffy centre encased within a crisp and tasty core. Or they can be combined with butter and milk to craft a small mountain of creamy extravagance that can serve both as meal and sculpture tool.\r\n\r\nThese seeds, small and unremarkable, can be transformed into meals of such exquisite flavour, that they can end wars. Or, as was the case of the feud between the Sherrydoe and Nab families, start one -- that ended up in a house burning to the ground, three failed marriages and a cow being briefly elected mayor. It’s best not to ask. ",
    "StoneBoulder": "Large boulders can be found just about anywhere. With even the most basic pickaxe they can be reduced to little more than pebbles, which are much more useful for crafting. It is perhaps humbling to see theses centuries old stones reduced to nothing. ",
    "SandstoneDeposit": "Large sandstone deposits can be mined for this useful crafting material. ",
    "BronzeSword": "Legend has it that when the first sword was forged, Guthix wept. Given that Guthix commissioned warriors and soldiers and, at times, encouraged war, this is perhaps not a truthful statement. Yet, the sword is a weapon with a singular purpose: to end the life of another. Of course, this is true of all weapons, but none so iconic as the blade. \r\n\r\nChildren would play in the street, striking one another with wooden blades rather than clubs or daggers. They would pretend to be knights rather than brigands or priests. To wield a blade was a sign of bravery. To draw a sword was the hero’s stance. \r\n\r\nWar shatters all childhood dreams. \r\n\r\nNothing removes the nonsense of chivalry more than seeing the life leaving the eyes of a terrified foe. A sword puts the wielder in that moment, forcing them to face what it is they destroy. To understand that there are no such things as heroes, only warrior against warrior and knowing that one of them will fall. ",
    "WillowTree": "Legends say that willow trees house the weeping spirits of those who died in the woods. Anywhere but Fellhollow this would be nonsense, but in a land so heavily infused with the dead, you can’t rule it out.",
    "WeakAntishockPotion": "Lightning is a primal fear and for very good reason. While it is rare on the mainland to feel its crackling sting, in the Highlands of Ghornfell lightning is far more vindictive. Ancient magic, formed from grudge after grudge, infects the clouds and fills them with hatred. Lightning loathes people and will strike them if it can.\r\n\r\nAn antishock potion can help weather this fury. It works by adjusting the flow of energy over the drinker, conducting much of the charge away from the flesh. This imperfect barrier reduces the shock on the system, which can make all the difference between life and death.",
    "SteelWarhammer": "Lionel wasn’t good at much in life, but he could swing a hammer and he could swing it hard. As one of the Amalgamated, born with hair of metal and skin of stone, Lionel worried that he would be as hated as the Amalgamated in Brynmoor had been, but Fellhollow proved a kinder place.\r\n\r\nInstead of being feared and shunned, his unnatural strength became a source of much pride in his family and friends. Locals would challenge him to heft heavier and heavier things, cheering and lavishing him with praise as he did so.\r\n\r\nLife had been good. He was happy. So, of course, it all had to end.\r\n\r\nWhen the dead began to rise, arrows and blades seemed less effective than before, but crushing force still got the job done. His family and friends escaped to the Silverthorn Keep and Lionel waited at the guardhouse, standing as a wall against the tide of the undead. Countless zombies and skeletons fell to his hammer, reduced to little more than powder in a few careful swings. He could have stemmed the tide if that were all, but when the Zogre descended upon him, even Lionel’s Warhammer eventually broke against the strain.",
    "AshPlanks": "Logs are great, but their shape can cause trouble in construction. Planks can be fitted into much more interesting patterns, which can help in building sturdier structures.",
    "DeathAndGravestones": "Look, there’s no sugar coating this, but one day we’re all going to die. Especially you. Especially here. Ashenfall is a violent and dangerous place, where even the chickens want you dead.\r\n\r\nWhen your Hitpoints reaches zero, that’s it, you’re dead. Fortunately, unless you’re particularly hardcore, you’ll find that death isn’t the end you expect. For some reason, your soul is preserved, and rather than journeying to the world beyond, you’ll wake up again with only your dignity suffering any permanent damage.\r\n\r\nIf you’ve claimed a bed for yourself, you’ll return to there on your death. Otherwise, you’ll find yourself back at the old Saradomin temple.\r\n\r\nSadly, whatever mysterious force saves you from eternity, appears to have a bad back and whilst they’re happy to lug you all the way to safety, your stuff is another matter. When you die, you’ll keep your runes, quest items and the clothes you have equipped, but everything else will be kept in a gravestone awaiting your return.\r\n\r\nIf you can make your way back to your gravestone, you’ll be able to reclaim all your lost equipment and resources, and continue your journey.\r\n\r\nIn hard mode, you can only have one gravestone at a time. So, if you die a second time, before reclaiming your gear, you’ll lose everything your first grave was protecting.",
    "FocusedArtisanPotion": "Looking and smelling suspiciously like coffee, this potion lets crafters sit down and create their favourite things. Tapestries, wood carvings, anything that requires time and focus, can be enhanced with the focus this potion provides.",
    "HearryCrunchies": "Loosely based on gnomish cooking, these delicious fritters make for hearty and comforting meals.",
    "PoisonedIronArrows": "Lougrim abhorred the use of poison on arrows, she felt it cheapened the hunt. The mages could wield it in their spells, but a hunter should produce smooth, quick kills, creating as little suffering as possible.\r\n\r\nMost garou, in turn, would at first refuse to use poisons on their arrows. They followed her teachings, and they hunted swiftly and cleanly. After Lougrim’s fall it became clear just how foolish it was to seek such a merciful path, their new High Queen had made it clear that they should use all tools at their disposal.",
    "GrievingMoonStaff": "Lougrim was a moon deity, born to a cold world of soft silvers and silence. Her people lived lives of quiet contemplation, each moment spent wondering about the next. They were passive, soft spoken and sedentary, which drove Lougrim wild.\r\n\r\nWhen a doorway to another world appeared and a grieving god wandered through it, Lougrim took the opportunity to run. To flee to another place, and live a life that she could endure.\r\n\r\nHistory skips the next bit. Her closest allies have never learned what elevated her from her previous form to that of the goddess of the hunt, but whatever it is was profound enough to change her completely. She no longer resembled her silent kindred. She was quiet, yes, but in the way a tiger is moments before the kill. She is contemplative, true, but it was the stillness of a spider waiting for its prey.\r\n\r\nWhen she found the garou, she knew she had found her real kindred. They were violent and deadly, and they yearned for the hunt. When she took her place as their goddess, she focused and refined them, creating hunters that were capable of felling almost any prey.\r\n\r\nYet still she kept the moon as her symbol, a reminder of her home and how far she has come since leaving it.\r\n\r\nNow her priests bear the symbol, atop their staves and as the focus on their altars. They accept and revere Kuldra as their High Queen, yet they grieve the moon goddess that made them who they are today.",
    "CurseFragment.SilverthornKeep": "Love conquers all, but hate doesn’t care about that. Hate doesn’t want to rule. It doesn’t want to control, or own, or nurture. Hate seeks only to raze everything to the ground, salt the earth and set fire to all that remains.\r\n\r\nThis fragment of a curse sets a flame of hate alight within you, urging you to tear down Imaru’s reign and leave nothing but ruin in its wake.",
    "WitherIncreasePotion": "Love is a strange emotion, powerful enough to start wars and destroy nations. It overrides reason, instilling all it afflicts with a beautiful sort of madness that many never choose to cure.\r\n\r\nIn Fellhollow it was the reason for many a life cut short. One man, Haager, was in love, utterly and completely with a farmhand called Claus. The two of them would meet in the pumpkin patches, laughing with one another about the village gossip. On moonlit nights they would dance in the silvery rays, forgetting the world around them and focused only on one another.\r\n\r\nSo Haagar’s mind broke alongside his heart, when Claus returned to those fields as a wither-rotted zombie.\r\n\r\nHe had been trained as a alchemist, and had spent some time studying the secrets of magic. Potions could perform wonderous feats, so he set himself to the task of reuniting himself with Claus.\r\n\r\nHe created a potion that would encourage within him the same affliction as had befallen the love of his life, and he smiled as his soul withered as a result.\r\n\r\nIt is said, that under a full moon in Fellhollow, two zombies can be seen dancing hand in hand in the ruins that were once farmland.",
    "SplitbarkStaff": "Magic always inspires people. Something about rewriting the underpinning rules of your reality, with little more than force of will and a handful of stones, makes people reach for lofty goals. In Fellhollow it was the wizard Falistrius that wanted to make a name for themselves.\r\n\r\nThey wanted to be a magical scholar. A researching who might uncover the secrets of the universe itself. They sought spells of greater and greater power, dabbling in mysteries both profane and forbidden, and came up empty handed.\r\n\r\nAfter constantly searching for power, it was an unexpected duel that would grant her the fame she sought, at a price she would have preferred to avoid. A travelling elven sorcerer arrived in the village of Emberwood one night and the local drunk foolishly picked a fight with him. Unfortunately for Falistrius that drunk was her wife.\r\n\r\nThe elf challenged the drunk to a duel, for satisfaction over a drink spilled, and by local custom Falistrius was forced to answer. They met in the forests of Fellhollow, and unleashed their magic.\r\n\r\nThe elf’s spells were like nothing she had ever seen before. Cold and dark, sanguine and terrible, they tore into her magical defences like they were nothing and writhed inside her soul like a foul nightmare. Against the strain, her staff shattered, leaving her vulnerable and in desperation she grasped a fallen hollow tree branch and urged her magic through it.\r\n\r\nIt wasn’t enough to save her. But it was enough to earn the respect and satisfaction of the sorcerer, who left the region with his pale-skinned cohorts. Ever since then, young Fellhollow mages would craft staves from the hollow bark from the forest, in memory of Falistrius.",
    "WildAnima": "Magic is made up of motes of anima, refined and collected into specific forms. Rune essence can be made to hold these motes, creating dedicated magical components for spells and enchantments. Wild anima is useful for magical construction, but cannot be converted into true spellcraft, as the motes and unaligned and prone to strange reactions.",
    "InspiringAmuletOfAttack.SoldiersAmulet": "Magic lives in the dirt, the ore and the rocks. It crystallises inside gemstones, matrices of magical energy just waiting to be unlocked. In Brynmoor the priests of Saradomin kept the secrets of magical amulets within their purview. They would craft jewellery that would enhance the fighting instinct of their soldiers, and hand then out like candy.\r\n\r\nAs the faith in Saradomin rose, the faith in Guthix waned. Partially because of the more active and present proselytising of the Saraodminist priests, who where more vocal than the quiet druids, but also because the paladins and bronze knights seemed significantly stronger than the Guthix-aligned guard. These amulets being their secret weapon.",
    "EnchantedBronzeCrossbowBolts": "Magic was never enough. Oh, sure, you could change bones into delicious peaches, you could summon water out of thin air, you could call down fire upon your foes and laugh as their flesh turns to ash (if you’re that way inclined). Yet, even so, it was never enough. There was always more death to deal, more damage to inflict, more pain to spread and the priests of Brynmoor wanted more of it.\r\n\r\nThey wanted to see the garou crushed. Wanted to see them begging as the last of their pups expired beneath Saradomin’s purifying flame. So, they commissioned the wizards and alchemists to find new and innovative ways to summon suffering.\r\n\r\nCrossbow bolts proved to be an interesting revelation. By affixing gemstones to the tips of these weapons, not only could they inflict deadly wounds on their enemies, but they could also inflict debilitating pain so powerful that the opponent would be stunned, making the next attack much easier.\r\n\r\nThe priests were very happy with this discovery. The so called “heretics” within Brynmoor were less so.",
    "MagicalMending_name": "Magical Mending",
    "Torch": "Mankind has always struggled against the darkness. The shadows hide a multitude of dangers, each of them longing to tear us apart. It is no surprise, then, that light has been weaponised. Simple wood and wrappings, introduced to flame and then one carries a small fragment of the day's protection in their hands. \r\n\r\nAlthough shadows are banished by its radiance, it serves as a poor defence against steel or claw.",
    "AshLongbow": "Many a villager longed to be a master archer. The youngsters would spend hours shooting cabbages, potatoes and whatever fruit or vegetable they could get their hands on, all to prove they had what it took to be a master of the bow. \r\n\r\nThe ash longbow was their first weapon in this journey. Many wouldn't be able to muster the strength needed to pull the string taught. More still would struggle to fire the arrow straight, missing their targets by embarrassingly large distances. But those who could wield the bow, who could strike the cabbages from as far as possible, they would become the most popular of the Bramblemead youngsters. ",
    "FortifyingStew": "Meat and mushrooms, an alliance that stretches back across time. Both flavours mixing together to create a unified taste explosion. On Ashenfall, the mushrooms are infused with anima, and draw toxins away from those who ingest them. Popular with anyone looking to avoid poison.",
    "AmuletOfStrength": "Might makes right, is the cry of every wannabe despot. Bandos in particular was very fond of this statement. This amulet upholds that notion and does its best to help make sure you are the most correct person around.\r\n\r\nIt imbues your muscles with body-aligned anima, the magic strengthening the sinew and fortifying your bones. It adds greater force to your swing that will crush your enemies before you.\r\n\r\nThe goblin general, Snotknucke, wore this amulet and attempted to hunt down Velgar. He believed that, with its magic, he could fell the beast in a single swing of his club. Snotknuckle was stronger, deadlier and more powerful than ever, but this did very little against the rock that Velgar dropped on his head.",
    "ThanesAuthority": "Might makes right. This is the truth of the Garou philosophy. Strength and cunning dominates all. The Garou thanes demonstrate this might in the weapons they wield. Though heavier and more tiring, this axe swings with deadly force, capable of cleaving through almost any armour.\r\n\r\nThe design is passed down to the garou from templates granted to the first garou blacksmiths by divine revelation. Lougrim taught them about the curve of the blade, and the heft of the hilt. Designed for the hunt, it could fell most quarry in a single blow.\r\n\r\nUnder Kuldra’s leadership, the chosen quarry was human. The dragon queen had a deep hatred for the humans, she found them weak, petty and flawed. The Thanes carried out her orders, using the axe to wield their authority, in her name.",
    "QuarryMasterPotion": "Mining is a strenuous task. Each swing of that pickaxe makes your muscles ache and your bones quiver. Exhaustion creeps into your soul and takes root, making it take longer and longer to gather that precious ore.\r\n\r\nThis potion acts as a pick-me-up. An energising drink that makes you a monster of a miner, able to swing your pickaxe as though it barely weighed anything at all.",
    "FineCloth": "More elegant and refined than cotton cloth, this fabric was very popular mong Emberwood citizenry. They would craft beautiful dresses, fine shirts and elegant robes, many of which included patterns of undulating blue and black in honour of the serpentine dances they would watch in the sky, as Imaru and Xikotal swam the air currents together.",
    "RingOfTheMiner": "Most magic focused on directly manipulating a substance, magic to enhance mining, for example, always focused on the rocks. Yet, the eldest of minors would know secret techniques and talents, that made their work in the mines seem like its own sort of magic.\r\n\r\nThese rings were modelled after one of them, and it is rumoured that the first of the enchantments included a piece of an old miner’s soul. Now when you wear one of these rings, you feel a sense of the right place to strike and the right way to carry ore, protecting your tools and reducing exhaustion.",
    "ChieftainsBlade": "Mouldfinger never wanted to be chief, unlike the other goblins she aspired to be just a background character – happy and forgotten while she worked on her own things. She enjoyed working with wood, bone and, on those special occasions, metal.\r\n\r\nShe crafted a sword, a simple blade that was sturdy and strong, and the other goblins went wild because of it. Seeing its sharpness, they became convinced it was magical, or imbued with the power of their draconic overlord. It wasn’t, it was just very well made.\r\n\r\nShe was made chieftain after the others challenged her, and her sharpened blade cut easily through their defenses. A fate she had never wanted, and a fate she could never escape. ",
    "GroundStone": "Much harder to stub your foot on in powdered form. Stone ground down and down until all that remains is a fine powder useful in ways that big lumps of stone are not. Like cake frosting for your enemies. ",
    "AnimalFangs": "Much more fun now that they’ve been removed from the mouth of a particularly angry critter. These teeth are surprisingly sharp, capable of piercing some armour. Scholars argue that the anima twist of Ashenfall makes the teeth more durable, whereas hunters would claim that the animals here are just more naturally belligerent than others. They prefer to use more colourful language to describe them, however.",
    "ClayDecoration": "Much prettier now that it’s not leaving nasty red stains everywhere.",
    "AnimalHorn": "Nature’s attempt to better the blacksmith’s craft, these powerful horns can do a lot of damage to their target when correctly applied. Fortunately they have a number of uses outside of skewering one’s organs, making them a useful resource prized by all skilled hunters and survivalists.",
    "NecromancerStaff": "Necromancy isn’t inherently evil, despite what some of the scriptures might say. Whilst some cruelly force the dead into service, others make bargains and pacts with spirits, offering them a chance to influence the mortal world again, rather than simply watch events unfold.\r\n\r\nIn Fellhollow a small group of mages encountered a smoking fissure in the earth. From it they heard strange whispering voices in the darkness, promising them power and knowledge. Greedily they listened, making pacts with the whispering in exchange for the ability to bind the dead to their service.\r\n\r\nIn life they performed their arts in secret, raising corpses none would miss, or binding ghosts no one even knew were haunting them. Yet in death, that’s where their true power lay.\r\n\r\nWith their souls withered away and consumed by the dread Imaru, the necromancers were no longer limited by the last vestiges of morality, and with the new powers at their command, turned a struggling defence against the undead, into a horror-filled nightmare where each fallen ally would rise as a foe.",
    "Seeds.Marrentil": "No longer do you need to traipse through the swamps in search of these precious little herbs, now you can grow them in your own back garden.\r\n\r\nWhen not drowned out by the foul stench of the swamps, these herbs have a pleasant odour to them that can attract invisible squirrels to your garden. Keep your eyes open for them!",
    "LesserAttackPotion": "No one is entirely sure who invented this potion, but those without the knowledge were invariably the losers in warfare. In ancient times armies would be given doses of this potion before they were sent off to battle. It made them deadlier, faster and vastly increased their chances of victory.\r\n\r\nThe Children of Shadows used to try brew large quantities of this potion every time they went off on a mission, so that they would have the edge were they ever caught. Many a guard could attest to the power of this potion, but fortunately it didn’t make up for a general lack of melee skill.",
    "EnchantedBluriteCrossbowBolts": "No one is entirely sure who thought to enchant blurite crossbow bolts in Brynmoor. The earliest references talk about Ignatius Wormwood, a failed apprentice wizard with too much time on his hands.\r\n\r\nThe story goes that he was challenged by one of the Knights to a duel, over some spat about a maiden or other. Evidently Ignatius may have been a failure in his robes, but worryingly successful out of them, if you get my gist.\r\n\r\nNow, the knight was a large and imposing man -- referred to by his compatriots as the immovable boulder -- and angry as he was, he had no intention of killing Ignatius, so he gave him a simple challenge instead. If Ignatius could knock him down, he’d win the duel, otherwise the Knight would claim victory and see Ignatius punished for his mischiefs.\r\n\r\nWhen Ignatius pulled the crossbow from his robes, the knight was concerned but knew that his armour and shield could stand against them. Yet, when the bolt impacted the knight’s pristine iron armour, he found himself hurled to the floor and the wind knocked out of him.\r\n\r\nGracious in defeat the knight let Ignatius go, only for the wizard to wander into the next person he had wronged.",
    "InspiringRingOfArtisan.CraftersRing": "No one quite knows why this ring works. It’s as if ancestral grandmothers hover behind the wearer, whispering to them the ancient secrets of their crafting techniques. Old styles, long since lost to tragedy or history, can be restored in a flash of inspiration when someone wears this amulet.\r\n\r\nFor most, though, they simply find themselves in a trance like state – able to ignore the world around them and focus on their craft. Learning more and more about it with each moment that passes.",
    "BagOfNoggin": "Noggin was never the best looker in Ashenfall, or so others claimed. He lived his life ashamed of his appearance and wore a sad-looking sack across his face so that none would fall ill from his hideous features.\r\n\r\nHe found himself an outcast. Mocked and jeered at by the locals of Bramblemead, so he hid himself away in the forests, but kept wearing the sack.\r\n\r\nOne day, while he was standing in a field scaring birds away, just for the fun, he met a lost villager who had wandered too far. She was frightened, at first, and fearful of the strange figure with a face of itchy cloth, but as they talked and laughed together that fear fell away.\r\n\r\nIn time, they would grow closer with one another. Developing feelings that Noggin never believed he would feel. On the day they were to be wed, Noggin finally let her see the face beneath the sackcloth, but there was no face to be found.\r\n\r\nInstead, when the sack was removed, Noggin’s clothes went limp and fell to the floor in a pile and the woman screamed as the silence engulfed her.\r\n\r\nNow Noggin’s face appears on scarecrows and training dummy’s all across Ashenfall, and on cold, dark nights, you could swear they are watching.",
    "BronzeCrossbowBolts": "Not a lot can be said about small spears of metal to be launched at high speeds into your opponent. They do exactly what you think they’ll do to the target, and it’s rarely pretty.\r\n\r\nGuards would buy crossbow bolts in bulk from the village blacksmiths, giving him a steady income. This sadly had the side effect of making the village blacksmith the number one most regularly broken into location.",
    "BluriteCrossbowLimbs": "Not all cats are terrified of crossbows, though most of the Bramblemead population quickly learned to fear them. Some cats could see their merit but were painfully aware of their human pet’s limited imagination.\r\n\r\nMittens was the lifelong owner of Gothren Actillian. A blacksmith’s cat, he had learned how to keep the forge fires perfectly hot by playfully jumping on the bellows periodically – something his pet seemed to enjoy. He knew the right length of time a sword should be cooled for and would make sure his human stuck to that timing by offering useful alarms made of glorious clattering things.\r\n\r\nBut blurite? That was a new one. Mittens could see there was little value to the metal, beyond some weird human aesthetic that he did not understand, yet there was some potential to the construction. With carefully timed meows, and some creative knocking over of reagents, Mittens was able to engineer a crossbow of superior design, worthy of his cherished pet.",
    "SplitbarkLegs": "Not all druids follow the notion that one must live a life of peaceful coexistence. After all, the animal kingdom is full of conflict and conquest, predator and prey, as well as battles for dominance. Some druids feel, therefore, that to truly emulate nature one must do so fully, including tooth and claw. The problem there is that druids are, by nature, generally on the squishier side and so they needed armour that would protect their vital areas better than their normal robes.\r\n\r\nAfter having her glittering armour stolen from her, Lavernius decided that she was tired of being “Miss Nice Wizard” and crafted a version of the armour that would blend worryingly well into the local scenery. She crept up upon her robbers as they spent an evening trying to prize gemstones from what remained of her helm, and she unleashed her terrible revenge.\r\n\r\nThousands of chinchompas, each of them suffering from a powerful confusion, were turned upon the miscreants. The resulting explosion was so bright that it turned night into day for a moment, and the flames spreads with worrying rapacity.\r\n\r\nLater bandits soon agreed that Lavernius was to be given a very wide berth.",
    "SplitbarkHelm": "Not all druids follow the notion that one must live a life of peaceful coexistence. After all, the animal kingdom is full of conflict and conquest, predator and prey, as well as battles for dominance. Some druids feel, therefore, that to truly emulate nature one must do so fully, including tooth and claw. The problem there is that druids are, by nature, generally on the squishier side and so they needed armour that would protect their vital areas better than their normal robes.\r\n\r\nOn Ashenfall, it was the druid Pikenmix who first tried to break out of the peace-loving mould. They learned magic that would allow them to hurl flame from their fingertips hoping it might be enough. Yet, as the enemies got too close, she soon learned that she needed stronger defence than her meagre hood would allow.\r\n\r\nIt was when she was lost in thought, that she found the solution – by walking straight into a tree. Her nose broke in several places and her entire body screamed in pain, yet the tree remained unaffected by her violent interaction.\r\n\r\nSo it was that she crafted herself a helm made from the very same tree. Partially in revenge, but mostly because she knew how strong the bark could be.  ",
    "SplitbarkBody": "Not all druids follow the notion that one must live a life of peaceful coexistence. After all, the animal kingdom is full of conflict and conquest, predator and prey, as well as battles for dominance. Some druids feel, therefore, that to truly emulate nature one must do so fully, including tooth and claw. The problem there is that druids are, by nature, generally on the squishier side and so they needed armour that would protect their vital areas better than their normal robes.\r\n\r\nSoon, many druids began wearing these robes made from the bark of Ashenfall trees. The first incarnations were deeply unwieldy, taking on an ungainly and unfashionable cylindrical shape – little more than tree trunks with holes for the arms and legs. They were effective insomuch that attacks rarely got through, but they made it somewhat difficult to retaliate.\r\n\r\nLater the wizard Lavernius realises that with a few tweaks, the addition of some elegant fine cloth, one could fashion the splitbark armour into a far more glamorous form. She took to decorating her robes with glittering gemstones and silver filigree, making her stand out from the crowd. An excellent distraction in magical duels, but a terrible spotlight for local thieves.",
    "RingOfTheMule": "Not everyone can be a warrior, yet heroes come in all shapes and sizes. The most beloved villagers in Brynmoor, were not those who through themselves headlong into battle, but those who took a share of everyone’s burdens.\r\n\r\nThis ring strengthens the muscles, making it easier to carry a heavier load. But weapons and armour feel heavier to the wearer, as though the emotional weight of warfare is manifest instead.",
    "AmuletOfDefence": "Not everyone can wear armour when faced with the hordes of shambling undead. Children and the frail, become targets with little to protect them from gnashing teeth and jaws. In these scenarios magic becomes incredibly important, and these amulets delayed the deaths of countless lives.\r\n\r\nNot everyone was happy with such utilitarian approaches to survival, however. As the days drew on and resources grew scarce, empathy began to wane in the population of Emberwood. Amulets of defence were handed out to those determined to have the most need, but the criteria for that became a hot subject of contention.\r\n\r\nIt would be nice to imagine a world where everyone pulled together to defend against the undead, but human nature sadly shattered that dream. Still, while there would always be those who would attempt to steal the amulets from the needy, there were plenty more who would stand against them.\r\n\r\nThese amulets now carry an echo of those protectors, shielding its wearers from harm.",
    "Durability": "Nothing lasts forever, just ask my knees. Time was they were spry and full of life, now they grate together like an angry pair of hedgehogs getting in a fight. Equipment is similarly affected. Weapons, armour and tools have a limited amount of durability before they stop working. Think of it like hitpoints for inanimate objects.\r\n\r\nYou can repair an object at a crafting bench, or you can use certain spells to recover durability. Once an item breaks, it won’t be destroyed, but it won’t be useful until you can repair it. So make sure to keep an eye on your gear’s durability.",
    "Seeds.Flax": "Now you can plant these pretty blue flowers wherever you like. Bring much needed vibrancy to your drab and dreary little wooden hovel. Or, of course, you could grow them to harvest them and turn them into thread, but isn’t that a waste when they’re so very pretty?",
    "OakTree": "Oak trees look down on the trees around them, convinced of their own superiority. Their bark is harder, stronger and more difficult to fell than their ash cousins. ",
    "Onion": "Often used in metaphors about layers. Also soups. The soups are more fun.",
    "CopperRock": "On its own copper is useful but not great for weapons or armour and must be combined with tin to create the sturdier bronze. ",
    "UnfiredClayMold": "Once cooked, this will serve as a useful template for further crafting projects.",
    "RotriddenCow": "Once gentle creatures, the rotridden cows were the few cows that hadn't been filled with an ancestral rage against humanity, for the slaughter of their kin back on the mainland. Unfortunately, this gentle nature made them carriers for the cursed hunger of Imaru and now these cows seek to damage the very souls of their victims, rotting them from within. ",
    "BirdsNest": "Once the home to a chirping little critter, this nest now best serves as a means of gathering materials. Don’t feel bad about destroying a little bird’s home, they were saying horrible things about you anyway. I mean really horrible, like so, so bad.",
    "ToxicSwamp": "Once these lands were fertile plains. Great fields of wheat and barely were sewn by the garou that made it their home. It was a resting place for a people that had been hunted for too long, a sanctuary at last.\r\n\r\nBut General Velgar put a stop to that dream. He was injured in battle with an arrogant god, flesh scarred in conflict, with wounds weeping. His blood, warped by the magic gifted to him by his queen, became a terrible toxin that cursed the region forever.\r\n\r\nNow, poison mists float out of the soil. All the water is corrupted by this toxic blood. The air alone is dangerous, tinted green by the vengeful cruelty of Ghornfell’s master.\r\n\r\nThe garou here have grown resistant to the poison, though their minds are addled by constant exposure. They are more dangerous, more violent and more filled with hatred, with an entire settlement that seethes with rage.\r\n\r\nMonstrous creatures roam this region. Ancient nightmares from another work prowl in the shadows, hunting any that might foolishly enter unprepared.  ",
    "SwampTar": "Once this tar was some living creature, perhaps and ancient dragon or other colossal beast. Time and pressure refined it down into this sticky substance that can be used for a variety of crafting purposes. ",
    "Potions": "Once you’ve crafted yourself a cauldron, you’ll have access to the wonderful world of experimental herblore. Why, I remember the good old days of chucking random ingredients into the pot and hoping they didn’t explode.\r\n\r\nLost a good few friends in those days. Fond memories.\r\n\r\nPotions can provide you a multitude of benefits. Some will knit together broken flesh, others might provide you with resistances to the harmful effects of Ashenfall. Still others might grant you temporary perks to make your skilling activities easier.\r\n\r\nYou’ll need herbs to make potions. They can be found all over the region, but especially in swampier biomes. Combine herbs with anything you can get your hands on to discover the wonderful benefits of herblore.",
    "Imaru": "Once, Imaru was a gentle creature. She and her mate, the black dragon Xikotal, would dance through the air together, lost in their love. Although the dragon’s rage still burned inside her, the cooling power of the water she was infused with, calmed her and brought the reason and clarity that she enjoyed.\r\n\r\nDragonkin experiments had robbed her of the one thing she craved, to be a mother. So when the humans came to the region, she convinced Xikotal that they should take them under their wing, becoming protectors and, perhaps, even parents to the fragile things.\r\n\r\nTogether they built a society with peace and cooperation at its core. The villagers thrived, and Imaru and her mate thrived with them. She used the magic within her to bless her new pets, making sure that she could protect them wherever they were.\r\n\r\nHow cruel a thing is fate? Once protector, Imaru became the threat. After being slain by Kuldra, Imaru became a blighted thing. She refused to pass into the afterlife and wedged herself within the river of souls, feasting on the dead of Fellhollow in search of power. Once the guardian of her beloved adopted children, Imaru was lost to everything but the rage and the grief. She would have torn apart the world for one more shot at Kuldra.\r\n\r\nFortunately, that won’t be an option.  ",
    "StoneDagger": "One of the first weapons ever created, the stone dagger has a sinister history. At first it allowed early humans, dwarves, elves and other intelligent species to thrive. They could take down prey and feast on the spoils, using the dagger to pare down the kill, making it easier to eat. It created the gap between animals and people, the first step toward civilization. \r\n\r\nIt’s also the first tool to lead to murder. \r\n\r\nA young man whose name has long been forgotten once created a dagger of glittering black stone. Pleased with his work, he proudly displayed it, earning admiration and envy from those around him. \r\nSo proud was he of the dagger that he would whisper to it, acting as though it would whisper back. On the day he slit the chieftain’s throat, he claimed that the dagger had told him to do it. \r\n\r\nFortunately most stone daggers are pleasingly silent.",
    "Toadflax": "One of the most popular plants with witches everywhere, as much as for the name as its use in herblore. One witch, a particularly odd duck called Henrietta, once tried to combine the herb with the amphibian of its namesake. The resulting monstrosity killed five people, before it could finally be put down.",
    "IronCrossbowBolts": "One sad part of human nature is that they will constantly strive to craft deadlier and deadlier weapons. Iron crossbow bolts were harder than their bronze counterpart, capable of penetrating the toughest garou armour.\r\n\r\nObrimex, of the garou, once tried to replicate these weapons. He crafted little sharpened tubes of iron that he felt would match their power. Alas, the garou hadn’t yet crafted crossbow technology, and throwing these little metal darts largely proved to be irritating rather than dangerous.",
    "MagicSecateurs": "Ordinary secateurs are good for the visibly problematic parts of the plant, but sometimes you need a little extra touch to help your flowers thrive. First created by the druids, thousands of years ago, these magical devices are imbued with a desire to heal. When you cut away the diseased leaves, the enchantment within seeks out hidden parts of the plant that are beginning to succumb to infection, and eradicate the disease before it begins.\r\n\r\nAs a result, your plants end up healthier and happier, often leading to better harvests. No wonder the druids of Ashenfall had the most extravagant gardens.",
    "SkeletalWarrior": "Perhaps these were once guards, or soldiers. Wild Knights protecting the balance of the land. It is impossible to say who these creatures once were, because now all they are is merciless dead.\r\n\r\nThe tragic truth is that many of these people fought as hard as they could against the tide of undead. They stood at the gates to the village holding back the ravening horde. They walled themselves up in the keep, protecting their allies. They stood at the gate into Fellhollow and said “you will go no further!”. Yet, now, they are the monsters they fought so hard against.",
    "PersonalChest_name": "Personal Chest",
    "WeakAntipoisonPotion": "Poison has always been a problem for hunters. The forests are full of venomous creatures, just waiting for a chance to strike. The difference between an edible mushroom and one filled with deadly toxins, is hard to spot on a moonless night. Thus the antipoison potion was once of the first to be created. A glorious catch-all that fought against all the venoms and toxins of the realm.\r\n\r\nThey’re also surprisingly good for a hangover.",
    "RelentlessInfusion": "Popular among Wild Knight scouts, this mixture focussed the senses and improved reaction times. It was taken by hunters as they explored the dangerous forests of Ashenfall, allowing them respond to threats faster than before. Those extra moments saved countless lives.\r\n\r\nIn the village of Bramblemead there was a contingent of foolish youngsters who began drinking this mixture in large quantities, due to the energetic thrill it created. It would allow them to engage in strenuous activities for far longer without getting tired.\r\n\r\nIts popularly began to grow among older members of the population as well, though no one would be willing to explain why.",
    "SweetVegBall": "Popular among gnomes, this sticky ball of fruits and vegetables is a combination of savoury and sweet that almost feels like it shouldn’t work. Yet it does.\r\n\r\nThere is a crispness to the outer shell, savoury and salty, which gives way to a delicious fruity sweetness beneath.\r\n\r\nGnomish ambassadors would create large numbers of these sweet treats, piled high on silver platters, to delight visiting dignitaries at lavish receptions. Diplomats would often joke about being spoiled by such treats. Which is ironic really, as these spoil very quickly, so enjoy them now while you can.",
    "Seeds.CorseCotton": "Popular with necromantic horticulturalists, the corpse cotton plant is as useful as it is mildly disturbing. These seeds will allow you to avoid getting yourself blown up in random locations, instead allowing to access this awful plant in the comfort of your own garden.",
    "SteelGreataxe": "Power corrupts, and what’s more powerful than an enormous axe comprised of a metal stronger than any seen before? Such was the logic of Olga Skulltaker (not her real name), the self-styled bandit queen of Fellhollow.\r\n\r\nAs a child, Olga had always enjoyed listening to her grandfather’s stories from the mainland. Tales of mighty warriors defeating terrifying foes. Purehearted heroes challenging evil knights to great battles across pointlessly high bridges and the like. Her parents always laughed and called her “their little hero” but the truth was she was far more interested in the villain.\r\n\r\nWhen she was old enough to wield a weapon, she trained with the biggest axes she could carry, and her sparring partners feared facing off against her because she always took things too far. Once even costing another student their fingers.\r\n\r\nAs an adult, she found a place for herself among the Children of Shadows. She had hoped they would be dangerous, wicked people, like herself, but instead they spend their time reciting terrible poetry and talking about evil, without ever doing it.\r\n\r\nSo she took her axe, changed her name, and found the highest bridge she could, and attacked anyone that tried to cross it.",
    "SkeletalNecromancer": "Power is a corrupting force, it can turn reasonable people into monsters. Have you ever noticed how a seemingly reasonable person can go mad with power in the silliest of roles? That’s what happened to the necromancers of Fellhollow.\r\n\r\nAt first they were mages, of varying skill. Some were scholars of the arcane. Others priests in training. But each of them was obsessed with the mystery of the dead. They would learn secrets in strange places. In strange whispers on the wind, in ancient tomes stolen from the mainland. Some even learned of the necromantic arts in their dreams.\r\n\r\nThey had sought to command the dead, but now they have become them. Their powers turned only to spreading their affliction, the last vestiges of their souls trying to claim some semblance of meaning when there is none left for them to take.",
    "Dwellberries": "Pretty blue berries, go well in a cheesecake.",
    "LinenApprenticeBody": "Prized for its lightweight and nimble nature, linen armour finds favour among apprentice wizards and those who value agility. Composed of layered cloth, it forms a rudimentary defence capable of withstanding minor assaults. \r\n\r\nAt first, acolytes would spend time at the wizard’s tower to learn about the magic the armour would protect the wearer against. It was common for young priestly acolytes to become enamoured with the mysterious life of the apprentice wizards. They would see them hurling spells with skill and panache, making their lives of quiet contemplation seem awfully dull in response. Many young acolytes would make linen armour for themselves, practising spellcasting in secret.\r\n\r\nThe priests soon discovered their numbers dwindling, while the wizard’s guild gained more members. So, the decree was given that robes were to be crafted in silent contemplation, far away from those who would later wear them. This would keep fewer acolytes from being lured away, but the defensive prowess of the armour diminished in return.\r\n\r\nThe high priests considered this a reasonable exchange.",
    "LinenApprenticeHead": "Prized for its lightweight and nimble nature, linen armour finds favour among apprentice wizards and those who value agility. Composed of layered cloth, it forms a rudimentary defence capable of withstanding minor assaults. \r\n\r\nIn the third age, apprentice wizards wore this armour as they practised basic spells. The more senior, blue-robed wizards guided them in their practice and taught them basic enchantments that helped them resist low-level spells and curses. \r\n\r\nThe creation of these robes was a sacred duty entrusted to the young acolytes of the faith. It was a testament to their devotion and a means of assisting others in their service to Saradomin.   ",
    "LinenApprenticeLegs": "Prized for its lightweight and nimble nature, linen armour finds favour among apprentice wizards and those who value agility. Composed of layered cloth, it forms a rudimentary defence capable of withstanding minor assaults. \r\n\r\nThis simple armour would remain popular with apprentice wizards long into the third age, falling out of fashion only when faced with something more dangerous than a goblin.\r\n\r\nThe beastmen further north of Brynmoor used weapons far more vicious than a goblin might wield. They tore through linen armour as if it were nothing, and the diminished enchantments of the acolytes did nothing to shield the blows.\r\n\r\nApprentice wizards died in droves, and the armour fell out of favour.",
    "WateringCan.Wooden": "Rain is unreliable. It is far too whimsical in when and where it falls. Relying on its good nature is a sure-fire way to court drought, famine and ruin. Because of this, the druids created rainfall you could hold, handheld containers of water that could be summoned when and where it was needed.\r\n\r\nFill one of these up from any water source and you can use it to keep your farming plots nice and moist.",
    "DraconicStaff": "Rasthin was a powerful sorceress, even among the dragonkin. She had mastered all manner of spells, and was even schooled in the techniques from the previous Revision.\r\n\r\nStill, magic as an art was largely looked down upon by dragonkin society. It was considered vulgar, and the obsession of children, and alchemy and science was the real way forward. In part it’s because of the connection between magic and the hated Elder Gods, and the dragonkin feared rousing, further, their anger.\r\n\r\nBut Rasthin knew the value of magic, as did the other members of her group. She created a staff, forged from glittering crystal and anima-infused iron, which allowed her to channel her magic into powerful spells.\r\n\r\nYet, even with a weapon as powerful as this, she discarded her magic when she uncovered the power of Ashenfall – preferring, instead, to enjoy her newfound connection with elemental flame.\r\n\r\nStill, the remnants of the staff bristle with potential, and while they may never reach the might of their ancestor, they remain powerful magic tools just waiting to be crafted.",
    "FireIronArrows": "Rasthin was the master of fire. It was the gift she had stolen from the “soul of the world” as she called it. She could use the inner flame to inspire confidence or rouse people into a great fervour. The fire could reveal secrets, forcing truth from those who stood before her. But, more practical than all of that was one simple truth, that fire could burn.\r\n\r\nThere were many a dragon that tried to battle Rasthin directly, believing their dragonfire could destroy her. Their bones litter Ashenfall. It was only Queen Kuldra herself who could challenge the dragonkin, and theirs was a battle that left scars in the lands for centuries.\r\n\r\nThese arrows borrow a piece of Rasthin’s power. Able to inspire only by the fear they kindle in the wielder’s enemies. Yet, kindle they do, for those who face these flames will surely feel echoes of the fury wrought by the dragonkin lord of fire.",
    "ReinforcedLeatherBody": "Reinforced leather, designed to withstand powerful attacks. Layers of stiffened leather folded together to strengthen one another, turning singular sheets into a powerful defensive force. \r\n\r\nThere was a bountiful supply of leather in Brynmoor, much to the chagrin of cows. Young villagers would be crafted sturdy leather armour when they trained with the guard. Few villagers would ever make the cut of town guard; the demands were strict, and the discipline required was more than many could handle. Yet, those who made the cut were afforded the uttermost respect among the people. \r\nAt least those who were not afflicted with the curse of the Amalgamated. ",
    "ReinforcedLeatherLegs": "Reinforced leather, designed to withstand powerful attacks. Layers of stiffened leather folded together to strengthen one another, turning singular sheets into a powerful defensive force. \r\n\r\nWhen an Amalgamated rose through the ranks of the guard, a tricky feat given the prejudice against them, it did not instil the populace with a sense of comfort. The villagers saw the marks of the elemental corruption on the guards and would turn and walk the other way. They would refuse to report a crime if they saw leaves instead of hair. \r\nAmalgamated guards would rarely be granted equipment of greater quality than reinforced leather. No matter what missions they were assigned. It was seen as a waste, considering the uncooperative nature of many villagers, making it hard for an Amalgamated guard to do their duty. \r\n\r\nIt is, perhaps, no wonder that few Amalgamated guards chose to remain in the service for long. ",
    "ReinforcedLeatherHead": "Reinforced leather, designed to withstand powerful attacks. Layers of stiffened leather folded together to strengthen one another, turning singular sheets into a powerful defensive force. \r\n\r\nWhile metal is the preferred material for armour, leather has its charm. Its wearers boast greater agility and find it easier to hide the sounds of their movements than they can in metal gear. But its most important benefit is the relative cheapness in construction. ",
    "StuddedLeatherBody": "Reinforced leather, with metal plates woven throughout. The plates rest underneath leather coverings, with metal studs to hold them in place, giving the armour its name. Favoured by scouts and hunters for the mixture of agility and defence. \r\n\r\nDealing with the armour’s distinctive scent soon became a training tool for more dangerous hunters. It taught them to use the wind as a weapon. They would always walk against it, the wind in their faces, meaning that the garou could not smell them coming. When the weather didn’t help them, the Wild mages would cast constant subtle air spells, changing the breeze’s direction.\r\n\r\nOne wild mage and her friend, a strange boy who wore a wolf’s skull as a mask, got the tactic down to an art. She used strange, sinister magics to enchant bones to maintain air spells, allowing her to precisely control the wind’s direction. She used this to lead the garou into traps, where her ally leapt from the shadows, taking out the unfortunate garou warriors. ",
    "StuddedLeatherHead": "Reinforced leather, with metal plates woven throughout. The plates rest underneath leather coverings, with metal studs to hold them in place, giving the armour its name. Favoured by scouts and hunters for the mixture of agility and defence. \r\n\r\nMost Wild Knights favoured studded leather armour over the more durable metal plate. Moving through the forests required as much grace as guts, and stealth is always a hunter’s friend. \r\n\r\nWhen they were brought into the war against the garou, the leather armour proved less popular at first. While durable and agile, leather has a strong and distinctive smell. The garou’s olfactory senses were much stronger than humans were prepared for, capable of tracking down the leathery smell from a disturbing range. Wind magic helped to keep the scent away from them, but it wasn’t enough to eradicate it. ",
    "StuddedLeatherLegs": "Reinforced leather, with metal plates woven throughout. The plates rest underneath leather coverings, with metal studs to hold them in place, giving the armour its name. Favoured by scouts and hunters for the mixture of agility and defence. \r\n\r\nWhile the design of studded leather made it excellent for hunters and invaluable in battles against the garou, it fell short against those who knew its construction. Betrayal is a constant foe, and once the garou war had come to a close, the Wild Knights found themselves at the mercy of it. \r\n\r\nThough there were more dangerous threats, with the dragons that appeared out of nowhere, there were always some that prioritized power and persecution over common sense. Daggers emerged from the shadows and drove between the plates sewn into the leather. The poison on the tips of these blades ended the life of more than a few Wild Knights. \r\n\r\nThe assassins called themselves the “Glass Knives” in memory of the serial killer that took the lives of so many Amalgamated. To them, the strangeness of the knights was a greater threat than the dragons, and they were happy to pay the terrible price that their actions would cost.",
    "Rocksplosion_name": "Rocksplosion",
    "UndeadCow": "Rotted and twisted, these cows have a terrible beef with the living!",
    "AirRunes": "Rune essence has the unique property of absorbing and transforming ambient magic into something that can be used by even races without a natural magical aptitude, like humans.\r\n\r\nAn air rune contains anima that is aligned with the wind itself. A practitioner of magic can take the rune and convert it into a gentle breeze. Or, with enough of them, a gale force wind.\r\n\r\nVery popular in the hot summer months.",
    "HealthAndStamina": "Sadly, most of us aren’t immortal. We are each made from squishy bags of flesh, that don’t do well when stabbed, sliced or otherwise turned into a pincushion. In Dragonwilds, we represent the stability of our squishy fleshy bits by our Hitpoints.\r\n\r\nWhen we’re injured, our Hitpoints go down. If they reach zero, why I’m afraid that’s rather it. Well, until you resurrect of course – handy thing that. You can recover Hitpoints with certain potions, or by making sure you’re well fed.\r\n\r\nStamina, is the measure of how tired we are. The lower your stamina, the less energy you have and you need a little bit of energy to handle most tasks. Attacking, dodging and skilling all use stamina when you engage in them. If your stamina is at zero, you won’t be able to perform the action you are attempting to do.\r\n\r\nFortunately, stamina recovers over time, especially if you keep yourself well hydrated.",
    "Saradomin": "Saradomin is known as the god of order. Once human himself, he is perhaps the most popular deity on Gielinor, especially among other humans. He preaches tradition, education and righteousness, and prizes human well being above almost everything else.\r\n\r\nDuring the third age, he was part of the God Wars and waged a holy campaign against the usurper, Zamorak. These two deities remain in eternal conflict, both considering each other their greatest foe. This hatred has spread to their followers and has been the cause of many conflicts across Gielinor’s past.\r\n\r\nThe church of Saradomin watches over much of the mainland kingdoms, with priests and the faithful advising the leaders of entire nations.\r\n\r\nHe is depicted as a bearded man wearing a crown, often carrying a staff. His symbol is a four pointed star.",
    "OakStaff": "Saradominist mages preferred the hefty swing of an oak staff over the more pliable ash. They felt that sturdier oak allowed for more powerful spells. The more durable wood was less prone to shattering itself into splinters when they cast their most devastating magic. \r\n\r\nWhile most wizards practised familiar spells, pushing their research into different practical applications for what was already known, one wizard would push the boundaries of spellcasting. Her name was Cynthia Valtis, and she would go down in local legend.\r\n\r\nShe would have liked to be remembered for her ability to transform twigs into carrots, thus preventing the famine that threatened Brynmoor. She would have wanted to be remembered for her clever application of wind and water magic to create a nice cool breeze during the summer heat. But, alas, she would be remembered for an ill-advised fire magic experiment that would see her travel across the continent faster than any wizard before. \r\n\r\nHer body has yet to be found. ",
    "WatermelonJerky": "Satisfying, if a little chewy.",
    "SqueakingSausage": "Sausages are proof that ignorance is bliss. When you bite into the juicy banger, you are best served not to think about its contents. Nor about the origins of the skin that contains the meat so well.\r\n\r\nAlthough simple, and lacking in much needed herbs and spices, these sausages manage to curb your hunger well enough to help you avoid thinking too much about what they’re made from.\r\n\r\nIgnore the squeak… and the whiskers.",
    "SteelBolts": "Sharpened bolts of steel projected at great speed is a formula for your target to have a very bad day.\r\n\r\nAlthough it was cheaper and quicker to forgo the coal and craft iron bolts, the relentless nature of the undead hordes required a more practical approach. Steel was stronger than iron, and was more likely to put the ravenous undead back in the dirt where they belonged, making it very popular in the last few years of Fellhollow’s resistance.",
    "EnchantedSteelBolts": "Sharpened rods of metal, enchanted with magic. A surefire way to guarantee that your enemies have a particularly bad time.\r\n\r\nIn Fellhollow these bolts were vital against the undead, as they provided that additional firepower needed to take down the relentless tide of shambling corpses.",
    "TinOre": "Shiny and metallic, combine with copper to make bronze bars.",
    "CopperOre": "Shiny and metallic, combine with tin to make bronze bars.",
    "SilverOre": "Silver is an excellent conductor of magic. It’s why it shows up in so many legends and myths about supernatural foes. As a precious metal, it is highly prized in the crafting of jewellry and strongly disliked by lycanthropes everywhere.",
    "Bread": "Simple wheat, water, yeast and salt, combined together and incinerated within an oven. A staple across many cultures and species across Gielinor and beyond. The Icyene of Hallow enjoy a flatbread that they can wrap food in, for a meal on the wind. The aviansie of Abbinah, when the limited wheat is available, prefer a thick and sturdy bread, so that each bite lasts longer. Elves enjoy a sourdough, priding themselves on how long they can keep the starter alive.\r\n\r\nFor the people of Ashenfall, much like their cousins in the mainland, a simple loaf is more than enough.",
    "WhitePlatebody": "Sir Percival Lateef only had a short moment of wonder, before the horror struck. The beach he had arrived on was surrounded by cliffs, meaning his only options were to climb or to swim, and since he was wearing the heavy armour of the White Knights, he decided that sinking wasn’t an option.\r\n\r\nIt took him many hours and several near falls, but he eventually made his way up and down the cliffside, only to encounter a region filled with the ravenous undead.\r\n\r\nAs a White Knight he trained to fight such evils and he fended them off as best he could, yet there were too many for him to face alone. He was forced to retreat, to a shrine, atop a rocky island, displaying the symbol of Saradomin, and there he prayed.",
    "WhiteFullHelm": "Sir Percival Lateef was a proud man, assured of his own prowess, character and moral standing. On the mainland, he would train the other knights in swordsmanship, as well as host several long and laborious lectures on his supposed life as an explorer. He had travelled across the lands, to the frozen polar regions, and the jungles of Karamja, but no one ever seemed particularly interested in his stories.\r\n\r\nTired of being ignored and teased for his exploits, he took a boat and sailed it north of the mainland into regions unknown. His boat was destroyed. His crew left for dead, and he found himself on a unknown beach of strange, unnatural hues. The first human on Ashenfall for centuries, he was finally the explorer he dreamed himself to be.",
    "SearedFarmSteak": "Sizzling on the grill, there is little that has united middle-aged villagers than a steak seared to perfection. Brynmoor villagers would gather around grills to discuss hunting, village politics and anything else that came to mind, all as an excuse to silently judge the way the current chef prepared this simple meal.",
    "MagicalMending": "Skilled craftspeople are worth their weight in gold. The ability to repair broken equipment, and weaponry, was a core part of any craftsperson's trade. But, in the wilds of Ashenfall, such trades lacked the excitement and adventure of more whimsical professions. Hunters, warriors and druids, were the roles that everyone wanted, and so it was that fewer and fewer apprentices took up the hammer. As such, smiths would focus on creating new equipment, and tools, leaving repairs on the backburner for sometimes weeks at a time. \r\n\r\nFrustrated, the druids created a solution to this problem. Using a variation on the ritual that preserved wood, timber, and stone, the druids could turn back the clock on a piece of equipment, returning it to a healthier state. \r\n\r\nOver time, more people began to favour less deadly professions, and the spell would be used in only the most dire situations. ",
    "FriedEgg": "Slimy, greasy and everything you need. There are few things as surprisingly satisfying as a fried egg sandwich. If only you had some bread!\r\n\r\nContrary to popular humour the aviansie have no issue with people eating eggs around them. Indeed many of them have raptor like tastes and will happily consume the eggs of livestock birds. Boiled almhas (a domesticated bat-like creature) eggs were popular snacks on Abbinah, yet those eggs could not be fried as they would combust dramatically when exposed to a naked flame.\r\n\r\nFortunately chicken eggs are far less flammable.",
    "StringyMeat": "Smaller creatures have much less meat on their bones, making them a less fulfilling meal. Hunters would subsist on these while stalking larger prey over several days. The Wild Knight Ilom was particularly fond of drying out these little morsels, treating them as a meaty little snack for boring patrols.",
    "SoftAnimalFur": "So fluffy.",
    "MediumWardstone": "Some materials are better at resonating with souls than others. This is why a home made of better quality stones feels more comfortable than one made from pebbles. When used in wardstones, they allow for more powerful effects at no greater cost to the soul itself. Drawing out more of the ambient anima within a person, whilst minimizing the damage done.",
    "SteakAndEggs": "Some meals require silence. As you chew each mouthful, you reminisce on times past. On loved ones lost. On friends remembered. On moments that bring a smile to your face – simple and uncomplicated, without the weight of the world’s burdens crushing down on you. This is one of those meals.",
    "IronPickaxe": "Some metals tower over others, laughing at the weakness of their lesser cousins. Iron is sturdy, strong and the foundation of many a civilisation. The metal makes strong bridges to cross dangerous waters. It can be smithed into tools, and weapons, stronger than any before.\r\n\r\nAn iron pickaxe is one such tool. The blade of the axe is hard and precise, delivering massive force at the point of impact. Few ores and stones can resist this power, which is why dwarves have long since favoured the iron pickaxe.",
    "SteelFireArrows": "Some people just want to watch the world burn! Some people want to watch the world burn, but have thought through the logistics of fire and so decide to set the world aflame from a distance. These arrows are popular among the latter category.",
    "WeakEnergyPotion": "Some times you just need an early morning pick me up. While many rely on the power of bitter brown beans, herblore masters have mastered the superior elixir. A shot of this fills the body with a sudden burst of adrenaline, banishing tiredness and filling their drinker with pep.",
    "FerociusFurnace": "Some wizards study the stars. Some wizards investigate the ways in which an enchantment can be used to make beautiful art. Jillian Varden was only interested in fire. She would study it relentlessly, she created spells to warm the hearth and light candles from a distance.  But in her heart she wanted only one thing, to create a flame hotter than anyone else. \r\n\r\nThe furnace was her answer and she spent weeks studying how one might push the flames harder and higher, so that metal might melt faster beneath their rage. She created a spell that would revolutionize smithing, yet she dreamed still of hotter flames. \r\n\r\nSoot was all that remained of her lab. Her body was never recovered. ",
    "GrilledTomatoes": "Somehow still juicy, even after a judicious application of fire.",
    "BluriteOre": "Sometimes colours just want to manifest themselves. There is the sweetness of orange. The cowardice of yellow, and the peaceful serenity of a summery green. Blue, tired with only being linked to sorrow and sadness, sought a physical existence. One that could be shaped into new and wonderous things, and so blurite was born.\r\n\r\nA metal that is so very, very, blue.\r\n\r\nIt’s in the name. After all, it’s blue, right?",
    "Watermelon": "Sometimes it’s not enough to drink water, you desire the urge to chomp on it instead. These melons are delicious and refreshing in ways that few other fruits seem able to match.\r\n\r\nSmashing one with a hammer is not recommended, but still manages to be weirdly satisfying.",
    "SteelBarbedArrows": "Sometimes it’s not enough to just kill your enemy. There are times when you really want them to suffer as well. Steel arrows are nasty enough, but covering them in deadly barbs that will rend the flesh and empty the liquid lifeforce of a person onto the forest floor? That’s just overkill.",
    "WoodenSpade": "Sometimes the very earth itself must be punished for its cruelty! These wooden tools, simple and elegant, can break through the planet’s crust, letting you show Gielinor exactly who’s boss.",
    "Seeds.SwampWeed": "Sometimes you don't want to grow pretty little flowers, you want to grow something you found in the murkiest corners of the world. Swamp weed, as it's name suggests, is normally found in the deadly swamps of Ashenfall, but with these seeds you can grow them from the comfort of your garden. ",
    "Seeds.Onion": "Sometimes you just want a way to make everyone around you cry. Onions have always served this purpose, and I often bring one with me to slice into at social gatherings when I feel everyone is being just a little too happy.\r\n\r\nWith these seeds, you can avoid scavenging for onions across the dangerous plains and instead produce a steady supply with them to torment your friends and family. Or you could eat them, I guess, if you’re weird.",
    "Seeds.Cadavarberries": "Sometimes you just want to fill your garden with deadly berries. This was certainly true of Agnes DeWithlington-Smithe, a self-proclaimed “witch” with zero magical talent. She delighted in filling her garden with strange and dangerous herbs and flowers. The more poisonous, the better.\r\n\r\nLocals would claim that she was a retired assassin, trying to hone her craft. In truth she just found something about the plants that she admired, and, ultimately, she thought they were pretty.",
    "GroundSandstone": "Sometimes you just want to prove how much better you are than a lump of rock, by grinding it down into a fine powder. Yeah, take that sandstone, you’re not all that tough as dust, are you?",
    "CurseFragment.Vault": "Sorrow can be a strength, misery can be might, despair can drive you to acts you would never have attempted before. This piece of a curse isn’t enough to push you into the darkness, but the chains of grief it forges give you a reason to fight, a purpose to push forward, if anything just to rid yourself of this burden.",
    "BoneBolts": "Special bolts whittled from bones, whilst only of use for a very specific crossbow, these bolts pack a surprising amount of punch.",
    "Tomato": "Squishy red little things, these fruits are a staple in sauces, salads or hurled at people in the stocks when you want to laugh at their misfortune.",
    "SteelCrossbowLimbs": "Steel crossbow limbs are sturdier than their counterparts, with a greater durability that allows them to create stronger and more dangerous crossbows.\r\n\r\nIndeed, this durability seems to have put a nominal end to the unfortunate curse between cats and crossbow limb related catastrophes.\r\n\r\nAs a result, in Fellhollow, cats still had a place in the home, even after they had been systematically outcast from Bramblemead. It was said that Xikotal, in particular, enjoyed the gentle purring noises that they made and would surround his lair with hundreds of the creatures so that he might be lulled into a gentle sleep.",
    "WoodenClub": "Sticks and stones may break your bones and this club is proof of the first bit. A large stuck that can be swung with impressive heft, capable of crushing bone with surprising ease. Popular by every warrior ever, when they're abandoned in the woods and need to defend themselves. ",
    "Stone": "Sticks and stones may break your bones, which makes them greatly desired in warfare. Stone can be found everywhere. In the grass, behind bushes, hiding in an uncomfortable part of your shoe just waiting for the right time to strike.",
    "EnchantedIronBolts": "Stone remembers, wood forgets, iron holds a grudge. So goes the Imcamdo saying. A reference to how much stronger metal weapons were than wood and stone. Still, with magic, this adage can become painfully true.\r\n\r\nThe first enchanted iron bolts on Ashenfall were crafted by Lina Hesperian, one of the Amalgamated. She had hair that flowered like a waterfall down her spine. Her eyes were crystalline lakes, sparkling in the light, and her smile was as refreshing as any spring. She enchanted the bolts so that they might carry the gift of water, washing away infection for a clean and gentle kill. She made them for the hunters, so that they might claim their quarries with as little suffering as possible.\r\n\r\nShe met the end that far too many Amalgamated suffered. Alone, frightened, in the cold and the dark of an alley, her blood seeping from the knife wound in her chest. As she gave her last breath, she cursed the world for it’s cruelty, and the iron listened.\r\n\r\nNow, each enchanted iron bolt taps into that grudge, inflicting a reminder of her suffering on any that feel the bolt’s sting.",
    "PoisonDragonfireShield": "Strange magics have transformed the head of a tyrant, into a mystical adornment to this powerful shield. It is still strong, still durable and resists the fury of dragons, but now the shield seethes with Velgar’s wrath.\r\n\r\nThe magic of the shield is twisted into a bitter poison. One that yearns to strike and rage against any in its path. In battle, it’s possible to turn this poison on one’s enemies, afflicting them with a dragon’s hate.\r\n\r\nSometimes, in the dark, when the night is silent, it’s as if the shield whispers angry curses into the darkness. As if the last vestiges of Velgar’s fury are carried on the wind as hateful poison.",
    "OakLogs": "Stronger than ash, the oak tree strands proud against its lesser cousin. Oak logs are more durable, sturdier and more desirable for more advanced construction. Though the trees are harder to find in Brynmoor, it is said that Ghornfell offers more of them, if one is willing to face the dangers of exploring it.",
    "OakPlanks": "Sturdy lengths of oak, refined for use in construction projects. A popular material for any skilled architect.",
    "SummonShelter_name": "Summon Shelter",
    "Adhesive": "Tacky like your great aunt’s wardrobe, this sticky substance has a wide range of uses when it comes to crafting.",
    "Leather": "Tanned animal hide has a distinctive smell and was surprisingly popular among some of the quieter villagers of Bramblemead. This versatile material can be used to craft a wide range of items, including armour and weapons.",
    "DraconicLeather": "Tanned hide infused with a dragon’s vigor, draconic leather was a gamechanging discovery to the people of Fellhollow. It was as flexible as normal leather, but more resilient than even hardened leather, and it could be fashioned into a variety of shapes useful in a variety of armoured styles.\r\n\r\nFor a short while, the fashionistas of Emberwood village would use this leather to create garments that evoked the majesty of their draconic protectors, without bearing the scales that might cause undue offence.",
    "GameMeat": "Tender meat, straight from the hunt. Traditionally you would hang the meat to dry out a little before consumption, marinating it in wines, spices and preservatives. However, you can lob it in a fire if you want a more immediate meal. We don’t judge.  ",
    "BloodblightCape": "The Bloodblight swamp was once a pleasant place. Golden fields of wheat, rustling in the breeze. Life here was simple yet fulfilling.\r\n\r\nUntil Velgar took it all away.\r\n\r\nHe fought the mighty god of war, Bandos, and he should have lost. Dragons are powerful, and Velgar is stronger than most, but even in his most arrogant days he was no match for a god.\r\n\r\nYet, arrogance is not a mortal taint and the war god reeked of it. Bandos cast aside his wards. He refused to don his armour. The god pressed into the lair of the great dragon, and tried to wrestle him to the grave.\r\n\r\nHe nearly succeeded. Bandos wrapped his arms around the dragon’s serpentine neck and dug his fingernails beneath the scales. Velgar, in his writhing fury, called down a poison-filled rock from the heavens and cast it at Bandos, striking him in the temple. As the god reeled, he released Velgar, allowing the dragon to breathe a jet of anima-infused poison, at the unsteady god. Still wavering from the blow, Bandos breathed in the toxins and felt weakness for the first time in centuries.\r\n\r\nA smart tactician, Bandos retreated. Better to face the shame of retreat, than risk the oblivion of death.\r\n\r\nIn his jubilant rage, Velgar spilled his blood on the once fertile plains, corrupting them with his hate-filled poison. The fields wilted. The crops blackened and then collapsed into venom.\r\n\r\nThis cloak, is reminder of that blighted day.",
    "PoisonedFangArrows": "The Children of Shadows loved using these. The very notion of bone and poison combined as a weapon; it touched some deep dark part of themselves and filled them with a malicious sort of glee.\r\n\r\nAlas such weapons proved relatively useless in any secret campaigns against the Saradominists of Bramblemead. Certainly, they could assassinate a baker if they needed to, but against a knight it would just ping harmlessly off their armour.\r\n\r\nStill, they made for good talking points at the secretive meetings, making enjoyably sinister table-decorations that paired well with the wine and cheeses.",
    "Kebab": "The Drunken Dwarf delights in the humble kebab, offering it to any that might grab his attention. The delicious combination of spices and subtle flavourings, makes it a marvellous meal. For those afflicted, or self-inflicted, with particular poisonings, the kebab is a time-honoured remedy.",
    "RangerTunic": "The Nightingale was the bane of the nobility, and they became obsessed with her destruction. Not just her capture, they needed her obliterated – gone from the memories of the population forever. But this was a tricky task indeed, because the wealth the Nightingale harvested, was given back to the people of Bramblemead, rather than lining her own coffers, making her a local hero.\r\n\r\nSo they hired Moragrin the hunter to track her down. Moragrin was a cruel and merciless assassin, trained in multiple weapon techniques. She delighted in little more than seeing the look in her victims eyes the moment they transition from living to dead.\r\n\r\nFor days she followed stories of Nightingale, wandering through the village, adopting multiple disguises that she might uncover Nightingale’s real name. In all that time she learned only one thing, that the villagers loved Nightingale and would never knowingly give her up.",
    "RotswornMarauder": "The Scalesworn were heroes. Only the strongest, most skilled and powerful marauders would be chosen by Xikotal to act as guardians of the dragons. They would guard the great beasts as they slept, standing strong against any force that might try to do them harm.\r\n\r\nThey were blessed with the mark of the dragon, granting them some of the power of their draconic masters.\r\n\r\nThese marks would be their undoing, when Imaru refused to stay dead. They became the first of the infected, inflicted with a terrible withering curse that would drag the souls of any they touched into Imaru’s hungry maw.",
    "BronzeGreatsword": "The Wild Knight Kessel was unusual in her choice of weaponry. Where most of the order chose to wield bows, daggers, or swift blades, Kessel favoured a bronze greatsword. On the battlefield, she hefted it across her shoulders, never letting the hilt leave her grip.  \r\n\r\nAs one of the Amalgamated, Kessel’s skin glowed like burning coals, her battle scars resembling faint reddish embers. She harnessed this heat, channelling it through her sword. Her magic set the blade ablaze, a sight that struck terror into the hearts of her enemies. With great arcs of red flame, she cleaved through enemy forces, incinerating them as she severed their limbs from their bodies. \r\n\r\nOn one fateful day, she would use this technique against a force of garou hunters. The battle was long, hard, and brutal, and both sides faced great casualties. But it was when she sliced her blade across the young garou prince that war was inevitable.",
    "AmuletOfAccuracy": "The Wild Knights knew the dangers of missing their target. If an arrow did not slay its target quickly, then too soon would the quarry either escape, or, worse, gather friends. Wolf packs could easily tear a small group of Knights to pieces if not dealt with fast.\r\n\r\nThe Amulet of Accuracy made sure that hunts went smoothly. When a shot landed, it landed hard. Alpha wolves would be slain in a single well-placed shot, and sturdier threats might go down in a small flurry of arrows.\r\n\r\nWhen Silverthorn Keep stood against the rising horde of zombies, these amulets were worn by the keep defenders who unleashed a torrent of arrows into the hungry dead.",
    "Treequipment": "The Wild Knights often spent weeks at a time alone in the wilderness, surviving on nothing but what they can forage and hunt. But tools and weapons, are subject to the curse of entropy and more than a few times a Wild Knight would find themselves alone and miles from civilisation, with a broken axe-shaft in hand. Death would often follow swiftly after. \r\n\r\nThe druids among them, created this spell to save their brethren. It would transform a tree into supplies enough to let the knight return home, where they could repair their equipment properly. \r\n\r\nAlthough not as flashy as a fire-blast, this spell has saved more lives than most and it's creator is still toasted by the Wild Knights on their nights of celebration. ",
    "InspiringAmuletOfRanged.ArchersAmulet": "The Wild Knights used many tools and tricks in their hunts. Looking for any edge they might have against the untamed wilds of Ashenfall. One jeweller, a water-Amalgamated by the name of Lochlan, created an amulet imbued with the communal spirit of the hunters. He would leave the gemstone, that would serve as its base, in the gathering hall and soon it became a tradition for each hunter to touch the gemstone if their hunt was successful.\r\n\r\nOver the years this gemstone took on their pride, prowess and skill and that became a magical enchantment that lay within the heart of the stone. Crafting an amulet from the crystal, Lochlan found a way of sharing that enchantment with everyone who wore it.\r\n\r\nNow, millennia later, the echoes of that enchantment linger within the crystals, just waiting for someone to remember them.  ",
    "WildArchersBody": "The Wild Knights were known for their ability to scout the wild areas of Ashenfall. They could walk where no one else dared to tread, and they were masters of the hunt, putting even the garou to shame.\r\n\r\nTheir archers wore sturdy leathers, using carefully laid folds to shield them against harm. They would break easily, but the archers made it part of their ritual to maintain them regularly. Each day they would wake and tend to the leather, making their armour seem brand new with each morning.\r\n\r\nArchers of the Wild Knights were quick, silent and deadly. A joke their more melee focused companions used to raise on those rare occasions that the knights would gather. Drink and company lead to banter and poor jokes, which created much missed laughter that the Wild Knights always enjoyed.\r\n\r\nArcher, warrior or mage, the Wild Knights would hurl jibes at once another, without a moment’s thought. Yet, when needed, there was no stronger camaraderie and they would all die for one another.",
    "WildArchersLegs": "The Wild Knights were known for their ability to scout the wild areas of Ashenfall. They could walk where no one else dared to tread, and they were masters of the hunt, putting even the garou to shame.\r\n\r\nTheir archers wore sturdy leathers, using carefully laid folds to shield them against harm. They would break easily, but the archers made it part of their ritual to maintain them regularly. Each day they would wake and tend to the leather, making their armour seem brand new with each morning.\r\n\r\nArchers worked alone, it was easier that way. They could stalk their prey, silent and slowly, sometimes taking days between kills as they followed their quarry around the lands. They would hunt monsters and people with equal focus, as dictated by the order’s commands.\r\n\r\nTo harm the wilds was to make yourself a target for the Wild Knights. To sully the green lands of Guthix, an unforgivable crime. Yet, there were always those who would risk their ire, wannabe despots, and greedy fools yearning to plunder the realm.\r\n\r\nThey all met the fate they deserved, in the end.",
    "WildArchersHelm": "The Wild Knights were known for their ability to scout the wild areas of Ashenfall. They could walk where no one else dared to tread, and they were masters of the hunt, putting even the garou to shame.\r\n\r\nTheir archers wore sturdy leathers, using carefully laid folds to shield them against harm. They would break easily, but the archers made it part of their ritual to maintain them regularly. Each day they would wake and tend to the leather, making their armour seem brand new with each morning.\r\n\r\nThe helm is designed to focus the wearer’s vision, so that their target receives all their attention. Distractions are cut out of the picture. Everything exterior to their quarry is removed from view. The archer’s sight is trained on their victim and, so the rumours claimed, they never missed their marks.",
    "PoisonedBronzeArrows": "The Wild Knights were not above poison, any edge in a battle was worth having. Poison meant that you could take down a dangerous beast, even if your initial shot wasn’t a mortal one.\r\n\r\nThe druids among the order felt little concern over its use either, after all nature is filled with wielders of poison and so the Wild Knight archers were simply emulating nature’s tools.",
    "FortifyingHeartySoup": "The Wild Knights would prepare this soup before heading into the poisonous Bloodblight swamp, or when facing venomous monsters. The mushrooms would absorb some of the toxins they encountered, and the soup would be filling enough to last them through the long journeys.",
    "AbyssalWhip": "The abyssal demon is a foul beast. A single one can massacre a village before the warning bell can even sound. Crafting a weapon from their remains symbolizes triumph against adversity or perhaps just hubris in its purest form. \r\n\r\nInfused with the hate of the demon, each whip crack generates unbearable pain. Victims find themselves overcome with suffering as the agony seeps into the wounds the whips leave behind. \r\n\r\nIt is said that the whip even feeds on that misery, growing stronger with each strike, each life taken. The hate imbues the weapon with a desire to inflict more pain, and it grows stronger each time such a wish is granted. Or, so the legends say. \r\n\r\nOnly one Wild Knight has ever chosen to wield the whip in vengeance against the demon for a fallen sibling. Her name is dust in the wind of history, but there are still creatures that hide in the shadows, afraid of the crack of her whip.   ",
    "Windstep": "The air can be wrapped as a cloak around you, making heights much less scary to face. Once, long ago, young druids would use windstep to hurl themselves from atop the highest mountains, descending to the ground below. Higher and higher they would climb, each trying to outdo the other. Until the inevitable finally intervened. \r\n\r\nSince that tragic day, it has been well known that there is a limit to the height at which Windstep might protect someone. They call it the splat-limit, because druids are very bad with names. ",
    "GhornfellPlains": "The anima of Ghornfell is less table than that of Brynmoor, and the anima twist’s effects are more pronounced. Plants take on the hues of dragonfire, making the entire region dance like an inferno when the summer wind blows.\r\n\r\nTowering in the distance is a great tower, shattered now yet still imbued with ancient dragonkin magic. A terrible roaring can be heard from this structure and a shadow passes over it when General Velgar returns. The dragon makes his lair in the remains of a dragonkin’s hubris, a monument to an architect long since dead.\r\n\r\nGarou make their homes across this region, sending out hunting parties to slaughter anything that crosses their path.\r\n\r\nAtop the mesa, something glistens in the moonlight and a mournful sound carries on the wind. A sorrow that resonates in shimmering crystal.",
    "FireSpirit": "The aviansie believe in five types of spirit, those of the water, earth, light, storm and fire. The spirits of fire governed inspiration and creativity and were known among the aviansie people as the Gal'Son – the passionate. While perhaps not replicating the dancing spirits of legend, the fire spirit that this spell calls is no less passionate about being helpful. It loves little more than lighting your way and shielding you, as best it can, from the cold. \r\n\r\nIn truth, this spell does not create a fire spirit, rather the runes attract nearby motes of elemental fire, bringing them together into a single entity. Each spirit is more like a colony of tiny lights, ready to banish the shadows in the darkest places. ",
    "Armadyl": "The aviansie deity, Armadyl, is know as the god of justice. Though young, by god standards, and naïve by many others, Armadyl has a devoted following due to his care for his people.\r\n\r\nIn the god wars, he perhaps most of all, suffered at the hands of Zamorak, for when Zamorak destroyed Forinthry, it took Armadyl’s people with it. Whole settlements were wiped out in a single sweeping motion, thousands of the aviansie reduced to ash. Including Armadyl’s own husband.\r\n\r\nSince the edicts, Armadyl has had a change of heart. While he will fight if he has to, he prefers a more peaceful path. He returned to Gielinor to put a stop to Bandos’ warmongering, preventing him from turning the planet into a war-torn wreck. Though Armadyl holds peace as the highest virtue, he was not afraid to kill the god of war.\r\n\r\nOn Ashenfall, Armadyl and his people did not take part in the dragon conflict. They disappeared deeper into the mainland and have not been heard of for centuries. Though Armadyl has left Gielinor and returned to the world of his people, there are rumours of winged beings living further into the continent.",
    "BarbedBronzeArrows": "The barbed bronze arrow was the ammunition of choice for the hunting party of Lord Gawurin, the regent of Ashenfall. He and his hunting party would head out into the woods alongside a small contingent of White Knight bodyguards. The lord would hunt deer, boars and other game there, but he wasn’t the most accurate shot. \r\n\r\nThis arrow was designed for bloodletting, meaning he only needed any hit to begin the prey’s downfall. A single strike would start them bleeding, slowing them down and making them easier to hunt. The hunt outcome was all but inevitable. Poor sport, some would whisper, but the lord cared only for the end result. \r\n\r\nIt’s perhaps fitting, then, that Lord Gawurin would be hunted with his own arrows on the night of Falling Embers. ",
    "Secateurs": "The best way to get leaves to stay healthy, is to instil within them a healthy sense of fear. By pruning away the leaves that have allowed themselves to fall to weakness, frailty and disease, you show the other leaves what happens if they don’t behave.\r\n\r\nI find that whispering their failures to the plant, as I slice away parts of their extremities, emboldens the plant with a desire to thrive.",
    "Whetstone": "The blade is not just a weapon in the wilds of Ashenfall, it’s a tool. Sometimes it’s the difference between seeing tomorrow and having a very bad today. \r\n\r\nDwarves have always understood the importance of properly maintaining one’s equipment. They turned the care for their weapons into a ritual of almost religious significance. Blades would be cleaned, serviced and sharpened so that they might cut through deadlier dangers and challenge greater threats. \r\n\r\nThe whetstone is a simple device, but one that served as a backbone of this ritual. A smith would learn to gently sharpen the blade to the thinnest edge possible, treating it almost like a competition. They would boast and brag with each other about the sharpness of their blades. \r\n\r\nA popular dwarven myth speaks of the Prime Whetstone, capable of sharpening a blade to the point that no armour or metal could withstand it. Sadly, or perhaps thankfully, no evidence exists to provide any truth to the myth. ",
    "DragonBlood": "The blood of a dragon is a strange thing. It is infused with magic in a way that most other creatures can’t match, a side effect of the alchemical processes that created their kind. Dragonkin scientists needed blood that could adapt to a wide range of chimeric additions to the body. As such dragon blood, unlike human blood, doesn’t possess any particular type and each could act as donor to the other.",
    "BronzeArrowBroadhead": "The broad-headed bronze arrow was the ammunition of choice for the hunting party of Lord Gawurin, the regent of Ashenfall. He and his hunting party would head out into the woods alongside a small contingent of White Knight bodyguards. The lord would hunt deer, boars and other game there, but he wasn’t the most accurate shot. \r\n\r\nThe broadhead of the arrow was designed for bloodletting, meaning he only needed any hit to begin the prey’s downfall. A single strike would start them bleeding, slowing them down and making them easier to hunt. The hunt outcome was all but inevitable. Poor sport, some would whisper, but the lore cared only for the end result. \r\n\r\nIt’s perhaps fitting, then, that Lord Gawurin would be hunted with his own arrows on the night of Falling Embers. ",
    "BronzeMace": "The bronze guard defended the alliance between the followers of Guthix and the followers of Saradomin. The metal symbolized the unity of two different materials and created something stronger. While most of the guards preferred the noble sword, a few preferred the physicality of the bronze mace. \r\n\r\nEach swing delivered powerful momentum straight into their enemy. The force broke bone, obliterated muscle, and often sent their victims reeling, allowing a second strike before the foe could recover. \r\n\r\nThere was a mountain of a man known as The Cairn, for his skill at delivering people to one, who favoured the mace over more noble weapons. He crushed anyone who defied the alliance, providing the force of justice with each swing of the mace. \r\n\r\nWhen he was asked to turn his fury on the priests of Guthix, it was the one time he stayed his hand.",
    "DorgeshuunCrossbow": "The cave goblins are a practical sort, using materials more readily available underground than any that might require potential damage to their subterranean dwellings, such as mining. This crossbow is made from bones, traditionally of creatures found beneath the surface.\r\n\r\nZanik once used this crossbow to defeat a deadly spectre in the underworld. A being infused with some dread malign power, that threatened the denizens of Um. Normal weapons had no effect on the creature, yet for some reason the bone bolts fired by this crossbow were enough to unravel its material form, banishing it back to the darkness.",
    "WoodenShield": "The children of Bramblemead loved nothing more than to craft themselves wooden shields and swords and then challenge one another to single combat. They would play the roles of brave heroes against dastardly villains, whacking one another with their wooden toys. It gave the local healers constant work. \r\n\r\nYey, a wooden shield is no toy and has saved the lives of countless warriors. As a beast’s claws slice against you, having a hunk of wood between you and the creature is much appreciated. Wood splinters, but flesh bleeds, and blood is less easily replaced than timber. ",
    "SteelDagger": "The children of darkness were very fond of steel daggers. They were powerful, dangerous and very expensive at the time, making them the perfect ritual tool to demonstrate their adoration of the god of chaos.\r\n\r\nMostly the offspring of wealthy families, the rituals of the Children were performed out of boredom more than religious fervour. They would gather at secret meetings and proudly display their elaborately carved daggers, trying to prove to each other who was the most devoted to the lord of darkness by way of expensive kitchenware.\r\n\r\nIf you asked any of them to use such a blade in battle, they would be more inclined to slit their own throats in fear, rather than mutilate their opponents.",
    "WeakAntifreezePotion": "The cold is a terrible force, responsible for countless deaths each year. The chill of winter seeps into a home and snuffs the life out of those who haven’t made adequate provisions. It was the herblore expert Valius Malone who created the first antifreeze potion, that held back the claws of winter’s grasp.\r\n\r\nIt was only years after his death, that it was discovered he only created the potion because the ingredients were cheaper than the lumber needed to keep his fire burning.",
    "BronzeCrossbowLimbs": "The complicated mechanisms of the crossbow sparked a small technological revolution in Bramblemead. Such devices had once been the domain of the dwarves, who guarded their advanced techniques to maintain their smithing dominion. However, soon human smiths found themselves creating these complex trigger mechanisms.\r\n\r\nThere were casualties, certainly. Some mechanisms tightened strings too greatly, snapping back with a force that could cause serious harm.\r\n\r\nOne notable example involved the pet belonging to the village priest, a curious feline with absolutely no sense of self-preservation. The cat entered the workshop, as she so often did, and took it upon herself to knock everything she could to the floor. Unfortunately, in doing so, she triggered the release mechanism being worked on and quickly learned that cats are not meant to fly.",
    "CorpseCotton": "The corpse flower is a strange creature. Strange, almost flesh-like sacks, filled with a noxious gas that explodes upon contact with the air. The resulting mass of fibres looks disturbingly like thin strips of a cadaver’s skin, earning it the name “corpse cotton” by the more macabre Emberwood villagers.\r\n\r\nIn truth it’s little more than thick plant fibres that can be woven together into a smooth, durable and, frankly, fashionable fabric.",
    "CrystalSeed": "The crystal tree is a magical thing, a native to the elven world Tarddiad and brought to Gielinor when the elves migrated. Unlike most seeds it is nourished not by soil, nor sunlight, but by music. Elf song reaches into the crystal, creating a harmonious resonance that causes the crystal to thrive.\r\n\r\nAs well as growing trees, this seed can also be sung into specific shapes and forms, becoming a tool or weapon of great power.\r\n\r\nThis seed is a little different to those found in Tirannwn, infused both with the magic of Ashenfall, but also the essence of cherished memories.",
    "Zombie": "The dead should not move like this, yet these creatures don’t seem to have gotten that message. Undead are normally animated by magic, though some are motivated by stranger things.\r\n\r\nThese undead are animated by hunger, and the desperate final moments of what is left of their soul. Regrets, need and pain drive muscle, sinew and bone into motion, creating this most pitiable of creatures.",
    "MonstrousFangs": "The deadly canines of a large creature. Much more fun when they’re not still in the snapping maw of the creature chasing you.",
    "FleetfootedInfusion": "The deer of Ashenfall are quick and nimble, able to dash across entire regions at the drop of a hat. By boiling the tainted water of Ashenfall combined with a deer’s antlers, some of this essence of swiftness is drawn into the clean water, granting anyone who drinks it a measure of that fleet-footed nature.",
    "DwellberryInfusion": "The delicious dwellberry infuses the water with a delightful flavour. It’s easier and far more refreshing than normal water. You’ll find this hydrates you much better than normal water.",
    "DragonkinDarkMageRobes": "The dragonkin of Ashenfall all reveled in power, gaining elemental mastery from the strange anima of the land. Although their kind tended to ignore magic, in favour of alchemy and science, the dragonkin of Ashenfall enjoyed their time as sorcerer kings.\r\n\r\nThey were robes that empowered their magic, using alchemical techniques to create cloth that would resist their elemental powers. Fire would not burn so easily, cold would not damage the threads and lightning could be turned aside with a flick of a cloak. Though these techniques are lost to time, the echoes of their powerful resistance remains.\r\n\r\nAlthough dragonkin tend to favour form over fashion, they stitched these robes with undeniable regal zeal. They were more elaborate than necessary, more stylish than required, feeding into the egos of their wearers.\r\n\r\nRasthin, it is said, was particularly fond of stitching elaborate filigree into her robes. Made from threads woven with precious metals, and gemstones, her robes would sparkle and glitter as she flew across the realm, and when she brought forward her flames, everyone marveled.\r\n\r\nGlittering against the sky, she inspired devotion as much as fear in her subjects.",
    "DragonkinDarkMageLegs": "The dragonkin of Ashenfall all reveled in power, gaining elemental mastery from the strange anima of the land. Although their kind tended to ignore magic, in favour of alchemy and science, the dragonkin of Ashenfall enjoyed their time as sorcerer kings.\r\n\r\nThey were robes that empowered their magic, using alchemical techniques to create cloth that would resist their elemental powers. Fire would not burn so easily, cold would not damage the threads and lightning could be turned aside with a flick of a cloak. Though these techniques are lost to time, the echoes of their powerful resistance remains.\r\n\r\nLacrussa was the only dragonkin to resist the alure of magic. She insisted that science was the greater power, capable of almost anything with time and study. Lacrussa wore the robes as an a small act of rebellion, claiming that magic would always be overcome in the end.\r\n\r\nUnlike her companions, Lacrussa refused to decorate her robes. She took no joy in precious stones, or shining metals. She refused to embroider designs across the cloak. She did, however, use alchemical techniques to enhance the defensive power of the robes. Something the other dragonkin soon came to copy, proving, in her mind, the superiority of science.",
    "DragonkinDarkMageHood": "The dragonkin of Ashenfall all reveled in power, gaining elemental mastery from the strange anima of the land. Although their kind tended to ignore magic, in favour of alchemy and science, the dragonkin of Ashenfall enjoyed their time as sorcerer kings.\r\n\r\nThey were robes that empowered their magic, using alchemical techniques to create cloth that would resist their elemental powers. Fire would not burn so easily, cold would not damage the threads and lightning could be turned aside with a flick of a cloak. Though these techniques are lost to time, the echoes of their powerful resistance remains.\r\n\r\nThe hood was designed for two purposes. The shadow it cast upon the dragonkin features, made their visage scarier and intimidation easier. Goblins, humans and garou would cower from the reptilian grins that was all they could see in the darkness. The second was to hide their frustration at the foolishness of others, their fellow dragonkin included. Rolling eyes is much easier when no one else can see.",
    "InternalAlchemy": "The druid Gastritix often struggled with Bramblemead dishes. Something about the rustic cooking troubled her stomach and she would find herself in constant distress. Even bread, simple as it was, would leave her doubled over in agony and there were many times where she regretted that day's meal. So it was that she created a spell, which would speed up her digestion to near instant speed, granting her the energy needed to survive, but somehow preventing her normal stomach issues. \r\n\r\nThereafter, with this spell in her repertoire, she became a noted local chef, finally able to enjoy a good meal. ",
    "InspiringRingOfMining.ProspectorsRing": "The dwarves know that the earth has a memory, gemstones especially so. What they don’t tell you, is that the earth holds a grudge. Each time a pick pierces the stone or smashes a piece of ore from its home, the earth remembers.\r\n\r\nA skilled enchanter can use that grudge, that earthen memory, and turn it back against the grudge-filled earth. Traditionally this is done via jewellery, with dwarven goldsmiths making most of their living from miners purchasing these trinkets.",
    "ImarusTalon": "The essence of an undead dragon, distilled down into a blade. What could go wrong?\r\n\r\nThis weapon is cold to the touch in ways far beyond chilling. Holding it feels as if you’re out in the cold, with no shelter to protect you. Exposure in the palm of your hand.\r\n\r\nWorse than the cold, is the hunger. This scimitar wants to cut, to stab, to slice and to maim. It wants to see your enemies bleed so that it can feast on their suffering. Imaru may be dead, but her hunger is eternal and you feel it infecting you with each swing of the blade.\r\n\r\nStill, the power of this blade makes it almost worth it. What’s a little hunger when faced with the destruction of your foes? Lament later, surely, and revel now in the carnage. Perhaps there’s a little dragon in you too?",
    "BonesToPeaches": "The essence of magic is change, this the druids believed. Elemental magics were assumed to balance one element with another. Mending spells transformed the present into past. When one druid found himself trapped in a wolf's den, he was forced to rely on this concept.\r\n\r\nThe wolf had him cornered, trapped behind a crack in the wall, too small for the wolf to attack him, but leaving him no room for escape. Around the druid, were the bones of the wolf's victims, which had been hurled through the crack like some burial ritual. Starving and thirsty, the druid worked out a spell, using the runes in his pocket to turn the bones into the sweet, sticky fruit that would keep him alive. Fed, on this strange discovery, the druid outlasted the wolf's hunger for his flesh and when the wolf went to seek his own food, the druid took his chance to escape. \r\n\r\nEating the transmuted bones of others is considered a little odd in civilised society, yet when one is in the wilderness, one takes what they can get.  ",
    "AdventurersLongbow": "The expedition to Ashenfall included skilled adventurers. If fate had been kinder, they could have gone down in legend as mighty heroes, rather than a slow, quiet, death where their names might be forgotten.\r\n\r\nThis bow was a design brought to Ashenfall by the hunter, Finn, a simple longbow crafted from easily obtained materials. Finn was a scout, and a ranger, who enjoyed getting into all the nooks and crannies when he would wander White Wolf mountain in search of treasure. He joined the expedition in hope of discovering new wonders, and new stories to share with the people back home.\r\n\r\nInstead he found a dragon, and the end to his story.",
    "CompostBucket": "The first bucket on Gielinor was created by Mrs Hyacinth, to help her around the farm. They had buckets back on the human homeworld of Teragard, of course, but she hadn’t brought anything with her from the village Guthix had recruited her from.\r\n\r\nThe problem was that Mrs Hyacinth had a cow, named the traditional Bessie, that produced an unreasonable amount of manure, and Mrs Hyacinth needed something to help with the clear up.\r\n\r\nShe used local twigs, at first, weaving together more of a basket than a bucket, and the results were not as watertight as she had hoped. In the end, she meticulously combined strips of wood, tied together with cord, and they were able to withstand even the worst of Bessie’s endeavours.\r\n\r\nNow, you too can fill one of these marvelous contraptions with horrifically pungent compost. Spread the smelly stuff across your farming plots and marvel at the health and happiness of your leafy little friends. ",
    "EnchantCampfire": "The first campfires changed the path of human destiny. Shivering cave dwellers lasted longer against the winter, and in time they learned to use this flame to dominate the land. Ever since, campfires have warmed a primal part of our spirit, and many yearn for the simple pleasure of sitting around one with their companions, on a summer night. So it comes as no surprise that the first druids found a way to make such fires even more appealing. \r\n\r\nThe runes drew out some essential nature of the campfire, letting it burn hotter, brighter and better than before. Food cooks faster, cold quickly burns away and even the campfire ghost stories feel better against a backdrop of this flame. ",
    "IronArrowBrutal": "The first of these weapons was not commissioned for war. Nor was it commissioned for hunting. Instead, this most deadly arrow was created for nothing more than vengeance.\r\n\r\nAgainst a pigeon.\r\n\r\nLord Gawurin was the ruler of Brynmoor, as appointed by the priests of Saradomin. He wore expensive garments of the finest materials, adorned with glittering jewels and gold chain. So, he was most affronted when a single pigeon determined that such a garment was the perfect restroom choice.\r\n\r\nThe pigeon's audacity was unforgivable. Day after day, Lord Gawurin's garments were defiled by this feathered fiend. His anger grew, fueling a desire for revenge that consumed him. He commissioned an arrow, a weapon of brutal design, with a single purpose- to make the pigeon suffer.\r\n\r\nWith a sinister smile, he summoned the most skilled hunter, a woman who had brought down countless foes, and ordered her to end the pigeon's reign of terror.\r\n\r\nShe stood at the top of the castle, her bow steady, her aim true. The tension in the air was palpable as she prepared to unleash the arrow of vengeance upon the pigeon. The world seemed to hold its breath in anticipation.\r\n\r\nShe missed. ",
    "BarbedIronArrow": "The first of these weapons was not commissioned for war. Nor was it commissioned for hunting. Instead, this most deadly arrow was created for nothing more than vengeance.\r\n\r\nAgainst a pigeon.\r\n\r\nLord Gawurin was the ruler of Brynmoor, as appointed by the priests of Saradomin. He wore expensive garments of the finest materials, adorned with glittering jewels and gold chain. So, he was most affronted when a single pigeon determined that such a garment was the perfect restroom choice.\r\n\r\nThe pigeon's audacity was unforgivable. Day after day, Lord Gawurin's garments were defiled by this feathered fiend. His anger grew, fuelling a desire for revenge that consumed him. He commissioned an arrow, a weapon of brutal design, with a single purpose- to make the pigeon suffer.\r\n\r\nWith a sinister smile, he summoned the most skilled hunter, a woman who had brought down countless foes, and ordered her to end the pigeon's reign of terror.\r\n\r\nShe stood at the top of the castle, her bow steady, her aim true. The tension in the air was palpable as she prepared to unleash the arrow of vengeance upon the pigeon. The world seemed to hold its breath in anticipation.\r\n\r\nShe missed. ",
    "WoolThread": "The fluffy coats from the fluffiest creatures, refined and wound down into a single thread. Useful for crafting various garments and highly prized by grandmothers everywhere.\r\n\r\nOn the mainland, the most impressive collection of wool belongs to Great-Aunt Potterington, in Kandarin. Her sister, Granny Potterington, is constantly trying to claim this title for herself and has accelerated her sheep breeding program in an attempt to mass produce as much wool as possible.",
    "Zamorak": "The god of chaos, Zamorak, is often associated with evil. In truth, it is more complicated than this, as Zamorak promotes freedom and self-improvement, rather than relying on tradition. Needless to say, he and Saradomin have been in conflict across the ages.\r\n\r\nZamorak is one of the Mahjarrat, the mysterious creatures from the world of Freneskae. He was recruited to serve under the Empty Lord, Zaros, but he found Zaros’ methods too hands-off for his tastes. Using two of the Elder Artefacts, Zamorak betrayed Zaros and drained him of enough of his power to allow Zamorak to ascend. Zaros, presumed defeated, disappeared into the void where he might rebuild his strength.\r\n\r\nNews of his usurpation of Zaros is what began the God Wars, the greatest and bloodiest battle in Gielinor’s history. An alliance of Saradomin, Bandos and Armadyl, pushed Zamorak to the brink and caused him to destroy the entire region of Forinthry in a last desperate ploy. This act awoke Guthix from his slumber, and caused him to enact the Edicts that would banish the gods.\r\n\r\nZamorak has tried repeatedly to reclaim Gielinor, using the legions of demons at his beck and call. He has been pushed back by alliances of heroes, yet is always scheming to return and finish what he started.",
    "CheekyFryUp": "The grease. The sizzling scents. The feeling of your arteries withering just from a single glance at the dish. Is there anything in life as close to the divine as a nice, cheeky fry up?\r\n\r\nPopular among those who have consumed a few too many gnomish cocktails the night before, a cheeky fry up is hailed as a great cure for the pounding headache that follows such indulgences.",
    "GraniteMaulHandle": "The handle for a powerful weapon. Wrapped in sturdy leather and imbued with ritual magic. A dwarf capable of crafting such a piece is acknowledged to have completed one of the trials required to be a master smith.  ",
    "WitheredHeart": "The heart is a pump, nothing more, designed to push precious blood around the circulatory system, so that our weak and feeble frames can function. Yet, metaphysically, it is the most important organ, for it houses our emotional centre, the very essence of what makes us human.\r\n\r\nA withered heart isn’t just a heart that has languished. Nor is it just a heart that is drained of blood. No, a withered heart has had all the humanity leeched from it, emptied of emotion, or connection, of self.\r\n\r\nThis process is imperfect, of course, and tiny fragments of soul-matter remains, that can be refined and extracted from the heart. Withdrawing the last remnants of what made the owner “them”.",
    "BramblemeadCape": "The heart of Bramblemead village was never really the castle, which came much later to the party. It wasn’t the town hall. It wasn’t the druid huts. It was the windmill. Grain heaped by the village was brought here and milled down into flour. The flour became bread, pastry and batter, turning village meals into something that helped people to live, rather than simply survive.\r\n\r\nFood, becomes a banner around which everyone can gather. To break bread with one’s enemy was a way to settle old scores, and forge new connections. More good has been done over the dinner table, than on any throne. Flour, turned Bramblemead from a settlement, into a thriving village.\r\n\r\nNow, the windmill lies dormant and still. The foundations warped by the centuries, despite the magic holding the rot at bay in the ancient timber, and the stone cracking from time. When humanity fell, it was rats and goblins that claimed the heart of the village, though they had no use for the swirling sails.\r\n\r\nTo the goblins it was a place where food could be hidden. Supplies sealed up from rival tribes. For the rats, it was a restaurant full of goblin supplies that were never well sealed.\r\n\r\nThe sail no longer turns. But, perhaps, in the fullness of time, this village heart might beat again.",
    "DriedCadavaberries": "The heat makes them edible, but only just. A once popular snack among the people of Fellhollow, who seemed overly cavalier about the consumption of deadly poisons. ",
    "FallenHopliteApsis": "The hoplites of Emberwood named themselves after Saradomin’s forces in the holy scripture. The legendary hoplites were warriors on the world of Hallow, who were trained from farmers and artisans, rather than military families or nobility, and the Emberwood hoplites came from similar stock.\r\n\r\nThey were best known for their fighting style using spears and shields, with the shield being the defining feature. These shields were designed to be worn for longer periods of time, resting against the shoulder for more comfortable carrying.\r\n\r\nThe hoplites may have been raised from peasant stock, but their camaraderie made them a force to be reckoned with on the battlefield.\r\n\r\nAs the withering took them, the hoplites retained the rote memory of their training and remained a deadly threat to all who faced them.",
    "IronGreatsword": "The iron greatsword is a devastating weapon favoured by the most powerful warriors. The strongest garou warriors wielded them in battle against goblin invaders, turning armies of green-skinned soldiers into nothing but viscera.\r\n\r\nWhen the dragons turned on their allies, these blades would be turned against armoured scales. Several dragons underestimated the power of these weapons in the hands of a skilled wielder. Garou thanes tore young dragons to pieces, wearing their bones as symbols of their success.\r\n\r\nIt made them an excellent target for the older dragons. Velgar, a mighty general, marked those wearing dragon bone adornments for death. He crushed them first, brutally and swiftly. Their strength shattered beneath his claws or swallowed in an instant.\r\n\r\nThe practice quickly fell out of favour. ",
    "RedberryInfusion": "The juice of the berries infuses the water with a pleasant flavour. It’s easier and more refreshing to drink, offering greater hydrating properties than normal water.",
    "PaladinBody": "The knights of Saradomin had many names over the years. White Knights. Temple Knights. In Ashenfall they were called Paladins. Brave, and faithful, members of noble families that took up the sword.\r\n\r\nThey were the second, third and fourth children of Brynmoor’s “nobility” – a title given to those families that were most loyal to the priesthood, and the most obedient were raised the highest.\r\n\r\nThe chest piece was especially well made. Blacksmiths would take extra special care to make sure that the process went smoothly, as nothing but perfection would withstand inspection. It made forgery practically impossible, or at the very least, far too expensive to work.\r\n\r\nIt is said that the paladins were capable of working miracles in battle. Rumour held that the power of Saradomin flowed through them, and helped them to smite all who stood against the church.",
    "PaladinLegs": "The knights of Saradomin had many names over the years. White Knights. Temple Knights. In Ashenfall they were called Paladins. Brave, and faithful, members of noble families that took up the sword.\r\n\r\nThey were the second, third and fourth children of Brynmoor’s “nobility” – a title given to those families that were most loyal to the priesthood, and the most obedient were raised the highest.\r\n\r\nThese platelegs were exceptionally well made. Sturdier and stronger than the armour worn by Brynmoor guards, and other members of Ashenfall’s human soldiers. It was certainly stronger than the helms of the Guthixian aligned knights, or the druids they guarded.\r\n\r\nPaladins were a righteous force, noble and true to the priest’s teachings. They brought order from chaos, and discipline to sloth. Remaking Bramblemead according to the church’s designs.",
    "PaladinHelm": "The knights of Saradomin had many names over the years. White Knights. Temple Knights. In Ashenfall they were called Paladins. Brave, and faithful, members of noble families that took up the sword.\r\n\r\nThey were the second, third and fourth children of Brynmoor’s “nobility” – a title given to those families that were most loyal to the priesthood, and the most obedient were raised the highest.\r\n\r\nThis helm was a symbol of pride to the respectable families of Brynmoor. A sign that the bearer had been chosen to stand as the guardian of Saradominist faith for all of Brynmoor to see. It earned them favour, it earned the status, it earned them the ire of those who followed a different faith.",
    "UndeadRangerHood": "The last hunter in Fellhollow hid in the trees for as long as he could. Beneath him the dead shambled and stumbled and clawed at the bark, yet he remained in the trees safely out of reach.\r\n\r\nHe fed himself on nuts and berries, stealing form the treasure troves of squirrels and birds, occasionally risking a trip to the ground to hunt a passing animal. He gave up on cooking the meat he hunted, as he could not stay on the ground long enough before the dead rose up to face him, so he tore into the carcasses of deer, sheep and rats, trying his best not to vomit.\r\n\r\nHe barely noticed when his appetite changed, since he was so used raw flesh and the sickly cold taste of blood pooling in his mouth. So, when the withering began to take him, he hardly noticed.\r\n\r\nSometimes, if you’re quick enough, you may catch a glimpse of a skeleton in the tree branches. Even in death he fears the monsters on the ground.",
    "LawRune": "The law rune is an odd sort of power, it embodies not some elemental force, but instead it represents the rigidity of reality. The rules in which all of creation operates, are conceptually encapsulated within this small stone. Gravity, movement, location, all these things are bound within, and when the rune is used in magic, these rules may be altered for a time. Teleportation relies heavily on these runes, overwriting the concept of distance for the casting. ",
    "WeakCoolingPotion": "The liquid in this potion is always cold to the touch. It tastes like an iced glass of lemonade on a hot summer's day, or a chilled glass of water when you've been working out in the sun. Drinking this potion makes the body cooler than before, shielding one against fire's fury. ",
    "OakLongbow": "The longbow is every hunter’s friend. Capable of taking down prey over a greater range than the shortbow, it has been the end of many a deer trying to flee through the woods. \r\n\r\nBramblemead would host a thrilling archery tournament. The villagers would proudly bring their own longbows, meticulously crafted by hand, and compete to take down cleverly hidden targets scattered across the village. It was a spectacle of skill and precision, with the only rule being that the target must be shootable from the open street. \r\n\r\nWith the introduction of magic, the competition took on a whole new level of silliness. Participants conjured magical air currents that sent arrows astray – often into unsuspecting washing lines or pies cooling on windowsills – or they would playfully blast arrows out of the air with fire spells. \r\n\r\nHowever, this stopped after the Amalgamated archer, Derien, joined the game. Gifted with the element of air, his arrows curved gracefully with each shot, missing traps, tricks and deceptions. His victory earned the Amalgamated more jealousy, which led to the competition ending out of envious spite.",
    "AnimaInfusedBark": "The magic of Ashenfall permeates the very trees of the region, imbuing their bark with mystical properties. This bark can be refined into wild anima, a magical crafting component that is popular with spellcasters of all kinds.",
    "BronzeCrowssbow": "The members of the Wild Knights who trained with melee weapons, generally found themselves the victors in friendly sparring matches against their companions. The reasoning was simple – shields. A shield could turn aside blade, arrow and spell, with equal skill and it left their enemy vulnerable to counterattack. This sense of superiority began to change from friendly competition, into a sense of smugness capable of souring many friendships.\r\n\r\nRoliker Hallister was particularly obnoxious. His skill with sword and shield was admittedly impressive. At the age of twenty he could best even some of the commanders of the Wild Knights in single combat. Healthy pride twisted into smug egoism, which then rotted down into unpleasant superiority. He was rude. He was dismissive. He was pretty much an obnoxious nightmare to be around.\r\n\r\nWhich is why he was very surprised by the bolt in his shoulder, and why no one else seemed too inclined to help him out. Vanida Mox was a skilled archer and inventor. She was quick to learn, quick to study and she had the enviable ability to see when something was wrong and work to find a solution. Popular with the other knights, because she would work with them, share accolades, and stand for what the order truly meant – a barrier between the world and the wild.\r\n\r\nAs Roliker’s ego grew and grew, she realised that the archers in the order were at a significant disadvantage in close combat. At range, they were obviously superior, but as all the sparring matches were held in the courtyard, there was precious little distance available. So she began working on a device that would deliver the power of an arrow but allowing one hand free to hold a shield.\r\n\r\nIt was very effective.\r\n\r\nShe named it a “cross-bow”, because she was very very angry when she unloaded it into the cloud of smugness known as Roliker. Later that name would be attributes to the mechanical structure of the device, but now you know better.",
    "Jade": "The more green the colour of jade, the higher the concentration of iron in its makeup. Popular among many cultures for both its beauty and it’s use as a crafting material.\r\n\r\nIn the eastern lands of Wushanko, jade is highly prized. Some islands have elaborate rituals about its usage. There are rumours of rare jades with magical properties, each a different colour of the rainbow.",
    "FortifyingCrunchies": "The mushrooms of Ashenfall have absorbed enough magic over the years to give them special properties. A well-cooked mushroom will absorb certain toxins from whoever ingests them, making them invaluable for scouting into poison-filled areas.",
    "VannakaDefault": "The mysterious warrior, Vannaka, is regarded as one of the premier slayer masters in the world. He trained under the legendary Duradel, and the two even duelled one another once. Duradel won. Something Vannaka has never entirely managed to get over. \r\n\r\nVannaka’s origins are mysterious. He claims no nation as his homeland, nor does he speak of family or friends. Conversations lead only to talk of combat and the slaying of dangerous creatures. Yet there is something about Ashenfall that creates a silence in him. He stares out across the continent as if searching for something. ",
    "SalveAmulet": "The name is a bit of a misnomer, since this amulet has very little to do with the river Salve. Instead its name refers to the crystal that traditionally sits at the heart. This crystal was first brought to the region by Saradominist priests, some time in the third age. They had harvested it from a cave near the river Salve and found ways to cultivate on Ashenfall’s soil, by utilising the natural magic infusing everything.\r\n\r\nSanctified by faith and magic, this amulet was deadly to the undead, and the dragonkin Lacrussa was fascinated with the properties. She stole the recipe for its creation and hid it within her vault, long before the withering curse spread over Fellhollow. Instead she had delved too deeply in her own experiments and discovered something buried within the earth that would twist and reanimate the dead.\r\n\r\nThis amulet allowed her to hold the forces back and keep them from overrunning her lab. That was, until, she met her end at Kuldra’s fangs.",
    "SteelMace": "The priests of Emberwood village were not as militant as their fellows back in Brynmoor. They saw little need for weapons, when a dragon watched over their flock. Still, they carried steel maces by their side as a symbol of office, each one ornately engraved with the symbol of their chosen deity.\r\n\r\nAlthough the Saradominist priests had first insisted that different faiths could only turn on one another, they found an odd sort of kinship in their fellows and would regularly enjoy debates of the merits and flaws of each other’s faiths.",
    "AbyssalAshes": "The remnants of an abyssal demon. Fine ashes, infused with malice. Not great for the skin, despite what Doric will tell you.",
    "Feathers": "The simple feather is the greatest invention of the avian species. The aviansie race is particularly proud of their plumage and hold lengthy conversations about the best maintenance methods. For the average bird, such as magpies and similar, feathers grant them the ability to soar through the skies, defying the whims of gravity.\r\n\r\nThey are a vital part of crafting arrows, as the feathers are used for the flights that cause the arrow to fly true.",
    "BakedPotato": "The simplicity. The elegance. Few things in life are quite as dependable as a good old baked potato. Crispy exterior, fluffy interior, and it goes well with just about anything.",
    "EvasiveInfusion": "The smaller creatures can be annoyingly good at getting out of harms way. When boiling water infused with a small fang, belonging to one of these critters, this evasive essence can be pulled into the clean water, allowing the drinker to benefit from it.",
    "DubiousMeatPie": "The smell from this pie is strange. Undertones of gamey meats, the tantalising touch of subtle herbs and spices. The pastry is crip, inviting…and yet, beneath the crust, hidden behind the pleasantries, is the scent of something otherworldly.\r\n\r\nDeath has touched this pie.",
    "AbyssalSpine": "The spinal column of an abyssal demon. A monstrous creature from between the gaps in reality. It pulses with hatred, and you’re convinced it wriggles occasionally.",
    "StoneArrow": "The stone arrow's weight and durability make it a more popular choice among hunters. The hardness of the tip makes it better at piercing tough animal hides or less durable forms of armour. In the second age, druids considered it a form of cheating, citing that it required a human's ingenuity to create rather than being a gift of nature. \r\n\r\nThey seldom complained when the evening meal was served, celebrating the success of the hunt alongside everyone else. \r\n\r\nEven with somewhere as remote as Ashenfall, some humans turned to banditry, and they were very fond of stone arrows. Cheap and easy to produce, they help to kill and threaten anyone who makes the mistake of travelling through bandit-controlled woodland. \r\n\r\nOne such bandit, the self-described \"bandit king, Otho\", would choose only \"interesting\" stones to turn into arrows. Glittering pebbles that swished through the air. He was nicknamed the \"Magpie King\" because of his love for shiny pebbles. A moniker he was very unhappy with.",
    "BlightwoodStaff": "The stranger the wood, the more powerful the magic. It’s unsure why this is, but it’s been true since magic was first discovered. When used as a tool for destructive magic, Garou druids learned the power of blightwood. \r\n\r\nAshien, a legendary druid among the garou, was among the first to wield such a weapon. As the story goes, they were wandering the swamps when the goddess Lougrim came to her in a vision and gave her a quest. Ashien was to journey deep into the swamps and do battle with a demon that dwelled within. \r\n\r\nThey found the demon; it emerged out of the fog with a chilling screech and destroyed their staff with a single swing of its blade-like claw. In panic, Ashien blindly reached for the nearest weapon, the root of a swamp tree that had fallen in the mud. Through it, they channeled their will to survive, and it burst free of the branch as a flash of lightning. \r\n\r\nThe creature burned beneath Ashien’s gaze, vanishing back into the mists as if it never were. \r\n\r\nSince then, garou druids would favour blightwood when crafting a staff. ",
    "DireWolfLeather": "The tanned hide of a powerful dire wolf. It's sturdy, thick, ready for enchantment and more resilient than normal leather. \r\n\r\nA legend speaks of a warrior that wore the tanned hide of a dire wolf. He wore it proudly as he walked the streets and used it to strike fear into the hearts of bandits and criminals alike. After all, a warrior that could best such a beast, was no one to be trifled with. \r\n\r\nThe story goes that one day he met another warrior, proud, skillful and handsome, and the two of them became the best of friends. They would fight together, spar together and rescued helpless villagers as an unstoppable pair. Until the night of the full moon, where the truth was revealed. Where once his new friend had stood, there was now a dread wolf of midnight-black fur and eyes of burning coal. They fought again, but gone was the jovial sparring of friends. Now they fought as man and beast, tooth and claw versus blade and shield, until all that was left of the two of them was this story. ",
    "CliffWoods": "The temple of Saradomin oversees all of Brynmoor, the stained glass windows once glistened in the sunlight as a beacon to the villagers of Bramblemead, below.\r\n\r\nIn the Third Age, when the rest of the world was at war, followers of Saradomin arrived on the continent and created a small settlement at the peak of the cliff. Here, the expedition could gaze out over the water, looking back toward the homes they had left, and gazing down towards Bramblemead, the home they would create.\r\n\r\nOvertime the expedition mixed with the villagers, leaving their tents and their huts, and finding homes in the village. As the camps were brought down, stones were placed as the foundation of the temple, as the word of Saradomin spread throughout the streets.\r\n\r\nNow, millennia later, the temple stands proud, but silent. The walls and windows remain steadfast against the onslaught of time. A faint trace of priestly magic keeps the more monstrous elements from creeping inside. It is a sanctuary against the wild frontiers of Ashenfall, and therefore the home base of the current expedition team.\r\n\r\nIt is said that the windmill, northwest of the temple, is haunted. The timber creeks ominously even though the sails no longer turn. At night, sinister creatures patrol its exterior, waiting for any opportunity to strike.",
    "RingOfTheWoodcutter": "The trees of Ashenfall are infused with magic, which makes them a little more cunning than a normal tree. While lacking sapience, nor sentience, these trees possess on characteristic in abundance – they possess spite.\r\n\r\nA tree’s spite resonates back across the axe that cuts it, leaving splinters in the handle and cracks in the blade. Over time this shatters the tool, forcing repairs, which can infuriate lumberjacks when they’re in the middle of a swing.\r\n\r\nThis ring makes the wearer’s anima feel more in tune with nature, reducing the spite of the tree’s retaliation. While unable to negate it completely, it makes axes last longer and logs feel lighter to carry.",
    "Dragonwolf": "The unholy fusion of dragon and beast, creations of twisted dragonkin science. These creatures seem somehow resistant to the cursed nature of the region, but they refuse to sit still long enough for anyone to ask them why.\r\n\r\nNot that they would answer, they have a wolf’s mind, not a dragons.",
    "OakShortbow": "The villagers who proved their skills with an ash shortbow were brought into the forest by a Wild Knight instructor in search of more durable wood. The oak stood proud and tall in the forest, standing firm against the ebb of time. Villagers would gather the sturdiest branches that they could and be brought to a quiet place in the wood to begin carving their new bows. \r\n\r\nThe Wild Knight would tell them that a good bow is more than just a weapon, but an extension of oneself. They encouraged each villager to put a piece of themselves into the bow, a decoration on the handle, or a poem carved into the wood. Whatever made the bow personal to them. \r\n\r\nThis connection in the crafting process would help the villagers feel comfortable with their new weapon, and many young hunters would use the same bow throughout their lives. It even became a tradition for young hunters to gift a bow to their beloved as a marriage proposal. A tradition that survived into the third age.",
    "NecroticBonemeal": "The walking dead are strange things, animated by hunger, need, rage and a touch of magic. In Ashenfall it is the hunger of Imaru that drives them, as her great maw draws in the souls of both the living and the dead, the afflicted try to cling to life with everything that they have.\r\n\r\nThey fail, but the drive remains. Fear, desperation, and simple need keeps the body moving, if not alive. This changes the body, that hunger and rot seeping into the very bones and changing them to be something other than they were.\r\n\r\nWhen ground down, these bones become a powder containing hints of that animating magic. A necromantic resonance with a wide range of uses.",
    "LargeAnimalHorn": "The weapons of the animal kingdom can make for useful crafting components. Unfortunately, you generally have to battle against said weapons in order to use them. Many a hunter has underestimated how damaging an animal horn can be.\r\n\r\nIt is not a mistake they repeat.",
    "BirdMeat": "The white meat of a bird is surprisingly versatile. While delicious on its own, it can be combined with various dishes, seasoned or marinated to create surprisingly varied flavours. The meat is often softer to consume, making it an easier meal for the on-the-go hunter.",
    "BlightwoodLongbow": "The wild creatures of Ashenfall have always been elusive. Bramblemead villagers would tell wild stories about the deer that got away or the sheep that shrugged off an arrow. Still, those who could wield a bow rarely left their family hungry. \r\n\r\nOne Wild Knight, Frode, was so skilled with the bow that he never returned from a hunt empty-handed. His siblings among the Wild Knights would send him out hunting before every great gathering so that they might feast heartily upon his return. For years, this was their happiest tradition. \r\n\r\nUntil the day he did not return. \r\n\r\nIt is said that his body was found in five separate places, spread out across the forests of Brynmoor like some gruesome compass. His quiver was empty, yet none of his arrows – marked as they were, with his symbol, an owl – would ever be found. ",
    "MagicFocus": "The wizard Mallory was never as good as he had hoped to be. He was proficient in spellcasting and his rote memory was excellent, but the power of his spells always fell a little short. He advanced through the ranks mostly through technicality, but he never quite earned the respect of his peers. \r\n\r\nThat was until the day he created the focus. Mallory had always been good at making things. He enjoyed the simpler magic of crafting, and he found you could weave small threads of magic into the construction of something, breathing just a little bit more life and wonder into it than before. True craftsmanship, he believed, carried a little bit of the creator’s soul in it. \r\n\r\nThe focus proved this theory and elevated it further. By creating a focus and subconsciously imbuing it with a bit of his essence, it would create a smoother, more fluid, channel of magic through the body, which created spells of greater potency. \r\n\r\nTragically, after sharing his knowledge with the other wizards, he was murdered by his mentor. Who then took his knowledge and claimed it as his own. Karma is a stern judge, however, and the offending wizard later found himself being swallowed whole and digested by a dragon. \r\n\r\nJust punishment, I’m sure Mallory would agree.  ",
    "Guthix": "There are few gods with a heart so heavy as that of Guthix. Born to the world of Naragun, which was destroyed by warring gods, he achieved ascension in an act of grief-ridden vengeance and has regretted that decision ever since.\r\n\r\nHe was the first god to discover Gielinor and the powerful anima flow within it, and he used this power to grow stronger. Yet, it was when he discovered the Stone of Jas, a powerful artefact and tool of the Elder Gods, that Guthix would become the legendary figure that he is.\r\n\r\nThe god of balance slept through the God Wars, only waking to be reminded of the danger that gods represent. He created a powerful barrier to banish the gods, keeping them from returning to this world.\r\n\r\nFor a time Gielinor was safe and Guthix slept beneath the ground, drawing power from the anima.\r\n\r\nAncient and full of a deep grief, Guthix had come to believe that a world could only be safe if there were no gods left to rule it, and he laid out the path for his own end. He was slain by the Mahjarrat Sliske, which brought an end to the Fifth Age and ushered in the return of the gods.\r\n\r\nFor a time the world fell into chaos, until powerful heroes forged an alliance and created Edicts of their own, banishing the gods and leaving Gielinor, once more, in the hands of mortals.",
    "SteelShield": "There are few things as good at shielding you from harm, as a great slab of metal between you and your foe. The steel shield is more durable than its iron kin, capable of resisting greater and more deadly blows.\r\n\r\nIt was popular among Fellhollow locals, who would play a game that indicated an intelligence shortage among the population. The game consisted of one player being equipped with a crossbow and other players would hide around the area carrying steel shields. The crossbow wielder would then hunt the other players and try and fire a bolt into their shields, marking them as out of the game.\r\n\r\nUnsurprisingly the injuries caused by this game were large and common place. Even the death of some of the players wouldn’t cause the activity to be banned. “It’s tradition”, “it’s just good fun”, “hardly anyone gets hurt”, these were the arguments made even as parents grieved the loss of their child from the game.",
    "Ectoplasm": "There are many theories on what ectoplasm is, each as disturbing as the last. Some say that it’s emotion, condensed over years of existence. Others think that it’s some sort of infection of the soul, as only the undead seem to produce it.\r\n\r\nIn truth, ectoplasm is none of these. It’s the outer shell of a soul, the skin more or less, and acts as a sort of lubrication between the flesh and the spirit. Tiny bits of reality combine with the immaterial otherness of the underworld and congeal into a sort of spiritual mixture that ghosts and other undead find incredible useful.\r\n\r\nNecromancers prize the stuff, finding a myriad of uses for it. Most people find it weird and decidedly “icky”.",
    "AxtralProjection": "There is nothing trees fear more than this spell. Crafted by their ancient enemy, the lumberjack, this spell has wiped out whole legions of arboreal sentinels. Guthix taught this spell to the people of Brynmoor, hoping that it would help them expand their settlement. It worked perhaps too well, and soon entire forests would find themselves reduced to stumps. Guthix was forced to implement restrictions to the spell, preventing any but the most senior druids from using it. \r\n\r\nSince his death this restriction has been lifted, and the trees tremble in the terror that is to come. ",
    "Blightwood": "There is something about the swamps of Ashenfall that encourage strange trees to grow. The result is blightwood, durable, powerful and somehow capable of channeling more potent magic than other trees.\r\n\r\nThe wild knights joked that blightwood houses the souls of the victims of the swamp, and that they are imbued with their regrets and hatred.\r\n\r\nIt’s probably not true. Probably.",
    "UndeadRangersBow": "There was a villager in Emberwood called Holtz, who was a master with the bow. He could shoot rats from across a field and never scored less than a bullseye whenever he aimed for an archery target. Yet he had no desire for warfare, and no matter the words of encouragement from the captain, he would never join the guard.\r\n\r\nFor him, archery was a passion, an art, never to be used against another person. A stance quickly forgotten by his shambling corpse.",
    "WildScoutsShortbow": "There was magic once, in this bow, though it has mostly faded now. Runes were ground down and mixed with plant oils, to create a special resin that allowed the Wild Knights to work their magic through the bow, as if they were carrying runes. That magic has faded away, and the recipe for the mixture lost to the ages, but these bows are still formidable in their own right.\r\n\r\nWild Knights told tales of creatures that lived within the wilds of Ashenfall, that could not be felled by normal bows. Deer with hides that glistened with starlight, sheep who’s silver fleeces repelled all arrows. They claimed that these magically infused bows were the only ones capable of harming the creatures, though none ever returned with trophies to share.",
    "IronArrowBroadhead": "There was once a battle between two archers, the human soldier, Eileena, and a garou archer called Krayth. The two were pitted in battle together across the King’s Pass. They camouflaged themselves and hid on opposite sides of the pass, picking off each other’s soldiers as they tried to pass.\r\n\r\nAs the battle raged on, the tension between Eileena and Krayth grew. Their stealth skills were unmatched, making it impossible for either to spot the other. Both were disciplined and ruthless, their focus solely on the kill. They were a perfect match, each move calculated, each shot deadly.\r\n\r\nFor months, their battle continued, neither side gaining a foothold over the other as these two archers stayed hidden. Until a single arrow changed the stalemate. It was an arrow from neither archer, a single broad-headed bolt that struck a garou child. Eileen refused to take another shot; children had never been acceptable targets under her watch.  \r\n\r\nThe pass fell to garou control shortly after. ",
    "SteelCrossbow": "There were no garou in Fellhollow for the people to deal with, so in the early days of the settlement people created steel crossbows primarily for hunting game. The dragons that guarded the people were gentle creatures, but possessed of an enormity so great that they required a great deal of food to keep going. Hunting, therefore, became a very popular activity, with many competitions and events crafted to provide meals for the draconic caretakers. \r\n\r\nWhen the withering began to spread and the dead began to rise, steel bolts proved just as effective at piercing a zombie’s skull, as it would catching the quarry from a hunt. \r\n\r\nThough the dead would come in droves, the crossbow would let them be taken out at great distance, keeping the last remaining settlements safter for just a few days longer. Every moment counted in those final days. ",
    "SkeletalMarauder": "There were once warriors in Fellhollow that wielded great weapons with tremendous force. They were boastful, proud and dangerous people – yet they were beloved.\r\n\r\nA tradition among these great hulking figures, was to seek out a far smaller person and act as their shield. They became the guardian angels of the bullied and the protectors of the abused. Wielding their great blades to fight against cruelty or unkindness, making the world happier by force.\r\n\r\nNow that instinct is gone.\r\n\r\nThese skeletal creatures remember only that a sword must be swung, and that a blade needs to cut into flesh. They remember only that bone must be cleaved and that the living must be turned into the dead, swelling their armies with each swing of the blade.",
    "StatusEffects": "There’s more than just bruising that awaits the unprepared adventurer, Ashenfall’s also home to a number of different conditions we in the biz like to refer to as status effects.\r\n\r\nThese effects, once inflicted, cause a continuing problem for a while. You could spend too much time in the Bloodblight Swamp and become poisoned, slowly losing health as time drags on.\r\n\r\nCertain creatures and weapons may inflict you with nasty cuts that cause bleeding, leading to a large amount of damage if not dealt with quickly.\r\n\r\nEach Status Effect is its own special little problem, and it’s a good idea to make sure you pay close attention to any that you run across.",
    "WizardRobeLegs": "These blue robes are the signature clothing worn by every respectable mage. Apprentices look forward to the day they first wear these, as it shows they have graduated to full wizard status. The wizard's tower, in particular, uses these robes as a uniform, signifying their magical power and fealty to the god of order, Saradomin.\r\n\r\nAll documents have been expunged of the events that came to be known as the Day of Purple Feathers. No wizard or knight will speak of what occurred. However, villagers soon learned that squawking like a chicken would send both knight and wizard into unexplained panic.\r\n\r\nFor some reason, the egg deliveries to the Wizard's guild were cancelled around the same time.",
    "WizardRobeBody": "These blue robes are the signature clothing worn by every respectable mage. Apprentices look forward to the day they first wear these, as it shows they have graduated to full wizard status. The wizard's tower, in particular, uses these robes as a uniform, signifying their magical power and fealty to the god of order, Saradomin.\r\n\r\nDespite the antagonistic intentions of the White Knights' arrival in the Wizard’s Guild, the two factions got along well. Apprentices would often prank low-ranked knights, using small spells to make them appear foolish. The knights would respond with more practical reprisals, which led to an escalation of shenanigans until the highest-ranking members of both orders got involved. Then things got really out of hand.",
    "WizardRobeHead": "These blue robes are the signature clothing worn by every respectable mage. Apprentices look forward to the day they first wear these, as it shows they have graduated to full wizard status. The wizard's tower, in particular, uses these robes as a uniform, signifying their magical power and fealty to the god of order, Saradomin.\r\n\r\nOn Ashenfall, it was rare for those wearing these robes to be seen outside the wizard's guild. The structure on the hillside was home to strange practices and even stranger experiments. Powerful spells were studied and unleashed across an already magically problematic countryside.\r\n\r\nThe White Knights, often summoned to address the perils that the wizard's guild inadvertently unleashed, were a frequent presence. Their duties ranged from quelling peculiar weather phenomena to managing the 'accidental' summoning of demons. To maintain control, a contingent of knights established a permanent residence within the guild, their vigilant eyes ever watchful.",
    "SalveCrystal": "These crystals only grow in a cave beneath the river Salve, back in Misthalin. They are imbued with the holy energies of that river, making them a powerful ward against the undead.\r\n\r\nDoric has been using one as a paperweight, ever since he found one on sightseeing holiday.\r\n\r\nIt’s probably blasphemous, but no one’s complained so far.",
    "BirdFillet": "These fillets go surprisingly well with most side dishes. The texture is pleasant, the flavour is subtle and not too overpowering. They go particularly well with garlic.",
    "Seeds.Pumpkin": "These gourds are most popular around the autumn, where they are hollowed out by local children and used to ward away spirits – poorly, as it seems they attract more than they repel.\r\n\r\nIndeed one strange fellow found himself so delighted by the strange gourds that he took an aspect of them into himself, feeling that they best embodied his favourite time of the year. So the strange individual known as “Pumpkin Pete”, part of the Pete family, came to be.",
    "GraniteDeposit": "These great lumps of rock are sturdier than normal stone and are excellent materials for construction. ",
    "Wheat": "These humble grains are the staple of many a burgeoning society. Breads, cakes, pastries and all manner of delicious foodstuffs can be forged from these marvellous stalks.",
    "GarouPack": "These leathery packs are carried around by garou warriors. They store rations, herbs and the spoils of battle inside. Crafted by elderly garou in the villages, or parents sending their child off to battle.",
    "RotriddenRat": "These nasty little creatures embody all the viciousness of their living kin, but with the added hatred of an undead dragon. Their bites will wither your soul, threatening to drag you into Imaru's gaze. ",
    "Seeds.Kwuarm": "These plants are greedily hoarded by goblins and garou, their poisonous capabilities highly desired by their scouts. With these seeds, you no longer need to slaughter goblins in droves to acquire these herbs, instead you are now able to grow them yourself in your own back yard.",
    "BlightwoodRoot": "These roots have absorbed more of the anima than other roots around them. It has made them stronger and more durable than the rest of the flora around them. ",
    "RedBerries": "These simple berries might be the gap between life and death, if eaten at the right time. A favourite treat for the villagers of Bramblemead, who would mix the berries into various desserts to enjoy. The red berry pie was particularly popular with visiting Wild Knights. ",
    "PrimordialHeart": "These strange hearts contain the lingering essence of a nature spirit. A gentle heartbeat, waiting to be reborn to a world free of Imaru’s corruption.\r\n\r\nAshenfall’s nature spirits are creatures of the land, possessing myriad forms and shapes as dictated by the wild anima flowing around them. In Fellhollow their favoured form is that of the terrorbird, a beast favoured by gnome riders on the mainland.\r\n\r\nAs Imaru’s hunger infected the land, it began to leech away the vitality that kept these creatures healthy and strong. Their forests waned, from great acres of willow trees, down to a handful of groves, the last places their power would reach.\r\n\r\nAs the land dies, so too do the spirits that protect it. As creatures of the endless cycle, they must be hunted, die and be reborn to return to strength. Yet, there are no hunters in Fellhollow any more. No great beasts roaming the land to slay these gentle spirits.\r\n\r\nSo, you must be that hunter. You must kill the spirit, so that it may be reborn when Fellhollow thrives once more. How ever many years from now that may be.",
    "Seeds.Watermelon": "These tiny seeds can grow into a delightful patch of scrumptious watermelons. Use them to grow plants that can create delicious fruit salads, add garnish to garish cocktails, or you could paint faces on them if you’re very lonely.",
    "KingsToken": "These tokens are a unique creation, crafted hundreds of years ago by high priests of Lougrim. They are passed down from monarch to monarch, a sign of their overwhelming power and strength. The power of a king has waned over the centuries, no longer sovereigns of nations, but now masters of the clan. Servants of the High Queen, Kuldra, the god-eater.\r\n\r\nInside each token rests a single pebble taken from Antumnos, the Garou homeworld. ",
    "PurifiedKingsToken": "These tokens are a unique creation, crafted hundreds of years ago by high priests of Lougrim. They are passed down from monarch to monarch, a sign of their overwhelming power and strength. This token has been blessed as part of an ancient Garou ritual, symbolically wiping away Gielinor's \"corruption\". Although it looks no different to before it was purified, it feels strangely lighter and is a little cold to the touch. \r\n\r\nInside each token rests a single pebble taken from Antumnos, the Garou homeworld, and if you focus your senses you're sure you can feel the faintest pulse from within. ",
    "GraniteMaulWrap": "These wrappings are made from the leather of a powerful and dangerous beast. The ambient anima of Ashenfall infuses it with a sturdiness that most leather wrapping can’t equal.   ",
    "SilverRock": "They say that silver is touched by the moon, explaining why the lycanthropes are so affected by it. Silver makes for an exceptionally good magical conductor, making it highly prized by enchanters everywhere. ",
    "Pumpkin": "They seem strange without faces, don’t they?\r\n\r\nHeralds of the spookiest of seasons, the humble pumpkin exists in two worlds, the world of the living and the world of the dead. When carved it acts as a ward against dangerous spirits and when mixed with spices and added to warm drinks, it acts as a lure for ex-girlfriends, distracting them as it gives you time to escape.",
    "FlankSteak": "Thick and juicy, the flank steak is more filling than you might first expect. The dense meat means each mouthful makes you fuller for longer.",
    "BestialMeat": "Thick and tough to chew, this meat makes for a filling meal, but an exercise to bite through.",
    "HardenedLeather": "Thicker and more resilient than normal leather. Still smells though.",
    "Perks": "Think of perks a little like status effects that don’t want to kill you. They’re friendly, helpful even. Certain foods, drinks, potions and other things, may give you temporary benefits. These benefits, called perks, might be resistances to certain effects, or perhaps they’ll give you an edge in combat.\r\n\r\nThere are several perks to discover and mastering them will make your adventures much easier.",
    "PoisonPlantBulb": "This bulb is infused with deadly toxins. Prized by herblore enthusiasts and hated by chefs in equal measure.",
    "CapeOfTheUndead": "This cape is all that remains of the once proud archers of Fellhollow. Hunters and scouts who would stalk the forests and woodland hunting game to feed the other villagers. As the time dragged on their flesh and clothes have rotted away, leaving only this cape as a reminder of their existence.",
    "UnfiredClayDecoration": "This clay nicknack will look really nice on a shelf, once you’ve cooked it in the kiln. Otherwise it’ll just leave red stains everywhere.",
    "AbyssalDemonCape": "This cloak is infused with the dark power of the Abyss. It's probably fine, definitely not cursed at all. Honest. ",
    "FarmMeat": "This dense meat is a sturdy meal when prepared properly. It comes from larger animals who are far less willing to give it up than their smaller kin, making it a well deserved delicacy. ",
    "GoblinPack": "This foul smelling container is made from animal hide and filled with whatever trinkets its owner collected. Occasionally a goblin will keep a pet beetle, worm or spider in these, so be careful when opening.",
    "RedTopaz": "This glittering gemstone really catches the light, sparkling like a tiny little star that’s dying. Count Valingard, one of Brynmoor’s nobility in the third age, was very fond of these stones and was said to have an entire chandelier made from them. Sadly, dragon fire appears to be particularly good at melting them, so the chandelier is no longer with us.",
    "Kwuarm": "This herb has travelled far to deliver its malicious benefits to the people of Ashenfall. Its name, in Dwarvish, means “warm death” since it causes a terrible burning fever if the sap is consumed.\r\n\r\nCrushed down, so that it’s sap and oils can be easily accessed, this herb is a requirement in some of the deadliest and most dangerous poisonous mixtures.",
    "ClayMold": "This mould can be used in crafting.",
    "Liverwort": "This plant is not fun to encounter, and many gardeners scream in horror when they see their telltale bulbs. They contain a strange gas that reacts violently to the air, which is problematic at the best of times.\r\n\r\nBizarrely they were much beloved by certain dragonkin seers, the mystical oracles without any clan. They would cultivate the bulbs and use the gasses for certain ritual practices that make little sense to anyone else.",
    "FocusedConstructionPotion": "This potion is sweet, yet strong. The ingredients have stewed within the mixture for some time and allowed it to increase in potency. While it’s perfectly fine to drink it directly from the bottle, you find yourself wishing you had a nice mug to use instead.\r\n\r\nStill, this potion makes you acutely (haha, see what I did there?) aware of angles. You can measure a plank of wood with just a few well placed glares. The inner architect in you revels at the new possibilities before you, and you find yourself planning much further ahead.",
    "FocusedMiningPotion": "This potion makes it easier to swing a pickaxe. Your muscles are more relaxed and able to handle greater strain, making it easier to strike at the ore, learning the weak points.\r\n\r\nDruids used to whip up huge batches of this for visiting dwarves, who would mix it with beer for dubious results.",
    "WeakWarmingPotion": "This potion never chills, always tasting warm on the tongue. It's sweet, like hot chocolate round a warming fire and that feeling spreads throughout the drinker's body, shielding them from the chill of night. ",
    "FocusedAttackPotion": "This potion streamlines your vision and hones your reflexes. Your synapses surge with clarity and you find it easier to understand the nuance of the blade.\r\n\r\nThe Wild Knights relied heavily on these potions to train their new fighters during the decline of the Guthixian faith. With fewer numbers, it became more important to teach people quickly.",
    "LumberjackPotion": "This potions helps you sleep all night and work all day. It gives you the feeling of well oiled elbow and shoulder joints, and it numbs the pain in your muscles that’s created from swinging an axe for long stretches at a time.",
    "RingOfTheMoon": "This ring glints in the moonlight with an unnatural sparkle belying the magic within. The ring’s power causes the wearer’s anima circulation to strengthen in the darkness, but struggling to resist the light of the day.\r\n\r\nVery popular with teenage villagers going about their black-tunic phase.",
    "InspiringRingOfConstruction.BuildersRing": "This ring is infused with tiny elements of the dragonkin Brakka, a powerful creature and an architect of some skill. His hubris remains, even after his death at Kuldra’s hands, and motes of his knowledge can be preserved within crystalline structures.\r\n\r\nAlthough you cannot glean the secrets of the dragonkin from these motes of memory, his instinct and innate talent for construction and be harnessed to create a ring that makes it easier to understand the art of architecture.",
    "RingOfTheHerd": "This ring was often used as an engagement ring. The magic weaves souls together and makes their anima resonate in harmony. The resulting change allows for the body to regenerate more readily, enhancing their time together and forging bonds of companionship.\r\n\r\nNaturally it only requires the bonds of camaraderie, rather than romance, and was very popular among bands of hunters, farmers or anyone else. Longtime comrades would often gift each other with these rings as a sign of their bond of friendship.",
    "Sketchbook": "This sketchbook has been damaged beyond repair, the pictures long-since faded. As far as you can tell, that’s for the best. Seemingly filled with people of unnatural proportions – muscles the size of watermelons, torsos easily five sizes larger than the waistlines, and the ears… so fluffy.\r\n\r\nHands so unnaturally large it creates some sort of primal fear within you.\r\n\r\nIt’s probably for the best that this book was buried beneath the stone.",
    "Clay": "This sticky brown substance is rarely considered the right answer to the classic joke, however it has many more uses outside the realm of low-tier comedy.",
    "Granite": "This stone is stubbornly resistant to magic. Whereas other stones absorb the nascent anima, granite refuses. It exists on its own terms, not the whims of magic. As such it is highly prized in construction projects, as it resists most magic and what enchantments do affect it, last much longer.\r\n\r\nLegend speaks of a weapon forged from this stone. A great hammer, said to be able to crush almost any armour.",
    "DragonAltar": "This strange altar is a creation of dragonkin alchemy, more than it is a place of religious observance. It hums with a faint power, imbued as it is with the energies of Ashenfall. Atop the altar sits a resting place for a challenge horn, a device used to summon a dragon and force them into battle. \r\n\r\nThis altar is attuned to General Velgar, a warrior dragon infused with the power of the earth. ",
    "RuneEssenceGeyser": "This strange material absorbs magical energy, causing it to defy gravity, among other things. It is best known for the creation of runes, powerful little stones that can be used to cast spells. A pickaxe is needed to harvest the essence. \r\n\r\nThe floating essence rocks can be knocked out of orbit, with a little effort, making them easier to mine. ",
    "ClayVessel": "This vessel can be sealed to allow for the storage of potions.",
    "PurifyingWater": "This water is drawn from a rejuvenation pool and contains within it the spark to fight off the Wither-rot. Although Death keeps these pools active in modern times, a long time ago these pools would have been blessed by Imaru and her magic. She created the water as a way to stave off illness and corruption, in the population she protected.",
    "DirtyWater": "This water will quench your thirst a little, but it contains a faint residue of a cataclysmic event that afflicted Ashenfall. As a result, there are traces of a tainted substance in the water that will damage the soul of anyone who drinks it, if they’re not a native that’s adapted to it.",
    "Seeds.Irit": "Thorny irit is a pain to harvest. You must seek out the spiky roots, and the battle with them before you can claim the herbs for yourself. With these seeds you can skip some of that faff and instead grow them wherever you like, the very pinnacle of convenience.",
    "GarouKing": "Those who complete the rite of the moon, and usurp their leaders, become the kings of the garou. The title of King is gender neutral, and kings of all genders are recorded across garou history. This is because the mantle of queen has belonged to only two beings, Lougrim and her devourer.\r\n\r\nWhen Lougrim first visited the world of the garou, a place known as Antumnos, she was treated as prey. Her hunters were no match for a goddess, of course, and she assumed the forms of powerful predators, forcing them into submission.\r\n\r\nShe fell upon the capital of the garou nation, the city of Magrawn, where she called out a challenge to all the reigning kings of the garou clans. They answered the summons and she defeated each of them together in a single, decisive, battle. From then on, she held the mantle of High Queen of the garou.\r\n\r\nA title she held until Kuldra devoured her, earning the dragon queen her moniker - God Eater.",
    "FallenHopliteChest": "Those who joining the Fellhollow military, who were not from the more noble bloodlines, would join the force known as the hoplites. This force of warrior farmers named themselves after the hoplite forces of Hallow, legendary forces spoken of in some of the Saradominist text.\r\n\r\nThese hoplites would meet at the end of the week, to train and practice battle manoeuvres. They became walls of impenetrable shields, or terrifying spiked shells, moving with the sort of unity that only comes over years and years of training.\r\n\r\nTheir secret, so they claimed, was the weekly barbeque. Once training was resolved, if they had performed well enough, the retired members of the hoplites would cook them delicious meats and roasted vegetables, served with an overly generous helping of alcoholic beverages.  ",
    "FallenHopliteHelm": "Those who joining the Fellhollow military, who were not from the more noble bloodlines, would join the force known as the hoplites. This force of warrior farmers named themselves after the hoplite forces of Hallow, legendary forces spoken of in some of the Saradominist text.\r\n\r\nThey were disciplined in a way that earned them great respect. Each of them acting as the shield for the person next to them and a spear for the enemy in front. They wore armour reflecting some of the style of Hallow, with plateskirts rather than sturdy grieves, allowing for more freedom of movement, a trait they took great advantage of.",
    "FallenHopliteTassets": "Those who joining the Fellhollow military, who were not from the more noble bloodlines, would join the force known as the hoplites. This force of warrior farmers named themselves after the hoplite forces of Hallow, legendary forces spoken of in some of the Saradominist text.\r\n\r\nWhen the withering began it was the hoplites that were most affected. Their unity and loyalty became a weakness, as members within their ranks fell to the affliction and then turned on the others. This shattered their synchronicity and spread paranoia and distrust among the ranks.\r\n\r\nAs their members fell, they would rise as the undead and immediately turn on their brethren, ripping the group apart from the inside.\r\n\r\nAt least in death they stand together again.",
    "NecromancersCrown": "Those who wield the power over life and death have a tendency to crave control. They need to manage things in their life on a pathological level, and this need often grows into a desire to manage the lives of everyone else.\r\n\r\nThe most well known necromancer in Fellhollow was a man called Calius Waithe. He was one of the Children of Shadows until he decided that their policies of peaceful evil wasn’t what he was looking for. He was more an overtly evil sort of guy.\r\n\r\nHe decided, on one fateful autumnal eve, to raise an army of the undead and march them on Emberwood. He imagined the village cowering before him as he named himself their new king.",
    "TinRock": "Tin and copper are the classic double act, combining their essence to make the superior bronze. ",
    "Seeds.Harralander": "Tired of sinking into swamp-mud in search of these fragrant little herbs? Well now you can grow them yourself.\r\n\r\nThe fragrance of these herbs is even better without the swamp to dull it, and can be a very pleasant pick-me-up first thing in the day.",
    "GoblinWarBanner": "To most goblins, war is like breathing. Every action a precursor to conflict. Every morning they wake with a focus on picking a fight. They rarely kill one another, it would be counterproductive, but they enjoy finding someone different enough that they can justify a spat.\r\n\r\nThis is what Bandos engineered them for. When the god arrived on Yu’Biusk, he urged the population into constant conflict. Nations became states, that were ground down into tribes. An entire civilisations reduced to platoons and squadrons.\r\n\r\nThis cape bears the symbol of Bandos, a weapon known as the kyzaj – a brutal blade designed to eviscerate an opponent.\r\n\r\nOnce this was a banner, praising the war god, but now it lies forgotten and the other banners defiled to worship a new Big High War God. Velgar, who defeated a god in single combat.",
    "BeefAndTomatoStew": "Tomatoes go well with just about everything. They enhance the flavours of anything they’re paired with and a nice beef stew is no exception. The combined flavours are comforting as well as filling, and almost make you forget that you’re a long way from home, on a continent filled with murderous dragons.",
    "SteelArrows": "Traditional wisdom says that to kill a zombie, you must destroy the brain. This is disappointingly untrue in Fellhollow, the animating force of Imaru’s hunger needs no thought to drive it forward.\r\n\r\nNo, a far better plan is to destroy the bones and muscles with sharp metal-tipped spikes launched at the creatures with as much force as you can manage. Steel arrows perform this task very nicely.",
    "SummonShelter": "Transforming dirt, stone and nearby twigs and leaves into canvas, this spell has saved many a Wild Knight from a cold night in the rain. A favourite among those engaged in long hunts in the Highlands, it has helped fight in the constant battle against fatigue. \r\n\r\nOne Wild Knight, a loner called Ansel, would use this spell to grant him shelter as he wandered round the lands. He would journey to the highest peaks or the deepest crevices, using this spell to grant him a pinch of comfort in the least comfortable of regions. Sadly, it would offer no protection against poison, so when he rested in the Blightblood Swamp, he never awoke again.",
    "WhitePlatelegs": "Trapped by a shrine that stood atop a rocky island, surrounded by the hungry dead, Sir Lateef prayed to Saradomin with all his strength. He begged for salvation, for a miracle that might free him, but silence was his only answer.\r\n\r\nHe barricaded himself atop the monument, the dead scrabbling at his defences, their unholy growling keeping him from even a moment’s sleep.\r\n\r\nAs time drew on, and hunger and thirst took him, he turned to the symbol of Saradomin in defeated rage. Where was his god? Why was he abandoned? Had he not spent his life in service of the lord of light?\r\n\r\nEnraged, he took his sword and cleaved Saradomin’s symbol from its plinth, causing it to crash to the floor. Filled with guilt, he hid the fallen piece from his sight. His last act was one of blasphemy, but perhaps an act justly performed.",
    "Treequipment_name": "Treequipment",
    "StoneAxe": "Trees are arrogant creatures, standing tall in the forests and refusing to bow to the wind. For decades they spread their roots claiming dominion over the nearby soil. The claws of beasts are a mere distraction. Raging storms barely rustle their leaves. They refuse to move for anyone, arrogant to the core.\r\n\r\nYet, it is not the wind, nor the heavens that shatter their resolve. Indeed, it is the lowliest of stones, refined to an edge. No matter how old the tree, how sturdy its fame, a single stone, the size of your fist, can bring the mightiest ash low.  \r\n\r\nThe stone axe is an alliance of such a rock, working together with branches cast aside in the tree’s hubris. Proof that even the lowliest of us can make a difference.",
    "Peach": "Try not to think too hard about the nature of transmutation as you consume this fruit. It's sweet, juicy and the pseudo-necromantic origins only make it a little disturbing. Oh, sure, it tastes nice and it goes surprisingly well in a crumble, but it's a little disturbing when you get that unexpected crunch. \r\n\r\nBest not to think about it. Ignorance is bliss. ",
    "WillowLongbow": "Ulv had many skills, but hunting was his strongest. He was inherently quiet, still as the night, and he could hold his position, in the trees or bushes, for hours at a time. Ravanna was never good at this. When the two of them hunted together, Ravanna would sneeze, cough or scream at the sight of an unexpected insect, and the two of them would watch their quarry disappear off into the night.\r\n\r\nAlthough this would occasionally test their friendship, Ulv never really held it against Ravanna. Instead he continued attempting to teach the necromancer the ways of the forest, and the techniques the Wild Knights would use to track down game. It never took. Try as she might, Ravanna was never going to be silent as a shadow.\r\n\r\nIn apology, she looked for a way to create a weapon that would reduce the impact of her noisy nature. Longbows fired further and faster, meaning that less stealth was needed. She made her way into Fellhollow in search of a material strong enough, and returned with willow branches that she crafted into a longbow as a gift for her friend.\r\n\r\nTheir hunts improved dramatically afterward. That was, until they became the hunted.",
    "Attack": "Unfortunately, conflict is inevitable on Ashenfall. You will need to hit things with clubs, stab them with daggers and impale your foes with a volley of carefully crafted arrows.\r\n\r\nThere are three main combat styles, magic, ranged and melee, each one has its strengths and its weaknesses.\r\n\r\nMagic attacks are capable of generating powerful side effects, air magic can knock something back, fire magic can burn your enemies to a crisp. However, you will need a steady supply of runes to keep casting spells.\r\n\r\nRanged attacks can snipe enemies from afar, but you’ll need to make sure you don’t run out of arrows.\r\n\r\nMelee attacks get up close and personal, but you won’t need to worry about ammunition to lay the smackdown on that uppity little goblin – not Zanik – who’s been giving you lip.\r\n\r\nWhen engaged in battle, keep an eye on your stamina, as each attack will cost you some of it. As you level up your combat skills, you’ll be able to deal longer combo chains to more efficiently use your stamina.",
    "Seeds.Snapdragon": "Unfortunately, these seeds do not grow adorable little scaly nightmares to terrorise your enemies.\r\n\r\nFortunately, they do grow into useful little medicinal herbs with various uses in herblore. So, an alright trade off, even if the tiny dragons would be better.",
    "Seeds.Tomato": "Unlock the plant that causes the most arguments amongst the truly bored, the humble tomato. Is it a fruit? A vegetable? A plague on sandwiches everywhere? Well, now you can start these arguments wherever you like, by planting one of these beauties in a fresh farming patch.",
    "WeaponPoison": "Used in crafting Poison Arrows or to coat your equipped Melee Weapon with Poison for a short time.\r\n\r\nThere are times in life where you really want someone to regret ever meeting you. It could be a long-held grudge, it could be on the battlefield, or it could be in a never-ending meeting that could so easily have been an email but has now persisted past your lunchbreak and you had so been looking forward to that sushi… ahem… sorry, where was I? Oh, yes, pain!\r\n\r\nPoison is sometimes considered to be a coward’s weapon. Personally, I prefer to think of it as the smart weapon choice, after all, honour is only worth anything if you’re the winner of the battle. A corpse with honour still rots in the dirt. With a blade coated in poison, you can almost guarantee the death of your enemies, all it takes is a single nick and their fate is sealed.\r\n\r\nAn investment well worth making, if you ask me.",
    "WeaponBarbs": "Used to craft Barbed Arrows or to coat your equipped melee weapon with barbs, causing Bleed buildup for a short duration.\r\n\r\nSometimes you just really want to hurt something. It happens. We each have that little voice that tells us to make something bleed, just a little.\r\n\r\nWell, blacksmiths thought to themselves “what if we listen to that voice?” and created these little doohickies. Barbs for your weapons, so that they may tear, rend and otherwise force your opponents to bleed with every strike.",
    "FireOil": "Used to craft Fire arrows.\r\n\r\nSome people just like to watch the world burn… and you can too, with these new handy fire oils! That’s right, just lob one of these beauties at your enemies and watch them ignite. Enjoy their screams as their skin boils away and breathe in the delightful aroma of a fresh barbecue!\r\n\r\nFire oil, like snake oil, was once the creation of a travelling merchant to Ashenfall. She had journeyed to Brynmoor alongside some of the Saradominist settlers, and with her she brought pungent unguents, elucidating elixirs, marvellous medicines, and weapons of localised destruction.\r\n\r\nWhen her wares summoned the call for refunds, she would dazzle the onlookers with a sudden fiery display and then disappear into the alleyways until she had been forgotten. She would then return in one of a staggering array of disguises, until the tragic day when her wagon hit a pothole during a quick getaway and her many, many, bottles of fire oils decided to sing the song of their people.\r\n\r\nFortunately, even though she and everything she owned, tragically found itself reduced to smouldering atoms, her recipe lived on and found more conventional uses.",
    "VannakaWerewolf": "Vannaka has shared his greatest secret; he’s a werewolf. It is why Duradel and Kuradel could so quickly best him, for they knew his curse and fought him with wolfbane, stripping him of his strength. He comes to Ashenfall in search of the Garou, the ancestors of the werewolves, so that he might understand his inner beast. ",
    "WolfbaneDagger": "Vannaka knows this dagger only too well, for it was used against him every time he tried to prove his mettle against the other slayer masters. Although he would never willingly transform to prove his might, the unsettling sight of the dagger broke his morale enough to make him falter, and that was enough of an edge for his defeat.",
    "RingOfRecoil": "Vengeance isn’t a dish best served cold, but one served quickly. Whilst taking years to enact the cruellest retaliation might work in the civilised world, in the wilds of Ashenfall a quick strike is far more valuable.\r\n\r\nThis ring was first created by one of the Amalgamated, worn as both deterrence and for some small satisfaction when they were targeted by vigilante mobs. It didn’t prevent the bruises, or fix the broken bones, but their assailants attacks would end much more quickly when they discovered they weren’t as untouchable as they thought.",
    "AnimalFleece": "Very popular with newbie argonauts.",
    "DragonboneDagger": "Viika was never fond of creating dragons, they thought the process was cruel and unbecoming. When an egg was laid, Viika saw in that moment both the egg and the dragon it would become. They saw past and presence at the same time, and they grieved the creature before it had even hatched.\r\n\r\nThey pleaded with their sibling, and with the other Dragonkin, to change their treatment of the beasts. But Viika’s curse, like so many Seers, was to be ignored by their kindred.\r\n\r\nA rare egg hatched once, containing a dragon with white scales like pearls. The dragon was infused with magic and possessing a power capable of travelling to other worlds, where she might have lived a happier life. But Viika wept, for they knew she would manage no such ending.\r\n\r\nViika kept the hatchling company. Treated them like their dearest friend, and held them softly when Lacrussa’s need for knowledge, took the dragon’s last breath.\r\n\r\nThe Seer kept only a single keepsake – a blade carved from the tooth of their white-scaled friend. A dagger capable of terrible damage, but as fragile as the friendship that it reminded them of.",
    "Wardstone": "Wardstones are handy tools for anyone working with wards. Armour, when created on an anima-rich continent like Ashenfall, is infused with threads of enchantment that harness natural magic to provide an extra layer of protection for the wearer.\r\n\r\nThis protection depletes upon use, as the energy within the ward is expended on the effect. But the wardstone can rectify this situation… for a price.\r\n\r\nThe stone around the wardstone is hollow and more brittle than it seems, and the rune essence is carefully laid within it along with a final ingredient – a drop of the creators own blood. This blood, and the rune essence formation, creates a sort of harmonious connection between the bearer of the wardstone and the stone itself.\r\n\r\nWhen the stone is broken, it activates the rune essence and draws anima in from the nearest pure source – the soul of the user.\r\n\r\nSouls are containers of pure anima. They grow over time from experience and adventure, the more the bearer of said soul enriches their life with activity, the stronger the soul. This means that the damage the wardstone creates, whilst not insignificant, is easily repaired through the simple passage of time. Especially on Ashenfall, where merely surviving to the next hour is an adventure.\r\n\r\nWardstones are great in a pinch, but one must be careful not to use too many, less the damage they deal to themselves becomes too great.",
    "WaterRune": "Water is a strange thing, required by all living things, yet capable of ending civilisations. Rain is vital to the survival of a kingdom, yet its citizens lament the grey clouds forming overhead. Mages understand that water is a special sort of power, capable of being shaped in a multitude of ways. Water runes embody this fluidity of concept, finding use in many different types of spell. ",
    "Infusions": "Water is great, drink lots of it, but sometimes it can get a little… repetitive. Dull even. If you find yourself in that situation then don’t fall to despair, because there’s a solution: Infusions.\r\n\r\nDue to some cataclysmic nonsense on the continent, water on Ashenfall is infected with some sort of taint or corruption. Boiling the water seems to remove this imperfection somehow, but it also allows you the opportunity to spice up your drinks.\r\n\r\nAdding certain things to water as you boil it, such as berries, bones or even antlers, will provide additional benefits. Fruity infusions are more hydrating, filling your Hydration meter faster. Certain animal parts may also provide other benefits, such as improved battle prowess or more efficient dodging.\r\n\r\nTry experimenting with different infusions to create drinks more tailored to your playstyle.",
    "Thirst": "Water is the most basic necessity for all known life on Gielinor. Dehydration is deadlier than the strongest monster, guaranteeing death in its victims if they suffer it long enough.\r\n\r\nYour Hydration meter tracks how well hydrated you are. The higher your meter, the better your rate of stamina regeneration – allowing you to perform more actions. Skill longer. Fight better.\r\n\r\nShould your Hydration meter reach zero, you’ll start to suffer constant health loss until you can refill your meter by drinking a source of water.\r\n\r\nDirty water can be found in ponds, lakes and other bodies of water around Ashenfall. Dirty water can be purified by boiling it at a campfire.",
    "Endurance": "We all need to rest from time to time. Sometimes everything just gets too much, we get tired, stressed, and just generally beaten down by the world, and it’s no different on Ashenfall.\r\n\r\nRest represents your overall state of resilience. Your ability to overcome to mental trials of the wild. It will deplete over time if you’re not resting, as well as every time you suffer lethal damage. Certain environmental effects will also take their toll.\r\n\r\nThe lower your Rest, the less functional you’ll be. You’ll find it harder to lug everything around. It becomes more draining to swing an axe, or a sword. Even magic will become more difficult as you tired yourself out.\r\n\r\nThere are four levels of Rest:\r\n\r\nWell-rested - this will leave you functioning at your best\r\n\r\nTired, you’ll start to suffer penalties to your Stamina, as well as your Carrying Capacity.\r\n\r\nKnackered - everything is really starting to get to you. You’ll suffer penalties to Stamina, Carry Capacity and even find yourself thirstier and hungrier.\r\n\r\nExhausted- you’ll suffer heavy penalties to Stamina and Carry Capacity, as well as finding spells more expensive to cast.\r\n\r\nFortunately you can recover your Rest with a nice long rest in bed.",
    "Food": "We are all slaves to the beast within the belly. Its gurgling roar urges us ever onward to consume, consume, consume. At least that’s the excuse I use when people ask about my ever-expanding midriff.\r\n\r\nStill, we need food to survive, even the most restrictive (sane) diet plans acknowledge that. Ashenfall has a wealth of options, some delicious and others… well… less so. Food will increase your Sustenance meter, which makes you healthier and heartier. Some food will also provide special benefits, such as resistances to poison.\r\n\r\nThe higher quality the food, the longer its effects last, so make sure you’re always making the best meals you can.",
    "BonePickaxe": "We are made of sturdier materials than we give ourselves credit for. The human body can withstand a staggering force before our bones give way to the stresses. So too is it for the bones of animals, especially those of Ashenfall. Here, the magic seeps into the marrow and strengthens the bone to be sturdier than before.\r\n\r\nWhen used as tools, refined into weapons, these bones can be made to break stone and release metal ore from its prison.  ",
    "GarouHunter": "Weakness: Air\r\nResistance: Crush\r\n\r\nThe lower-born garou can never rise to the role of king. Many have tried, but all have failed. The highborn are simply stronger and wield magic that the lowborn cannot match. They are not mistreated by their kin. There are no garou slaves or servants, but a lowborn garou can never rule.\r\n\r\nMost accept their place and find a way to be useful, a means to enjoy the most important thing to their kind—the hunt. \r\n\r\nNothing ignites the blood of a garou more than the hunt. It reaches into a primal part of their being and gives them a reason to live. All worries are cast aside. All responsibilities forgotten. There is nothing but the thrill of the chase and the anticipation of the kill. \r\n\r\nIt is no surprise, therefore, that lowborn garou often take up the hunter's mantle. Their precision with a bow is practically unmatched, and they delight in nothing more than seeing their quarry draw its last breath. ",
    "VaultOverlord": "Weakness: Crush\r\nResistance: None\r\nImmune: Poison, Bleed, Burning\r\n\r\nA pride of Brakka’s ingenuity, the Vault Overlord towers over the other vault guardians as a shiny example of one dragonkin’s ego. Larger and more physically powerful than the other guardians, the Overlord was a siege engine put to work as a bodyguard. \r\n\r\nIt was set to guard Brakka’s most prized treasures. Objects he kept in the vaults, so that none but he might gaze upon their beauty. \r\n\r\nIn time the other dragonkin learned of the Overlord and created versions of their own, setting them to guard their secrets against any that might dare pilfer them. \r\n\r\nIn the time of the dragon uprising, the Overlords could have proved a useful soldier against Kuldra’s brood. Yet the arrogance and shortsightedness, of their dragonkin masters, meant that these walking siege weapons would never see the light of day. ",
    "VaultGuardianRanged": "Weakness: Crush\r\nResistance: None\r\nImmune: Poison, Bleed, Burning\r\n\r\nIt was Avisk who raised the question of whether the vault guardians had souls. They weren’t intelligent and were useful for little more than manual labour and defence. Yet, they possessed a spark of creativity that Avisk argued was a sign of something more profound.\r\n\r\nLacrussa dismissed him; of course, they were tools, nothing more. Still, there were moments when even she doubted. When she found her sibling, the seer Viika, laughing with one, she questioned everything, especially since Viika had never laughed with her.\r\n\r\nShe studied them carefully for years afterwards, trying to figure out what Viika saw in them. Yet, there was never enough there to make her believe. Viika was, after all, prone to behaviour that Lacrussa could not understand. She loved her sibling dearly; breaking them free of the gilded prison they’d been raised in was what had made her an outcast to her people. Yet she knew that, deep down, she would never truly understand them.\r\n\r\nThe vault guardians were a tool and a mystery that would haunt her until her final moments.",
    "VaultGuardianMelee": "Weakness: Crush\r\nResistance: None\r\nImmune: Poison, Bleed, Burning\r\n\r\nIt was the scientist, Lacrussa, who discovered the properties of rune essence, when properly prepared. It could be refined into a powder and then inscribed across stone and metal, to create complex magical effects. The vault guardians were one of her proudest achievements. Reflections of living beings, a power belonging only to the Elder Gods.\r\n\r\nThough slow of wit, they made up for it with an excess of muscle. Their attacks were powerful and deadly, capable of toppling almost any threat. In the early days of their creation, thousands of years ago, there were no living beings that could have withstood their power.\r\n\r\nDuring the dragon rebellion, it was Lacrussa’s greatest regret that she could not send the guardians beyond the magical enchantments of the vaults. The magic that animated them only worked within those walls, and dragons had no need of dragonkin secrets.",
    "VaultArchon": "Weakness: Crush\r\nResistance: None\r\nImmune: Poison, Bleed, Burning\r\n\r\nRasthin was never fond of just leaving things to chance. She always saw a need to create bigger, deadlier, weapons with which to destroy her foes. With the dragons she encouraged Lacrussa’s experiments, searching for ways to make them into a more potent force. With the vault guardians she pushed them further still.\r\n\r\nThe archon is a marvel of dragonkin science. Infused with enough magical force to level a small village, if left uncontained. The magic that runs through its circuits acts as a connection to all the other guardians, motivating and animating them by the archon’s will.\r\n\r\nAt the height of their power, these creatures could out-strategise the most cunning generals. Fortunately, time has dulled this wits and their reflexes, reducing them to a strength only deadly to a village, rather than a nation.",
    "GarouThane": "Weakness: Crush\r\nResistance: Stab\r\n\r\nPowerful noble-born garou follow two paths in life. Either they pledge themselves to magic and follow the path of the druid, or they rely on their strength and adopt the role of the thane. \r\n\r\nThanes are brutal, powerful creatures, capable of snapping spines with a single well-placed kick. They wear armour that would crush a human warrior beneath its weight, and their shields can stop almost any attack. \r\n\r\nWhen a thane thinks they are strong enough, they challenge the local king to single combat. This is a ritual practice that involves an ancient rite whose meaning has long been forgotten. The thane anoints themselves with special warpaint and offer reverence to a long-dead goddess before they find the king and call them to battle. \r\n\r\nMost die. \r\n\r\nA king has earned their place by being a master of warfare, capable of defending themselves against countless challengers. Yet no thane turns down this chance to do battle because what greater glory is there than killing a warrior king?",
    "FungalRat": "Weakness: Crush\r\nResistance: Stab\r\nImmune: Poison\r\n\r\nSometimes a giant rat can feast on too much magic, suffusing themselves with the energies of their environment. For the putrid rat, they can take the pestilence, rot and decay into themselves, merging with the mushrooms and poisonous fungi that they consume.\r\n\r\nThese pitiful creatures have veins that pulse with poison and upon their deaths the spores they create leave scars of rot in the world.",
    "PlagueKing": "Weakness: Crush\r\nResistance: Stab\r\nImmune: Poison\r\n\r\nWhen a fungal rat lives too long, it can consume so much pestilence and decay that it bloats beyond the size of its weaker kin. In this state it absorbs ambient anima, becoming stronger and deadlier, a monarch of its kind.\r\n\r\nThe plague king is bane to the healthy. It spreads sickness wherever it goes, becoming a terrible disease against the living.\r\n\r\nIt was the duty of the Wild Knights to slay any fungal rat in sight, to make sure that a plague king never had a chance to develop. With the passing of the Wild Knights of Bramblemead, the world finds itself in terrible danger, should this beast ever learn how to swim.",
    "Chicken": "Weakness: Fire\r\nResistance: Air\r\n\r\nMost chickens are perfectly normal, content to live happy lives consuming seeds, insects and anything else that comes their way. Their eggs provide the means to create some of the most delicious recipes, and their meat sustains many a weary adventurer on those cold Ashenfall nights. \r\n\r\nBut not all chickens are so innocent. Once a generation, beneath the blood moon, a chick will hatch that knows nothing but malice. Consumed with a desire to corrupt and destroy, these creatures grow powerful on concentrated hate. Many fireside stories speak of the evil chicken and the lives they have destroyed. To keep this threat at bay, farmers traditionally prevent any egg from hatching if it gives them the heebie-jeebies.",
    "GarouDruid": "Weakness: Fire\r\nResistance: Crush\r\nImmune: Poison\r\n\r\nNoble-born garou are larger than their common-born kin, resulting from very careful breeding practices. The noble bloodlines are stronger, faster, and capable of magic, clearly superior to the lower class of their society. \r\n\r\nThose who practice magic are known as druids. They are different to the druids of the Guthixian faith. They do not follow the idea of harmonious collaboration with nature. Instead, the garou see everything through the eyes of the hunter. They use magic to chase down their prey. Cast spells to cripple their quarry. Incantations to strip the life from their target, guaranteeing the kill. \r\n\r\nIn the age of Lougrim, the druids were tempered by tradition and ritual. They were encouraged to focus only on the right sort of prey. But under the auspice of the dragon queen, they are more prone to cruelty. \r\n\r\nSurvival of the fittest is the chant they now sing. ",
    "DireWolf": "Weakness: Fire\r\nResistance: None\r\n\r\nDire wolves are the apex of their species. They have absorbed more of the ambient anima of the region, making them stronger and more powerful than their smaller kin. Their attacks rend the flesh, leaving lasting damage that their foes might never recover from.\r\n\r\nThey lead their packs with absolute authority, refuting any challenges with only a glance.\r\n\r\nThe Wild Knights respected the dire wolves, preferring not to kill them if there was any other choice. They believed that if the dire wolves could be deterred from a particular course of action, the other wolves would follow. Invariably this was true, but there were always exceptions.\r\n\r\nThere are legends of a silver wolf among the garou.  Some of them whisper that it is a goddess reincarnated. That she is rebuilding her power, to challenge their new High Queen again.",
    "GoblinRunt": "Weakness: Fire\r\nResistance: None\r\n\r\nGoblin society is not one of equals. The strong do not guard the weak, and the vulnerable should expect to be exploited at every turn. Those too weak to stand, die. Those not strong enough to earn respect, receive nothing but scorn.\r\n\r\nSo it is for the runts of goblin society. They survive birth and they live their lives grabbing what sustenance they can from the world around them. They scour the ruins of farms and villages, desperately looking for weak creatures to cook and eat. A diet of scrawny rats, sickened kebbits and on the really desperate days, a vegetable or two.\r\n\r\nThe runts of Brynmoor expect nothing and receive less. It is the dream of each of them to be able to prove themselves worthy of the respect of their peers. They must win a battle, show they can fight and defend themselves. Might proves right, after all, and they are so very tired of being wrong. So they hurl themselves at any passing adventurer, in the hope of a swift kill that might end their life of quiet shame.",
    "GoblinWarrior": "Weakness: Fire\r\nResistance: None\r\n\r\nGoblins were once the minions of the war god, Bandos, until his death at the hands of Armadyl, the god of justice. Raised on the brutal world of Yu’Biusk, goblins have known no other existence than one of constant battle and bloodshed. Their religion and society decries education and self-improvement in favour of conflict for conflict’s sake. To run from battle is the greatest sin to goblinkind. \r\n\r\nGoblins reproduce quickly and grow up fast. Many of their wars have been won by sheer attrition, as there are always more goblins to hurl at the enemy.   \r\n\r\nThe goblins of Ashenfall have turned away from Bandos, putting their faith in the dragon that circles Ghornfell. Velgar is a cruel and uncaring master, yet the goblins live longer lifespans under his watch than they ever did under Bandos. ",
    "GoblinBruiser": "Weakness: Fire\r\nResistance: None\r\n\r\nMight makes right and these goblins are never wrong.\r\n\r\nThe leaders of the goblin tribes by strength alone, the bruisers enjoy nothing more than a life of bullying other goblins around. They are assumed to be the smartest, because if you tell them they’re wrong, you’ll get a fist in the face. They’re the most charismatic, because if you don’t like what they’re saying… well… fist and face return once more. In fact most of their skillset consists of fists approaching faces at high velocity.\r\n\r\nThey are one of the few goblins with brains enough to wedge a large sheet of metal between their vulnerable internal organs and the person poking at them with a sharp object. So in goblin society that makes them an unrivalled genius.",
    "Wolf": "Weakness: Fire\r\nResistance: None\r\n\r\nThe wolves of Ashenfall are dangerous creatures. Their anima-infused muscles make them faster. Their razor-sharp teeth would pierce the armour of the Brynmoor villagers. It would be the job of the Wild Knights to hunt them down and prevent them from attacking the human settlements.\r\n\r\nThey did this by culling the leaders of the packs, as well as to lure larger groups away from human settlements, with fresh deer kills.\r\n\r\nOne year, an alpha wolf, known as Shatterfang, ignored the lures and followed his nose to Bramblemead. The resulting confrontation was an unmistakable bloodbath, with villagers locking themselves in their homes, praying to Guthix that the wolves wouldn’t break through the door.\r\n\r\nThe Wild Knights sent in their hunters, but Shatterfang was as clever as he was cruel. He used the urban structures against the hunters, avoiding their arrows and escaping their wrath. He was finally felled when an unlikely ally used dark magic to lock the wolf’s bones in place, while her companion struck from the shadows with blades forged from the wolf’s kin.   \r\n\r\nThe villagers offered any reward to their saviours. Money, weapons, gems, yet the pair asked only for Shatterfang’s skull in return.",
    "JadeVine": "Weakness: Fire\r\nResistance: None\r\nImmune: Poison\r\n\r\nThe jade vine is an unusual plant, that likes to attack anyone that gets too close. With a maw full of thorns and a stem full of hatred, the jade vine has been the bane of many a hunter.\r\n\r\nThey aren’t actually native to Ashenfall, at least not originally. They were brought to the continent by the druid Hokistix, who had a terrible taste in houseplants. He was forced to live on the edge of the village, until he disappeared.\r\n\r\nIt’s not really a mystery as to where he vanished to, but suffice to say his plants were very well fed.",
    "WildAzureVine": "Weakness: Fire\r\nResistance: None\r\nImmune: Poison\r\n\r\nThese plants have become infused with the storms that occasionally rage across Ashenfall. They have absorbed the magic into their cells and are ready to turn them against any that might approach. While mindless, these plants are filled with a desire to turn their electrified fury upon any that come near them, ready to shock anything not adequately prepared. \r\n\r\nThe dragonkin would cultivate these plants, the seeds having been brought with them from their city of Orthen. They would use the oil, generated by crushing the bulbs, in strange alchemies, the uses of which have yet to be fully discovered. ",
    "Kebbits": "Weakness: None\r\nResistance: None\r\n\r\nKebbits are rodent-like creatures that are hunted for their pelts. The kebbits of Ashenmoor are more aggressive than most, though they remain at the bottom of the food chain.\r\n\r\nThey were first brought to the continent by Guthixian settlers, sometime in the first age, and have thrived in their absence. The kebbits of Brynmoor seem uniquely resistant to the effects of the Anima Twist phenomenon.\r\n\r\nLegend says that following a kebbit under the full moon will lead you to your one true love. This has been definitively disproven by scholars from the Wizard’s Tower. However, their papers have been rebuked on the grounds that “they clearly have too much time on their hands”.\r\n\r\nComing from members of the Wizard’s Tower, that’s quite the statement.",
    "Magpie": "Weakness: None\r\nResistance: None\r\n\r\nOne for sorrow, two for joy, yet these little tricksters are rarely found in pairs. The magpie is well known as the thief of the bird family, delighting in stealing objects that glitter. It is said that if you can find a magpie’s nest, it will be full of tiny treasures. The results of testing this theory are often underwhelming.\r\n\r\nA member of the corvid family, the magpie pretends to have no affiliation with the more sinister members of the genus.\r\n\r\nThe magpie’s feathers are much prized by fletchers due to their sleek and elegant design.",
    "Gyrfalcon": "Weakness: None\r\nResistance: None\r\n\r\nThe gyrfalcon is an agile predator. They silently dart through the air, seeking out small game to hunt. Giant rats are their favourite prey, though their flesh is stringy, they are comparatively large meals, compared to the kebbits and voles of the region.\r\n\r\nSome Wild Knights would forge bonds with the falcons. They’re raise them from eggs and carefully train them to assist with scouting and hunting. This symbiotic relationship would allow both hunter and falcon to thrive in Ashenfall’s dangerous environment.",
    "Cow": "Weakness: None\r\nResistance: None\r\n\r\nTo err is human, to moo bovine. So the adage goes. The cow is a poor, lamentable creature. Slaughtered in droves by fledgling adventurers seeking an easy start to their heroic lives, a cow’s life is brief and brutal. Those that survive, the swarms of merciless warriors, are ready to defend one another from their destroyers. \r\n\r\nThey also taste delicious with roast potatoes, gravy and just a smidgeon of horseradish. ",
    "Velgar": "Weakness: None\r\nResistance: None\r\nImmune: Poison, Burning\r\n\r\nGeneral Velgar was one of Kuldra’s most trusted allies in the dragon rebellion. In the years that led to her betrayal of their dragonkin masters, she would seek Velgar’s advice on how to best handle the situation. His answer was always clear, brutality was the only language that their masters would understand.\r\n\r\nHe had been Brakka’s beast of burden. A mighty warrior, forced to be little more than a pack mule for the arrogant architect of the dragonkin. When the war against the goblins and the humans brought the dragons into the conflict, Velgar was finally allowed to join the battle. He slaughtered without mercy, obliterating settlements and entire platoons with barely a sweat.\r\n\r\nBrakka rewarded his brutality with yet more chains. When the conflict dulled, Velgar was made to haul stone and metal, so that Brakka could rebuild damaged fortresses.\r\n\r\nIt is perhaps no wonder, therefore, that he accepted Kuldra’s scheme without hesitation. The first to join her war, aside from her own child. She taught him to absorb the power of the anima font, transforming him into a dragon infused with the might of the earth itself.",
    "GarouBerserker": "Weakness: Ranged\r\nResistance: Air\r\n\r\nThe garou berserker is a force to be reckoned with. Before each battle, they feast on a freshly killed animal and ingest a concoction that alters their perception. This turns their world into kill or be killed, and nothing can persuade them otherwise. \r\n\r\nDruids control berserkers with subtle magic. They manipulate the air around them so that they don’t give off a scent. To the fury-addled mind of the berserker, this makes them practically invisible, and at the very least, it confirms that they are not a threat. \r\n\r\nOnce in their battle state, a berserker will hurl themselves at an opponent, regardless of the enemy’s might. Fighting tooth and nail, as if each breath is in desperation.  In the dragon wars, they were known to charge at the great beasts, becoming little more than snacks in their recklessness. ",
    "Chinchompa": "Weakness: Ranged\r\nResistance: Fire\r\n\r\nThe chinchompa is a nature’s bad joke, a weird branch of evolution that makes very little sense. The creature contains, within its body, a number of organs containing various chemicals and, when the creature feels its life is in danger, it combines these chemicals and immediately explodes.\r\n\r\nIt is assumed that these creatures must have some sort of communal sense of responsibility, martyring themselves for the sake of their herd. Yet chinchompas will happily explode when they’re the only one present, making it a someone pointless moment of self-sacrifice.\r\n\r\nMost sane creatures avoid chinchompas where possible, for very obvious reasons.\r\n\r\nThey are, however, worryingly cute. A fact that has caused trouble for many Brynmoor children. It can be very upsetting when the family pet explodes, taking most of the house with it.  ",
    "Deer": "Weakness: Ranged\r\nResistance: None\r\n\r\nThe graceful deer is a skittish beast, easily frightened by the slightest sounds. On the mainland, deer are a rare sight, sticking to deep forests and areas of fairy enchantment. They were extensively hunted for their delicious meat and sturdy antlers.\r\n\r\nOn Ashenfall, magic has infused their bodies, making their pelts versatile and highly valuable. Well worth the effort of any hunter.",
    "AbyssalDemon": "Weakness: Slash\r\nResistance: Air\r\n\r\nThe Abyssal Demon is a nightmare incarnated. Appearing suddenly out of nowhere and capable of tearing a man to shreds in a single swing.\r\n\r\nNo one knows how the demons arrived on Ashenfall, as they are not native to this plane. They dwell, as their name suggests, in the Abyss, the realm between worlds. They were cast out from their homeworld by the god Zamorak when he led the demon uprising on Infernus, and they have remained hateful ever since. Unable to strike at the god that banished them, they turn their fury on every living thing, existing for nothing more than to perpetuate suffering.\r\n\r\nA necromancer and her Wild Knight ally were the first to encounter the beast. She had been investigating one of the dragonkin ruins, a vault buried deep into the soil, and when they broke the seal, the foul beast emerged.  At least, that’s what she claims. None have been able to venture deep enough into the vaults to confirm the truth of her words.\r\n\r\nShe and her companion vanished from the area soon after, their whereabouts unknown.",
    "Ram": "Weakness: Slash\r\nResistance: Fire\r\n\r\nAs defender of the herd, the ram sees itself as a powerful warrior that can slay the strongest of foes. This might explain why there are fewer of them around than simple genetics might suggest.\r\n\r\nMore aggressive than the average sheep, the ram is prone to picking fights with anything he can. They have been known to headbutt rocks, stomp on the roots of trees and have long, noisy, arguments with the wind.\r\n\r\nThey taste delicious with mint sauce.",
    "Sheep": "Weakness: Slash\r\nResistance: None\r\n\r\nAshenfall’s sheep are wilder than most, running free through the woodlands without a care in the world. Their fleeces are thicker and sturdier than their domesticated counterparts, and there’s barely any danger that one of them might be two penguins in disguise. It happens.",
    "Terrorbird": "Weakness: Stab\r\nResistance: Air\r\n\r\nThe terrorbird lives up to its name with both it’s violent tendencies and the nightmarish cries it makes in the shadows. There are few who can look into the eyes of a terrorbird and not regret every decision in their lives, leading to this moment.\r\n\r\nEarly garou tried to tame the creatures, but the terrorbird’s wild nature prevented them from accepting the yoke of domestication. Instead, they turned on their would-be masters, leading to an even the garou would forever refer to as “the night of green feathers”.\r\n\r\nBest taken out from range, the terrorbird makes itself known by it’s distinctive war-warble, that strikes fear into the heart of any sane creature. ",
    "GiantRat": "Weakness: Stab\r\nResistance: Crush\r\n\r\nRats gorge themselves on anything that they can find. Sometimes, a rat finds itself feasting on powerful magic, such as the pestilence of swamps or the blood of some dread beast. Those who discover such banquets grow to great size, becoming fearsome giants of rodent kind.   \r\n\r\nOn Ashenfall, the Anima Twist has become such a feast, warping these once timid creatures into aggressive and dangerous monsters. The more they feast, the larger their appetites, and they can never truly be sated. ",
    "RatKing": "Weakness: Stab\r\nResistance: Crush\r\n\r\nThe rat king is a giant rat that has consumed the hearts of other rats. A monstrous creature infused with the fear, rage and hatred of its own kind. The magical essence in the hearts infused the rat king, causing it to swell to monstrous size and imbuing it with a terrible hunger it can never sate.\r\n\r\nLegend speaks of a rat king so large, that it threatened the entire village of Bramblemead. It was slain by a brief alliance of the Saradominist Paladins and the Guthixian Wild Knights, but not without significant casualties.\r\n\r\nThe beasts heart was burned over a great pyre in the village, so that no rats might consume any of its remains and threaten a successor rat monarch.",
    "GoblinArcher": "Weakness: Stab\r\nResistance: None\r\n\r\nA goblin archer is swift and merciless. They have no sense of honour to hold them back, no morality to keep their hand. To a goblin, battle for battle’s sake is the purest endeavour, and a clean kill is the most noble act. \r\n\r\nArchers possess a trait rarely found in goblinkind, patience. They wait out their prey, lining up that perfect shot to send their target to the afterlife. Despite this, they are often looked down on by other goblins for their cowardice. The warriors mock them for hiding in the rear lines and attacking from the safety of distance. \r\n\r\nIt’s an insult that goblin archers hear often, though rarely from the same goblin twice. This is mainly because the warriors don’t tend to live long enough to repeat it. ",
    "GreenDragonhideBody": "Wear the skin of your enemies, so that they know to fear you! This is the ranger way, to dress themselves in the outer layers of weaker dragons so that they larger dragons know not to mess with them.\r\n\r\nLove makes fools of all of us. Infatuation more so, especially one-sided. Bilar was in love, so he claimed. He spoke of her beauty. He spoke of her acumen. He spoke of… well, he spoke a lot about things that you do not repeat in polite society. He wanted her and he wanted the ultimate gift to win her affection: a dragon’s heart.\r\n\r\nYet, dragons are unwilling to part with such a vital organ, and Bilar had to get creative. He found a dead dragon, one that had been slain in battle with a rival, and he decided to skin it. With it’s scaley hide in his possession he spent weeks fashioning powerful armour from the remains, until he had an outfit that resembled the creature he hunted.\r\n\r\nSo dressed, he set out towards the great stone circle near Emberwood, knowing that a dragon had recently made it its home. He would slay it, claim its heart and present it to the woman he loved and she would surely agree to be his forever.",
    "GreenDragonhideCoif": "Wear the skin of your enemies, so that they know to fear you! This is the ranger way, to dress themselves in the outer layers of weaker dragons so that they larger dragons know not to mess with them.\r\n\r\nSuch was the logic of Bilar Amaris, a hunter that dwelled withing Emberwood. He was renowned among the locals, skilled with the bow and capable of taking down almost any prey. When the green dragons descended on the region, he decided that he would be the apex predator, hunting them down.\r\n\r\nThis was partially done in a misguided attempt to win the affections of the “black-haired beauty” that had set up a little potion shop in the village. Like many idiots, he had convinced himself that all a woman could ever want was a strong man that was capable of killing something bigger than he was.\r\n\r\nHe was, unsurprisingly, deeply mistaken.",
    "GreenDragonhideLegs": "Wear the skin of your enemies, so that they know to fear you! This is the ranger way, to dress themselves in the outer layers of weaker dragons so that they larger dragons know not to mess with them.\r\n\r\nThe funeral of Bilar Amaris was not a particularly popular one. A few fellow hunters stopped by to pay the briefest of respects, and a drunk great uncle turned up and got the name wrong. His death would have passed into complete obscurity, except for the clothes he was wearing when he died.\r\n\r\nDespite the mauled condition of his corpse, the villagers that found him noticed the surprisingly supple and yet sturdy armour. Barely a scratch covered the dragonhide, and they remarked that if only he’d covered the neck a bit better, then he might not have ended up in the state he did.\r\n\r\nThe villagers brought the armour back and leatherworkers set to work on repeating it and improving it. Soon the best hunters in Emberwood would be wearing green dragonhide armour, and matched with better common sense, it kept them alive much longer.",
    "AshLogs": "What ash logs lack in durability, they make up for in availability. Found all over Brynmoor, ash trees became a vital resource to the humans that resided there. Their druids would enchant the products of the trees, so that they did not rot, nor crumble against time’s assault. This would allow the trees to regrow before it would be necessary to cut them down again, creating a sustainable system.",
    "RotswornZombie": "What is worse than a shambling corpse that years to feast on all your precious fleshy parts? A shambling corpse that’s infected with a terrible rot that will tear your soul apart if you let it.\r\n\r\nThese zombies were once the most faithful followers of Imaru, her chosen servants. Each of them was branded with a special mark that would allow her to gift them a small semblance of her power. As humans they were healthier, lived longer and they thrived under her care.\r\n\r\nYet when Imaru’s hunger spread from the underworld, it was these unfortunates that fell first to this terrible affliction. Now they act as extensions to her hunger, sharing the rot with any unfortunate enough to cross their path.",
    "Charcoal": "What’s better than a dead tree? A dead tree that has been burned to the point of carbonisation. No longer do you see unsightly green leaves, but now the wonderful blackness of charcoal. Highly praised by smiths. Way better than stupid old logs of wood.  \r\n\r\nThis journal entry was sponsored by the tree haters of Lumbridge, if you’re interested in joining their focus group, please speak to Tim near Bob’s Axes.",
    "PoisonedSteelArrows": "What’s better than sharpened pieces of wood with super strong metal made into even sharper points, firing at your enemies with unreasonable speed? Why, the very same thing, but coated with a deadly toxic poison!\r\n\r\nI’d say your enemies complain about you doing this, but they’re too dead to fill in the forms. So you’re good.",
    "IronShield": "When Glass Knife was caught, she had no concerns about safety. She was well armed as a blacksmith, better than most hunters who tracked her down. She wielded a dagger in one hand and an iron shield in another, and she snarled defiance at those who tried to condemn her for her crimes. \r\n\r\nShe was, after all, protecting humanity. Purging impurity. Ridding them all of the Amalgamated stain on their bloodlines. In her mind she was doing the whole village a service. A worrying number of villagers agreed with her. \r\n\r\nAs the wife of one of the elders, her sentence was small. An act of community service, doing what was already her job. She wasn’t imprisoned. She wasn’t executed. She wasn’t publicly condemned for her crime. She laughed off over a dozen murders with a cruel, smug-filled smile. \r\n\r\nWhile she carried an iron shield with her, and it weathered the reprisal of many an Amalgamated enraged at the lack of justice, her real shield was the status she held. Wife of an elder, blacksmith of the town, proof that when an Amalgamated was a victim, justice had its limits. ",
    "Swamp": "When war broke out between humans and garou, it was the swamps that formed a buffer between the two factions. The muddy water was difficult to fight in and neither side enjoyed the idea of drowning on the battlefield.\r\n\r\nNow, the swap retains the memories of that conflict, swallowing down the remnants of that battle into its murky depths.\r\n\r\nYet, among all the ruin and the decay, the swamp is rife with ingredients for crafting. Herbs grow between the weeds and the caves that run along the swamp’s edges are filled with ore.\r\n\r\nStill, one must be ever careful. Goblins roam these regions, hunters keeping an eye out for northern invaders. The garou watch from Ghornfell, ready for any foolish trespasser that might enter their domain.\r\n\r\nAnd in the darkness of night, an ancient horror walks the region, offering only death’s embrace to any it finds.",
    "BronzeAxe": "Where bone and stone fail, metal provides. The bronze axe is strong enough to withstand greater force than its predecessors and can break through the bark of some of the strongest trees. On Ashenfall, nature has been hardened by the ambient magic, the bark of trees makes them resistant to most attacks. Yet, the bronze axe breaks through that barrier, not by magic, nor by cunning, but by sheer durability and resolve.\r\n\r\nThe lumberjacks of Brynmoor favoured bronze axes over iron. While iron was stronger and felled a tree quicker, the relatively cheapness and availability of copper and tin ore, made it a much more cost effective choice.\r\n\r\nThe villagers disapproved when the leadership pushed for iron in everything. They felt that bronze was more than enough for the village needs. But the lord wanted progress, and didn’t care about the price",
    "BronzeShield": "While heavier than other materials, there’s little argument that a bronze shield is a superior defence to wood and leather materials. The metal turns aside almost any blow, protecting the wielder behind its mass. \r\n\r\nEven the Wild Knights, who usually shunned metal weapons, would don bronze shields into battle. Their philosophy was that a life is worth more than a principle. \r\n\r\nBefore the peace treaty with their goddess, Lougrim, the garou would often get into conflict with travelling villagers. They saw the humans as prey, willfully intruding on their land and therefore consenting to be hunted. The humans, of course, saw things differently. \r\n\r\nGarou weapons were powerful and delivered by creatures of unnatural strength. Yet bronze would defend against these attacks, and it gave the garou pause, for they had yet to battle foe with such intriguing technology. Battles that would previously have been a slaughter became skirmishes as the garou tested their might against these new bronze defences. \r\n\r\nThis comparative peace did not last long. ",
    "IronAxe": "While it might be cheaper to use bronze, there are some times when sheer power is necessary. For those times, a lumberjack need look no further than the glittering might of an iron axe’s blade. The mightiest of Ghornfell trees falls before its onslaught, and none in that region may stand against its might.\r\n\r\nIron was so powerful, that it created a problem in Brynmoor. The population craved the metal so desperately that they mined the region almost dry. This let to infighting, small civil skirmishes and the white knights of the Blue Flame Keep, needed to be brought in to keep the peace.\r\n\r\nIt is said that where iron goes, blood soon follows.",
    "BittercapMushroom": "While many mushrooms are poisonous, the Bittercap Mushroom turns against the defences of its kin and is an ingredient in several poison remedies.",
    "GraniteMaul": "While metal may be the preferred choice of weapon makers, the granite maul stands as a testament to the raw power of stone. This legendary weapon, revered among hammer wielders, is nothing more than a massive, heavy stone affixed to a stick. Yet, in the hands of a skilled wielder, it becomes a force of unparalleled destruction. \r\n\r\nIt was developed by ancient dwarves long ago after one dwarf got fed up with the rock hammer he was using to fight a nest of gargoyles. The beasts were strong and numerous, and their skin was almost impenetrable to conventional weaponry; only rock hammers seemed to hurt them. In frustration, one dwarf took the biggest hunk of granite he could find and turned it into a legend. \r\n\r\nSince that moment, the weapon has been refined, using various special components to make it more wieldy in combat. The original creator having done himself an injury with the recoil. ",
    "WateringCan.Bronze": "While the druids relied on wooden watering cans, the more pragmatic farmers turned to metal. Bronze was sturdier, lasted longer and proved to ultimately be the better tool. Crops across Ashenfall found themselves constantly existing a state of blissful hydration, never knowing the agony of thirst. ",
    "LesserAntipoisonPotion": "While the weaker variant of antipoison is sufficient for everyday usage, there are some situations that require a more potent resistance. The toxic mists in Ghornfell, over the swamps, are more powerful than a weaker resistance will protect against. These lesser potions strengthen the immune system and keep the toxic miasma from the drinker’s blood. Imperfect, still, but a much greater chance of survival.",
    "RoastPumpkin": "Whilst some prefer to hollow out a pumpkin, carving features into it that should scare away the creatures of the night, more practical people eat them.\r\n\r\nA roasted pumpkin is a hearty affair, full of texture and a mellow taste that goes especially well in desserts.",
    "CorpseCottonPlant": "Why would nature create such a thing? Seriously, why? What cruel god thought that this was a reasonable plant to add to the world?",
    "WillowLogs": "Willow wood is a wonderful material. Flexible and yet strong and durable, it makes for an excellent resource in a variety of projects. Rare to find, it exists predominantly in small groves connected to a pool of water. Legend has it that the pools of water are formed from the tears of weeping willows, whose spirits lament the loss of a long-lost god.",
    "Windstep_name": "Windstep",
    "LeatherArmourLegs": "With a distinct aroma, leather armour became very popular with warriors and hunters, as well as a few villagers, but that’s another story. Leather, when properly layered and cured, can turn away both the claws of a beast and the blades of an assailant.\r\n\r\nBrynmoor villagers favoured simpler styles, which were easy to produce and focused on defence. When traversing the wild forests of Ashenfall, they quickly found that more elaborate designs got in the way. It was better to keep things simple so that they might be more easily repaired than to choose style over function.\r\n\r\nStill, it was common for villagers to add small adornments to their leather armour to make them stand out. Patches made to resemble animals were common practices, though flowers were also popular.\r\n\r\nGentle perfumes were also favoured to help limit the leather’s distinctive smell.",
    "LeatherArmourHead": "With a distinct aroma, leather armour became very popular with warriors and hunters, as well as a few villagers, but that’s another story. Leather, when properly layered and cured, can turn away both the claws of a beast and the blades of an assailant.\r\n\r\nIt takes time and skill to craft leather armour. The material needs to be cured, cut, shaped and then stitched together with more durable material than simple thread. It can be stiff to move and surprisingly heavy, something young villagers would discover on their earliest hunts.\r\n\r\nYoung hunters would be gifted leather armour by their family as a sign of their adulthood. Good quality leather armour showed great pride in one’s progeny. Many villagers would compete with one another to gift more elaborate armour as if doing so was a symbol to all others that their child was special.",
    "LeatherArmourBody": "With a distinct aroma, leather armour became very popular with warriors and hunters, as well as a few villagers, but that’s another story. Leather, when properly layered and cured, can turn away both the claws of a beast and the blades of an assailant.\r\n\r\nSome craftsmen would create more elaborate armour, trading functionality for impressive style. With the discovery of anima twisted plants that existed in vibrant hues, new dyes were crafted, allowing for colourful and eye-catching armour.\r\nThis would prove problematic when attempting to be camouflaged on the hunt. Many a colourful set of armour would be found in tattered shreds in the woods. The owner of the armour lost to the wilds.",
    "WateringCan.Steel": "With the discovery of steel, most people found new and wonderful ways to bring death. They crafted sharper swords, stronger shields, axes that could slice through a foe like a hot knife through butter. Yet, some people saw this new material, as a chance to bring life.\r\n\r\nSteel watering cans were sturdier and more durable. Able to bring much needed moisture to dehydrated gardens than before.",
    "ArcaneInfusion": "Wizards always push themselves too far. They compete academically with anything that moves, insisting on demonstrating their intellectual superior over everyone around them. Their intelligence is a glittering brooch that must be seen by everyone.\r\n\r\nNaturally, this sort of mentality causes many a wizard to burn out. Academia creates a sort of stress that could be bottled and weaponised, only to be banned by conventions around the world for its cruelty. Yet, a wizard does it to themselves, pushing and pushing beyond the limits of the human condition.\r\n\r\nInevitably they turn to external solutions, and this particular mixture has pushed many a mage past academic burnout just long enough to get by. Somehow it makes magic flow easier, spells cast stronger than before, and the wizard is capable of feats they might never have achieved before.\r\n\r\nThey also tell themselves that it lets them get by on only two hours of sleep a night. It does not and it all catches up with them in the end. Wizard burnout can be disturbingly literal.",
    "BronzeSpade": "Wood breaks, shatters and splinters, which can be very frustrating when out in the garden. Metal is more durable and longer lasting, and bronze is a very malleable metal. With one of these spades you can assault the skin of the planet, reshaping Gielinor to suit your own grand designs.",
    "FocusedWoodcuttingPotion": "Woodcutting is less about speed and strength, and more about precision. The focus this potion provides, lets you hone your senses, attacking the same place repeatedly along the tree trunk. The result is more efficiently felled trees and far fewer splinters.",
    "SwampThread": "Woven from swamp weed, this thread is both stronger than it appears and magically stable. Enchantments woven into garments, crafted with this thread, are more potent than those woven with normal fibres.",
    "Thread": "Woven strands that create a more durable thread. Useful in a wide range of crafting projects.",
    "AdventurersLegs": "You brought simple cloth trousers with you from the mainland. They barely qualify as armour, but they’re better than no armour at all. \r\n\r\nThese clothes have seen you across the oceans, keeping you warm in the cold sea breeze. Now starched with sea salt and weathered by labouring away on the ship deck, these clothes are a testament to how far you’ve come. This is the furthest you’ve ever been from home, and perhaps it’s a chance to craft a new one.  ",
    "CurseFragment.Village": "You feel the power of the curse thrumming through you. Like a heartbeat in your soul, resonating with all the frustration and anger within you.\r\n\r\nThis is a part of Imaru’s curse, a force created to destroy the dragon queen, Kuldra, herself.",
    "UndeadBone": "You may think an undead bone is no different from a normal bone, and in most cases that’s true. The animating forces of necromancy normally just puppet the bones, allowing them to be possessed by a spirit or controlled by some external force.\r\n\r\nThe undead of Fellhollow are different. They animated by the last remnants of their humanity, tiny bits of soul clinging on in tattered forms. They move because they need to and they shudder because they fear what happens when they stop.\r\n\r\nAs such, the bones of the Fellhollow dead have strange properties that a skilled craftsman can refine and find uses for.\r\n\r\nMorbid, yes, but inventive.",
    "Sapphire": "Your eyes are like sapphires, sparkling in the night\r\nEvery syllable you utter creates pure delight.\r\nA day in your company is infinitely bright\r\nWill you be my valentine tonight?\r\n\r\nA poem by a lovestruck Bramblemead youth. It was not successful.\r\n\r\nAside from their use in terrible teenage poetry, sapphires are a vital component in jewellry crafting. They can be found hidden among the metal ores around Ghornfell, or hidden in the various chests left behind by the old kingdom.",
    "TheStorySoFar.Title": "a. The Story So Far",
    "Movement.Title": "b. Movement",
